#: 4.x/basics/understanding_delta/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=understanding-delta>Understanding 'delta'</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>The or &ldquo;delta time&rdquo; parameter is a frequently-misunderstood concept in game development. In this tutorial, we&rsquo;ll explain how it&rsquo;s used, the importance of frame-rate independent movement, and practical examples of its use in Godot.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>To illustrate the problem, let&rsquo;s consider a node moving across the screen. If our screen is pixels wide and we want the sprite to take seconds to cross the screen, we can use the following calculation to find the necessary speed:</p><p>We&rsquo;ll move the sprite every frame using the function. If the game is running at frames per second, we can find the per-frame movement like so:</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>Notice the units are consistent in all the calculations above. Always pay attention to the units in your calculations - it&rsquo;ll save you from making mistakes.</p></div></div><p>Here&rsquo;s the necessary code:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Run this code and you&rsquo;ll see the sprite takes 5 seconds to cross the screen.</p><p><a href=#image-84a15db984f036fc059da72bc828da43 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/delta_01.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-84a15db984f036fc059da72bc828da43><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/delta_01.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Maybe. The trouble begins if there is something else occupying the computer&rsquo;s time. This is called <em>lag</em> and can come from a variety of sources - the cause could be your code or even other applications running on your computer. If this happens, then the length of a frame might increase. As an extreme example, imagine that the frame rate is halved - each frame took 1/30 instead of 1/60 of a second. Moving at px/frame, it&rsquo;s now going to take twice as long for the sprite to reach the edge.</p><p><a href=#image-805e9f1c423c6f6e943202176d95d80f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/delta_02.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-805e9f1c423c6f6e943202176d95d80f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/delta_02.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Even small frame rate fluctuations will result in inconsistent movement speed. If this were a bullet or other fast-moving object, we wouldn&rsquo;t want it slowing down like this. We need the movement to be <em>frame rate independent</em>.</p><h3 id=fixing-the-frame-rate-problem>Fixing the frame rate problem</h3><p>When using the function, it automatically includes a parameter called that&rsquo;s passed in from the engine (so does , which is used for physics-related code). This is a floating point value representing the length of time since the previous frame. Typically, this will be approximately 1/60 or seconds.</p><p>With this information, we can stop thinking about how much to move each frame, and only consider our desired speed in pixels/second ( from the above calculation).</p><p>Multiplying the engine&rsquo;s value by this number will give us how many pixels to move each frame. The number will automatically adjust if the frame time fluctuates.</p><p>Note that if the frame rate decreases by half (meaning the frame <em>time</em> doubles), then our per-frame movement must also double to keep the desired speed.</p><p>Let&rsquo;s change the code to use this calculation:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now when running at frames per second, the travel time is consistent:</p><p><a href=#image-b07eb0b3b2f2e2083bf1f2961fc8e724 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/delta_03.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-b07eb0b3b2f2e2083bf1f2961fc8e724><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/delta_03.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>If the frame rate gets <em>very</em> low, the movement is no longer smooth, but the <em>time</em> remains the same.</p><p><a href=#image-e7679f20811f97aaa053dbc5861b6376 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/delta_04.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e7679f20811f97aaa053dbc5861b6376><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/delta_04.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=using-delta-with-motion-equations>Using delta with motion equations</h3><p>What if your movement is more complex? The concept remains the same. Keep your units in seconds, not frames, and multiply by each frame.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>Working in pixels and seconds is much easier to conceptualize too, since it relates to how we measure these quantities in the real world. &ldquo;Gravity is , so after the ball falls for 2 seconds, it&rsquo;s traveling at 200 pixels/second.&rdquo; If you&rsquo;re working with frames, then you have to think about acceleration in units of . Go ahead and try - it&rsquo;s not very natural.</p></div></div><p>For example, if you are applying a gravity, that&rsquo;s an acceleration - each frame it will increase the velocity by some amount. As in the above example, the velocity then changes the node&rsquo;s position.</p><p>Try adjusting and in the following code to see the effect:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Note that we&rsquo;re multiplying by our timestep each frame to update both and . Any quantity that is updated every frame should be multiplied by to ensure it changes independent or frame rate.</p><h4 id=using-kinematic-functions>Using kinematic functions</h4><p>In the above examples, we&rsquo;ve used a to keep things simple, updating the every frame. If you&rsquo;re using a kinematic body (in 2D or 3D), you&rsquo;ll instead be using one of its movement methods. Specifically in the case of , there tends to be some confusion, because it uses the velocity vector, not the position. This means you won&rsquo;t multiply your velocity by to find distance - the function does that for you. But you will still need to apply it on any <em>other</em> calculations, such as the acceleration. For example:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>If you don&rsquo;t use when applying acceleration to your velocity, then your acceleration will be subject to fluctuations in frame rate. This can have a_much more subtle effect on movement - it will be inconsistent, but much more difficult to diagnose.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>When using you still need to apply to any other quantities such as gravity, friction, etc.</p></div></div><h2 id=related-recipes>Related Recipes</h2><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

