#: 4.x/basics/node_communication/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=node-communication-the-right-way>Node communication (the right way)</h1><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Info</div><div class=box-content><p>Many thanks to @TheDuriel on the <a href=https://discord.gg/zH7NUgz target=_blank>Godot Discord</a> for the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_access_theduriel.png>original diagram</a> that inspired this article. Save this and keep it handy.</p></div></div><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>Your project has started getting complex. You have multiple scenes, instances, and a <em>lot</em> of nodes. You&rsquo;ve probably found yourself writing code like the following:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>If you do this, you&rsquo;ll soon find that node references like this break easily. As soon as you change one thing about your scene tree, none of those references may be valid anymore.</p><p>Communication between nodes and scenes doesn&rsquo;t have to be complicated. There is a better way.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>As a general rule, nodes should manage their children, not the other way around. If you&rsquo;re using or , then you&rsquo;re probably headed for trouble. Node paths like this are <em>brittle</em>, meaning they can break easily. The three main problems with this arrangement:</p><ol><li><p>You can&rsquo;t test a scene independently. If you run the scene by itself or in a test scene that doesn&rsquo;t have the <em>exact</em> same node setup, will cause a crash.</p></li><li><p>You can&rsquo;t change things easily. If you decide to rearrange or redesign your tree, paths will no longer be valid.</p></li><li><p>Ready order is children-first, parent-last. This means that trying to access a parent&rsquo;s property in a node&rsquo;s can fail because the parent isn&rsquo;t ready yet.</p></li></ol><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>See <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/basics/tree_ready_order/>Understanding tree order</a> for an explanation of how nodes enter the tree and become ready.</p></div></div><p>Generally speaking, a node or scene should be able to be instanced anywhere in your game, and it should make no assumptions about what its parent is going to be.</p><p>We&rsquo;ll go into detailed examples later in this tutorial, but for now, here&rsquo;s the &ldquo;golden rule&rdquo; of node communication:</p><blockquote><p><strong>Call down, signal up.</strong></p></blockquote><p>If a node is calling a child (i.e. going &ldquo;down&rdquo; the tree), then is appropriate.</p><p>If a node needs to communicate &ldquo;up&rdquo; the tree, it should probably use a signal.</p><p>If you keep this rule in mind when designing your scene setup, you&rsquo;ll be well on your way to a maintainable, well-organized project. And you&rsquo;ll avoid using the cumbersome node paths that lead to problems.</p><p>Now, let&rsquo;s look at each of these strategies along with some examples.</p><h2 id=1-using-get_node>1. Using </h2><p> traverses the scene tree using a given <em>path</em> to find the named node.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>See <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/basics/getting_nodes/>Understanding node paths</a> for a more detailed explanation of node paths.</p></div></div><h4 id=get_node-example> example</h4><p>Let&rsquo;s consider the following common configuration:</p><p><a href=#image-d5acee18b75cd0d3fbd95f27c5b43308 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_access_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-d5acee18b75cd0d3fbd95f27c5b43308><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_access_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>The script in the node needs to notify the which animation to play, based on the player&rsquo;s movement. In this situation, works well:</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>In GDScript you can use as a shorthand for , writing instead.</p></div></div><h4 id=a-better-way>A better way</h4><p>The downsides of this approach are that you have to specify the node path, and if that changes later, you&rsquo;ll have to edit the code as well. Instead, you can use the feature to directly select a node.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>With this method, you can assign the value of the variable directly in the Inspector by choosing the node.</p><h2 id=2-using-signals>2. Using signals</h2><p>Signals should be used to call functions on nodes that are higher in the tree or at the same level (i.e. &ldquo;siblings&rdquo;).</p><p>You can connect a signal in the editor (most often for nodes that exist before the game starts) or in code (for nodes that you&rsquo;re instancing at runtime). The syntax for connecting a signal is:</p><blockquote><p></p></blockquote><p>Looking at this, you may be thinking &ldquo;Wait, if I&rsquo;m connecting to a sibling, won&rsquo;t I need a node paths like ?&rdquo;. While you <em>could</em> do this, it breaks our rule above. The answer to this puzzle is to make sure that connections are made by the <em>common parent</em>.</p><p>Following the rule of calling <em>down</em> the tree, a node that&rsquo;s a common parent to the signaling and receiving nodes will by definition know where they are and be ready after both of them.</p><h3 id=signal-example>Signal example</h3><p>A very common use case for signals is updating your UI. Whenever the player&rsquo;s variable changes, you want to update a or display. However, your UI nodes are completely separated from your player (as they should be). The player knows nothing about where those nodes are and how to find them.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s our example setup:</p><p><a href=#image-605c3247957ecd14bdb5e921e539c1a7 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_access_05.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-605c3247957ecd14bdb5e921e539c1a7><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_access_05.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Note that the UI is an instanced scene, we&rsquo;re just showing the contained nodes. This is where you often see things like , which is what we want to avoid.</p><p>Instead the player emits a signal whenever it adds/loses health. We need to send that signal to the UI&rsquo;s function, which handles setting the value. In the script we use this code whenever the player&rsquo;s health is changed:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>In the script we have:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now we just need to connect the signal to the function. The perfect place to do that is in , which is the common parent, and knows where both nodes are:</p><div class=highlight></div><h2 id=3-using-groups>3. Using groups</h2><p>Groups are another way to decouple, especially when you have a lot of similar objects that need to do the same thing. A node can be added to any number of groups and membership can be changed dynamically at any time with and .</p><p>A common misconception about groups is that they are some kind of object or array that &ldquo;contains&rdquo; node references. Groups are a <em>tagging system</em>. A node is &ldquo;in&rdquo; a group if it has that tag assigned from it. The SceneTree keeps track of the tags and has functions like to help you find all nodes with a particular tag.</p><h3 id=group-example>Group example</h3><p>Let&rsquo;s consider a Galaga-style space shooter where you have a lots of enemies flying around. These enemies may have different types and behaviors. You&rsquo;d like to add a &ldquo;smart bomb&rdquo; upgrade that, when activated, destroys all enemies on the screen. Using groups, you can implement this with a minimal amount of code.</p><p>First, add all enemies to an &ldquo;enemies&rdquo; group. You can do this in the editor using the &ldquo;Node&rdquo; tab:</p><p><a href=#image-1a33b13c03a9cac2f22fe03daed9d99b class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_access_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-1a33b13c03a9cac2f22fe03daed9d99b><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_access_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>You can also add nodes to the group in your script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Let&rsquo;s assume every enemy has an function that handles what happens when it dies (playing an animation, spawning dropped items, etc). Now that every enemy is in the group, we can implement our smart bomb function like this:</p><div class=highlight></div><h2 id=4-using-owner>4. Using </h2><p> is a property that&rsquo;s set automatically when you save a scene. Every node in that scene will have its set to the scene&rsquo;s root node. This makes for a convenient way to connect child signals up to the main node.</p><h3 id=owner-example> example</h3><p>In a complex UI, you often find yourself with a very deep, nested hierarchy of containers and controls. Nodes that the user interacts with, such as , emit signals, and you may want to connect those signals to the script on the UI&rsquo;s root node.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s an example setup:</p><p><a href=#image-3a603877f2097c9b776418fc5c8f1a5e class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_access_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-3a603877f2097c9b776418fc5c8f1a5e><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_access_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>The script on the root has the following function, which we want to call whenever any button is pressed:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The buttons here are instances of a scene, representing an object which may contain dynamic code that sets the button&rsquo;s text or other properties. Or perhaps you have buttons that are dynamically added/removed from the container depending on the game state. Regardless, all we need to connect the button&rsquo;s signal is the following:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>No matter where you place the buttons in the tree - if you add more containers, for example - the remains the .</p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/basics/tree_ready_order/>Understanding tree order</a></li><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/basics/getting_nodes/>Understanding node paths</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
