#: 4.x/basics/file_io/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=savingloading-data>Saving/loading data</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You need to save and load local data between game sessions.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Godot&rsquo;s file I/O (input/output) system is based around the object. You open a file by calling .</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle warning\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-triangle\"></i> Warning</div><div class=box-content><p>User data should only be stored in the path. While can be used when running from the editor, when your project is exported, the path becomes read-only.</p></div></div><p>The second argument after the file path is the &ldquo;Mode Flag&rdquo;, which can be one of the following:</p><ul><li>FileAccess.READ - Open for reading.</li><li>FileAccess.WRITE - Open for writing. Creates the file if it doesn&rsquo;t exist and truncates if it does.</li><li>FileAccess.READ_WRITE - Open for reading and writing. Doesn&rsquo;t truncate the file.</li><li>FileAccess.WRITE_READ - Open for reading/writing. Creates the file if it doesn&rsquo;t exist and truncates if it does.</li></ul><h3 id=storing-data>Storing data</h3><p>You can save data using its specific data type (, , etc.), or using the generic , which will use Godot&rsquo;s built-in serialization to encode your data, including complex data like objects (more on this later).</p><p>Let&rsquo;s start with a small example: saving the player&rsquo;s high score. We can write a function that we can call whenever the score needs to be saved:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;re saving our score, but we need to be able to load it when the game starts:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Don&rsquo;t forget to check for the file&rsquo;s existence before attempting to read from it - it may not be there! If that&rsquo;s the case, you can use a default value.</p><p>You can and as many times as you need for any number of values.</p><h3 id=saving-resources>Saving Resources</h3><p>The above technique works great when all you need to save are a few values. For more complex situations, you can save your data in a Resource, just like Godot does. Godot saves all its data Resources as files (Animations, TileSets, Shaders, etc.) and you can too!</p><p>To save and load Resources, use the and Godot classes.</p><p>For this example, let&rsquo;s say you have all the data about your character&rsquo;s stats stored in a Resource like this:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>You can then save and load like so:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Resources can contain subresources, so you could have your player&rsquo;s inventory Resource included as well, and so on.</p><h3 id=what-about-json>What about JSON?</h3><p>I see it very often (and some readers may be asking it already): &ldquo;What if I want to use JSON to save my data?&rdquo; This is my response:</p><blockquote><p>Don&rsquo;t use JSON for your save files!</p></blockquote><p>While Godot has <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/class_json.html target=_blank>JSON support</a>, saving game data is not what JSON is for. JSON is a data <em>interchange</em> format - its purpose is to allow systems using different data formats and/or languages to exchange data between each other.</p><p>This means JSON has limitations that are negatives for you when it comes to saving your game data. JSON doesn&rsquo;t support many data types (no int vs. float, for example) so you have to do a lot of converting and validating to try and save/load your data. It&rsquo;s cumbersome and time consuming.</p><p>Don&rsquo;t waste your time. Using Godot&rsquo;s built-in serialization, you can store native Godot objects - Nodes, Resources, even Scenes - without any effort, which means less code and fewer errors.</p><p>There&rsquo;s a reason that Godot itself doesn&rsquo;t use JSON for saving scenes and resources.</p><h3 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h3><p>This article just scratches the surface of what you can do with . For the full list of available methods, see the <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_fileaccess.html target=_blank>FileAccess documentation</a>.</p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

