msgid "<h1 id=understanding-node-paths>Understanding node paths</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>It&rsquo;s probably the most common problem seen in the Godot help channels: an invalid node reference. Most often, it appears as the following error message:</p><blockquote><p>Invalid get index &lsquo;position&rsquo; (on base: &rsquo;null instance&rsquo;).</p></blockquote><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>It&rsquo;s that last part, the &ldquo;null instance&rdquo;, that&rsquo;s the source of this problem, and the main source of confusion for Godot beginners.</p><p>The way to avoid this problem is to understand the concept of <em>node paths</em>.</p><h3 id=understanding-node-paths>Understanding node paths</h3><p>The scene tree is made of nodes, which are connected together in parent-child relationships. A node path is the path it takes to get from one node to another by moving through this tree.</p><p>As an example, let&rsquo;s take a simple &ldquo;Player&rdquo; scene:</p><p><a href=#image-a303f1865ca2ae5a020a60720ee9080f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_paths_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-a303f1865ca2ae5a020a60720ee9080f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_paths_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>The script for this scene is on the node. If the script needs to call on the node, it needs a reference to that node:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The argument of the function is a string representing the <em>path</em> to the desired node. In this case, it&rsquo;s a child of the node the script is on. If the path you give it is invalid, you&rsquo;ll get the dreaded error (as well as &ldquo;Node not found&rdquo;).</p><p>Getting a node reference with is such a common situation that GDScript has a shortcut for it:</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Info</div><div class=box-content><p> returns a <em>reference</em> to the desired node.</p></div></div><p>Let&rsquo;s look at a more complex scene tree:</p><p><a href=#image-e40adf9d6213c9b49d8530d60c37801d class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_paths_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e40adf9d6213c9b49d8530d60c37801d><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/node_paths_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>If the script on needs to access it can do so with this path:</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>When using notation, the Godot editor will autocomplete paths for you. You can also right-click on a node in the Scene tab and choose &ldquo;Copy Node Path&rdquo;.</p></div></div><p>What if the node you want to access is higher in the tree? You can use or to reference the parent node. In the above example tree, to get the node from the :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Let&rsquo;s break that down. The path means &ldquo;get the node that&rsquo;s up one level (), then one more level (), then its child &rdquo;.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>Does this seem familiar? Node paths work exactly like directory paths in your operating system. The character indicates the parent-child relationship, and means &ldquo;up one level&rdquo;.</p></div></div><h3 id=relative-vs-absolute-paths>Relative vs absolute paths</h3><p>The above examples all use <em>relative</em> paths - meaning they start at the current node and follow the path to the destination. Node paths can also be absolute, starting from the root node of the scene.</p><p>For example, the absolute path to the player node is:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>, which can also be accessed with is <em>not</em> the root node of your scene. It&rsquo;s the Viewport node that is always present by default in the SceneTree.</p><h3 id=a-warning>A warning</h3><p>While the above examples work just fine, there are some things you should be aware of that may cause problems later. Imagine the following situation: the node has a property, which you want to display in a node somewhere in your UI. You might write something like this in the player&rsquo;s script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>While this may work fine at first, it is <em>brittle</em>, meaning it can break easily. There are two main problems with this kind of arrangement:</p><ol><li>You can&rsquo;t test the player scene independently. If you run the player scene by itself or in a test scene that doesn&rsquo;t have a UI, the line will cause a crash.</li><li>You can&rsquo;t change your UI. If you decide to rearrange or redesign your UI, the path will no longer be valid and you have to change it.</li></ol><p>For this reason, you should try to avoid using node paths that go <em>up</em> the scene tree. In the above situation, if the player instead emitted a signal when the health changed, the UI could listen for that signal to update itself. You could then rearrange and separate nodes without fear of breaking your game.</p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>Once you understand how to use node paths, you&rsquo;ll see how easy it is to reference any node you need. And put a stop to seeing those error messages.</p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

