#: 4.x/ui/level_select/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=level-select-menu>Level Select Menu</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>Your game needs a &ldquo;level select&rdquo; menu, where the user can choose from a grid of options.</p><p><a href=#image-5986241219040982706dd6864f73e92b class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_03.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-5986241219040982706dd6864f73e92b><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_03.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>As shown in the example above, we&rsquo;ll make a scrolling grid of level &ldquo;boxes&rdquo; that the player can choose from. Let&rsquo;s start with the individual level boxes:</p><h3 id=1-level-box>1: Level box</h3><p>Here&rsquo;s the node setup:</p><p>The <i class=gd-TextureRect></i> TextureRect is for displaying the lock icon, and the <i class=gd-Label></i> Label for displaying the level number. When one is showing, the other is hidden.</p><p>You can style these as you like, here&rsquo;s an example:</p><p><a href=#image-6acfc53722afe4c2b08163cae4392029 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_01.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6acfc53722afe4c2b08163cae4392029><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_01.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Make sure to set the &rsquo;s <strong>Custom Minimum Size</strong> in the Inspector. We&rsquo;re using in the example, but it depends on what size layout you&rsquo;re going for.</p><p>Now add a script and connect the signal.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;re using here so that we can make changes to the properties in the inspector and see them right away, without running the scene. Go ahead and try clicking the <strong>Locked</strong> property and verify that you see the lock appear/disappear.</p><p>Since we don&rsquo;t have actual levels to load in this project, the statement can help test that the click is being detected.</p><h3 id=2-grid>2: Grid</h3><p>Once you have the box scene completed, add a new scene with a <i class=gd-GridContainer></i>. Add any number of instances under it, making sure to set the <strong>Columns</strong> value. Here&rsquo;s one with 6 columns:</p><p><a href=#image-8eff2fbb757f0947acd5555b3044ef11 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-8eff2fbb757f0947acd5555b3044ef11><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>In this example <strong>Theme Overrides/Constants/H Separation</strong> and <strong>V Separation</strong> are set to .</p><p>Save this scene as . In the menu, we&rsquo;ll use multiple instances to display the desired number of levels.</p><h3 id=3-menu-screen>3: Menu screen</h3><p>Now we can put together the final menu.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s the basic layout we&rsquo;re going for:</p><p><a href=#image-5e091687125157c3c56d9fab70889297 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_04.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-5e091687125157c3c56d9fab70889297><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_04.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We&rsquo;ll create it with these nodes:</p><p>Adjust the node properties:</p><ul><li><i class=gd-MarginContainer></i><ul><li><strong>Theme Overrides/Constants/Margins</strong>: </li></ul></li><li><i class=gd-VBoxContainer></i> <ul><li><strong>Theme Overrides/Constants/Separation</strong>: </li></ul></li><li><i class=gd-Label></i> <ul><li>Style the font however you like</li></ul></li><li><i class=gd-TextureButton></i> / <ul><li><strong>Ignore Texture Size</strong>: </li><li><strong>Stretch Mode</strong>: </li><li><strong>Layout/Container Sizing/Horizontal/Expand</strong>: </li></ul></li><li><i class=gd-Control></i> <ul><li><strong>Layout/Clip Contents</strong>: </li><li><strong>Layout/Custom Minimum Size</strong>: (size of the )</li></ul></li></ul><p>The node is where the grid goes. Enabling <strong>Clip Contents</strong> means that if the contents are larger than the control, they&rsquo;ll be cropped. That will allow us to make a horizontally scrolling set of grids. Add an <i class=gd-HBoxContainer></i> called to , and instance 3 (or more) s inside it.</p><p>Make sure to set <strong>Theme Overrides/Constants/Separation</strong> to .</p><p>Your layout should look something like this (we&rsquo;ve disabled <strong>Clip Contents</strong> in order to show what&rsquo;s happening):</p><p><a href=#image-99e74b181ca9e07abc0ad4bc0cb2659f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_05.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-99e74b181ca9e07abc0ad4bc0cb2659f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/level_select_05.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>With <em>Clip Content</em>, the three grids are all there, but the only shows one at a time.</p><p>Now, to scroll the menu, we need to shift the by pixels to the left/right.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Info</div><div class=box-content><p>You may be wondering why we&rsquo;re not using a <i class=gd-ScrollContainer></i> here. You certainly can, but we don&rsquo;t want continuous scrolling, and we don&rsquo;t want to see a scrollbar.</p></div></div><p>Add a script to the and connect the signals of the two buttons.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>When you run the scene, try clicking the &ldquo;Next&rdquo; and &ldquo;Back&rdquo; buttons and verify that it&rsquo;s scrolling as expected. Clicking the individual level boxes should print to the console.</p><p>Download the example project to see the whole thing in action, including some tweens for the scrolling action (because tweens make everything better).</p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/ui_level_select target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/ui_level_select</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/ui/floating_text/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=floating-combat-text>Floating combat text</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want units to display damage as floating numbers when hit.</p><p><a href=#image-30391be7ac790315c4144771a411a69f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/fct_demo.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-30391be7ac790315c4144771a411a69f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/fct_demo.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>There are many ways to approach this problem. For example, you could use a bitmap font and build an image for each number out of its digits, then use a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> node to display and move it.</p><p>However, for this recipe, we&rsquo;ll use a <i class=gd-Label></i> node (named &ldquo;FCT&rdquo;). This will give us the flexibility to change the font, as well as making it easy to display the number as a string - or even other messages such as &ldquo;miss&rdquo;.</p><p>Add a resource and use your favorite font. In this example, we&rsquo;re using &ldquo;Xolonium.ttf&rdquo; with a font size of and a black outline of pixels.</p><p>Add a script to the label.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>When we spawn the floating text, we&rsquo;ll call this function to set the parameters:</p><ul><li> - the number (or string) to display</li><li> - a representing the direction of travel</li><li> - how long the text will last</li><li> - movement will be randomly spread across this arc</li><li> - if , indicates the damage was a &ldquo;critical&rdquo; hit</li></ul><p>Here&rsquo;s what the function does:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>First, assign the value and randomize the movement based on the given spread (+/-90 degrees, for example). Since we may also be animating the scale, we set the to the center of the control so that it will scale from the center.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Next, we have two properties to interpolate: for the movement and for the visibility.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>If the hit is a critical, we&rsquo;ll also change the color and animate the scale for a bigger effect. Note, I&rsquo;ve hardcoded this to be red, but you should probably make this a configurable value.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Finally, we start the <i class=gd-Tween></i> and wait for it to finish, then remove the Label.</p><h3 id=floating-text-manager>Floating text manager</h3><p>Next we&rsquo;ll crate a small node to manage placing and spawning the floating text. This node will be attached to the game entities that you want to display floating text effects.</p><p>This is a <i class=gd-Node2D></i> named &ldquo;FCTManager&rdquo;. It contains the following script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Here you can expose the settings to the Inspector for convenient changes. The method here spawns the floating labels and sets their properties.</p><p>In your game unit, you&rsquo;d attach an instance of this node, and position it wherever you want the text to appear. Then add something like the following to the unit&rsquo;s method:</p><div class=highlight></div><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>Optimization: in the case where you have a very large number of enemies/bullets, you may experience some performance impact from repeatedly spawning and freeing floating text. In this case, you can spawn a fixed number of text objects in the manager, and show/hide them rather than freeing them at the end of the animation.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>Art in this demo by <a href=https://www.patreon.com/ansimuz target=_blank>Luis Zuno</a></p></div></div><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/floating_combat_text target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/floating_combat_text</a></p><p><a href=#image-dcae6b476dd3150c4d9fc885db30f2d8 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/fct_demo.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-dcae6b476dd3150c4d9fc885db30f2d8><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/fct_demo.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

