msgid "<h1 id=displaying-debug-data>Displaying debug data</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You need an easy way to display debug data on-screen.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>We&rsquo;ll create an overlay that any node can register a property with, and that property will be displayed in an appropriate format.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s an example of what we&rsquo;re going for:</p><p><a href=#image-30eb7b813e75dd18a773a673dd0db916 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/debug_stats_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-30eb7b813e75dd18a773a673dd0db916><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/debug_stats_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Start by adding a <i class=gd-MarginContainer></i> named with a <i class=gd-VBoxContainer></i> child. Set the margins to a comfortable value (I usually use ).</p><p>Let&rsquo;s take a look at the code:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We begin with a custom class to encapsulate the data for the object and property we&rsquo;re tracking. Let&rsquo;s break down the class properties:</p><ul><li> - This is a reference to the object we&rsquo;re tracking.</li><li> - This is in the form of a , meaning we can track something like , but also .</li><li> - Each property is linked to a <i class=gd-Label></i> node, this is a reference to the label so that we can set its .</li><li> - This is an optional setting to configure how the value should be displayed (see below).</li></ul><p>In the method we build up a string to display in the label&rsquo;s property. We include the object&rsquo;s name and which property we&rsquo;re showing, plus the value modified by the option.</p><p>For this example, we&rsquo;ve shown two settings for the option:</p><ul><li> - if the is a vector, we&rsquo;ll display its length.</li><li> - if the property is a numeric type, we&rsquo;ll round its values.</li></ul><p>We then need functions to add/remove properties from the display:</p><div class=highlight></div><p> can be called from any node (we should load the debug display as a singleton - see below).</p><p>Note we&rsquo;re loading and instantiating a &ldquo;debug_label&rdquo; scene. This allows you to customize how you want the individual labels to appear - font, size, color, etc. Make a separate scene with a <i class=gd-Label></i> node and configure it to your liking.</p><h3 id=adding-to-game>Adding to game</h3><p>To use it in game, you&rsquo;ll want the debug display to be rendered on top of your game, whether in 2D or 3D. Create one more scene called &ldquo;DebugOverlay&rdquo; and make its root node a <i class=gd-CanvasLayer></i>. Add the scene as a child.</p><p>For easy reference, I like to add this in a script on :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now add in <strong>Project Settings > Autoload</strong>.</p><p>Now any node in the game can register/remove properties from the display like so:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>You can see the debug overlay in action in many of the example projects on this site. See the related recipes for adding a debug drawing layer as well.</p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

