#: 4.x/ui/minimap/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=minimapradar>Minimap/radar</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want a minimap or radar-style UI item showing the locations of objects outside of the player&rsquo;s view.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Here&rsquo;s an example of what we are going for: <video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_01.webm></video></p><h3 id=project-setup>Project setup</h3><p>To illustrate this feature, we&rsquo;ll start with a simplified top-down game using the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/autotile_intro/>Autotile recipe</a> and a player based on the <a href=godot_recipes/2d/topdown_movement/#option-2-rotate-and-move>Top-down character recipe</a>. See the linked recipes for details on how these parts work.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>The art in this project comes from <a href=https://kenney.nl target=_blank>kenney.nl</a>, which you can download here: <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/files/minimap_assets.zip>Minimap Assets</a>.</p></div></div><p>Our main scene setup looks like this:</p><p><a href=#image-fb1f1a50ea58c86d84a17db51da0ec69 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-fb1f1a50ea58c86d84a17db51da0ec69><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>The node is there to hold our UI, including the minimap/radar we&rsquo;re making in this recipe.</p><h3 id=ui-layout>UI Layout</h3><p>The first step will be to create the layout for the minimap. In order to work with whatever other UI elements exist in the game, it must resize smoothly, and integrate well with a container-based layout.</p><p>Add a <i class=gd-MarginContainer></i> first. Set its <strong>Theme Overrides/Constants</strong> all to . This control will hold the rest of the nodes and ensure it doesn&rsquo;t bleed over into any other elements. Name it &ldquo;Minimap&rdquo; and save the scene.</p><p>Next, add a <i class=gd-NinePatchRect></i> node. This node is similar to a but handles resizing differently by not stretching the corners/edges. Drop the image from the asset folder into the <strong>Texture</strong> property. This is a image and if we scale the root <i class=gd-MarginContainer></i>, the image becomes stretched and ugly:</p><p><a href=#image-21ffb4cb7419785bcf2d754eca46fab2 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_02.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-21ffb4cb7419785bcf2d754eca46fab2><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_02.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Using the <i class=gd-NinePatchRect></i>&rsquo;s properties, we can ensure that the frame remains the same size when stretched. You can define these properties graphically in the &ldquo;TextureRegion&rdquo; panel, but it&rsquo;s sometimes easier to enter the values directly. Set all four properties in the <strong>Patch Margin</strong> section to and change the node&rsquo;s name to &ldquo;Frame&rdquo;.</p><p>Now observe what happens when we change the size:</p><p><a href=#image-5be0392ea86c17048e1ae1ade140edc5 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_03.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-5be0392ea86c17048e1ae1ade140edc5><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_03.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Next, we&rsquo;d like to fill in the inner part of the frame with the grid pattern :</p><p><a href=#image-fac2528cf541faf34256d40edaf981ad class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/pattern_blueprintPaper.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-fac2528cf541faf34256d40edaf981ad><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/pattern_blueprintPaper.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>However, we need it to tile automatically no matter what size we make the frame. Also, since this grid area is where our minimap markers will appear, we don&rsquo;t want the grid extending past the edges of the frame.</p><p>As a child of the (and a sibling of the ), add another <i class=gd-MarginContainer></i>. Set all four margin properties in <strong>Theme Overrides/Constants</strong> to . As a child of this node, add a <i class=gd-TextureRect></i> and assign its <strong>Texture</strong> to the above image. Set its <strong>Stretch Mode</strong> to &ldquo;Tile&rdquo;. Name this node &ldquo;Grid&rdquo;.</p><p>Try changing the size of your root node to see the effect:</p><p><a href=#image-cde1d520d15fe0d39c403e144a0da239 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_04.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-cde1d520d15fe0d39c403e144a0da239><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_04.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>For now, let&rsquo;s leave the minimap&rsquo;s size at - you can check the root node&rsquo;s <strong>Size</strong> property in the <strong>Layout</strong> section to confirm.</p><p>At this point, your scene tree should look like the following:</p><p><a href=#image-07a95bb8c80bfe3bb1a0f919262e7c54 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_06.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-07a95bb8c80bfe3bb1a0f919262e7c54><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_06.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=map-markers>Map Markers</h3><p>As a child of , add a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> node named &ldquo;PlayerMarker&rdquo; and give it the texture. Note the sprite&rsquo;s <strong>Transform/Position</strong> property: , which places it exactly in the top-left corner of the :</p><p><a href=#image-4497b1a4e987671c7f3a3c95f3e0563f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_05.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-4497b1a4e987671c7f3a3c95f3e0563f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_05.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>If our size is currently (you can check this in its <strong>Size</strong> property), then its center will be . Put the &rsquo;s <strong>Position</strong> there:</p><p><a href=#image-89d69a0189966d6b34182ea83cbcd64e class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_07.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-89d69a0189966d6b34182ea83cbcd64e><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_07.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Don&rsquo;t worry, we&rsquo;ll automate this later.</p><p>Add two more <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> nodes: &ldquo;MobMarker&rdquo; and &ldquo;AlertMarker&rdquo;, using the and textures.</p><p><a href=#image-f5f9d1fcad676ab553e79e369b95d705 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_08.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-f5f9d1fcad676ab553e79e369b95d705><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_08.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>These will represent two different types of objects in the game world. Click the &ldquo;Toggle Visibility&rdquo; button next to each so that they won&rsquo;t appear by default.</p><h3 id=scripting-the-map-markers>Scripting the map markers</h3><p>At this point, we have some decisions to make. How we approach populating the minimap with the objects in the world has a lot to do with how the game is set up. Since this is a very minimal demonstration project, we&rsquo;re going keep the process simple. In a larger game, you may need to use a more robust approach.</p><p>For this demo, we have two game objects: a , which wanders around the map randomly, and a , which the player can pick up. Many of these are scattered around the main scene. Each will need to be represented by one of the map markers we made.</p><p>Add each item that you want to appear on the minimap to a group named &ldquo;minimap_objects&rdquo;. In each object&rsquo;s script, assign it a property:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now we can begin adding a script to the . First, a reference that can be assigned in the Inspector when the minimap is added to the main scene and a property to calibrate the scale - how far the minimap can &ldquo;see&rdquo;. We also have some variables to make it more convenient to access the nodes we need.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Next, we&rsquo;ll use a dictionary to map the tags we gave our units to the corresponding marker:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Then we need a variable to hold the calculated ratio of map size to world size. We&rsquo;ll use another dictionary to assign active markers to each object. The key will be the object (ie the or instance) and the value the assigned marker.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>In we&rsquo;ll center the player&rsquo;s marker at the center of the grid. and calculate the scale factor. (<strong>Note:</strong> you&rsquo;ll need to connect the signal and do both of these things in the callback if you have a dynamically sized UI).</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle warning\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-triangle\"></i> Nodes in Containers</div><div class=box-content><p>Due to the way that <i class=gd-Container></i> nodes handle their children, at time you won&rsquo;t get the correct value for the child&rsquo;s size. For this reason, we need to wait until the next frame to get the Grid&rsquo;s size.</p></div></div><p>We&rsquo;ll also create markers for every game object (using the &ldquo;minimap_objects&rdquo; group) by duplicating the matching marker node and tying the marker to the object via the dictionary:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now that we have created the markers and linked each one to an object, we can update their positions in . If no is assigned, we&rsquo;ll do nothing:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>If there is a , we&rsquo;ll first rotate the player marker to match the player&rsquo;s heading. Since our sprite points upwards rather than along the axis, we must add 90 degrees:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Next, we&rsquo;ll find each object&rsquo;s position relative to the player and use that to find the marker&rsquo;s position (remembering to offset by because the control&rsquo;s origin is in the top left corner).</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The problem with this is that markers can be placed outside the grid:</p><p><a href=#image-39c151a78012dbdf0091bffa472b125a class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_09.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-39c151a78012dbdf0091bffa472b125a><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_09.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>To fix this, after calculating , but before setting the marker&rsquo;s position, clamp it to the grid&rsquo;s rectangle:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-caca114e58b46647705c46df9b440e71 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_11.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-caca114e58b46647705c46df9b440e71><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_11.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We can also decide what to do about markers that are &ldquo;off-screen&rdquo; - when they would be outside the grid&rsquo;s rectangle. Choose one of the following options (do this also before using ). The first option is to hide them:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The second is to change their appearance, in this case we&rsquo;ll make them smaller to show they&rsquo;re at a farther distance:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-e7f1eec05f2c12388eac88a571ab3512 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_12.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e7f1eec05f2c12388eac88a571ab3512><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_12.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=removing-objects>Removing objects</h3><p>If a mob gets killed or a crate picked up, the game will crash because the marker reference is no longer valid. We need a way to ensure markers are removed when the object is. Here&rsquo;s a quick way to do this in our rudimentary demo setup:</p><p>Add to any object that you&rsquo;ve put in the &ldquo;minimap_objects&rdquo; group. Emit this signal when the object is destroyed (or collected), along with a reference to itself so the map can identify it:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>In the of the main script, connect these signals to the minimap:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now add the receiving function to the minimap script to free the marker and remove the reference:</p><div class=highlight></div><h3 id=adjusting-zoom>Adjusting zoom</h3><p>If you&rsquo;ve stuck with it this far, we have one more feature to add: adjustable zoom level. With this, scrolling the mouse wheel when hovering over the map will zoom its scale in and out.</p><p>First, add a setter to the property:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>On the node, connect the signal in the Inspector so we can process the scroll wheel events:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>That&rsquo;s it - observe the effect of scrolling in and out:</p><p><a href=#image-2aeef522310c8a1d8355a71cbbe05046 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_10.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-2aeef522310c8a1d8355a71cbbe05046><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/minimap_10.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>While this is a pretty big recipe, I&rsquo;ve tried to make it flexible enough for you to incorporate into whatever project you&rsquo;re working on.</p><p>Some other things you might want to add:</p><ul><li>More marker types for different game objects.</li><li>Adding new units when they&rsquo;re spawned (hint: use a signal just like we did for removing units).</li><li>Clicking on a marker to get info about it.</li><li>Use a picture of your map as the minimap background instead of the grid.</li></ul><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/minimap target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/minimap</a></p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/topdown_movement/>Top-down character</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

