#: 4.x/ui/heart_containers_3/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=heart-containers-3-ways>Heart Containers: 3 Ways</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You need to display a heart container bar (or other icon-based bar).</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>A common way of displaying the player&rsquo;s health is via a series of icons (typically hearts) that disappear as the player takes damage.</p><p>In this recipe, we&rsquo;re going to explore three ways of displaying this information, which I&rsquo;m labeling &ldquo;simple&rdquo;, &ldquo;empty&rdquo;, and &ldquo;partial&rdquo;:</p><p><a href=#image-23e3f2e375e180855fb7a5232e30ab48 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/heart_bar_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-23e3f2e375e180855fb7a5232e30ab48><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/heart_bar_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>This image shows what the bar displays when the player has health.</p><ul><li><strong>simple</strong>: Only the hearts are displayed.</li><li><strong>empty</strong>: Empty heart containers are displayed.</li><li><strong>partial</strong>: The player can have partially filled containers.</li></ul><h3 id=setting-up-the-bar>Setting up the bar</h3><p>The heart images I&rsquo;m using are 53x45. You can get them here:</p><p><a href=https://kenney.nl/assets/platformer-art-deluxe target=_blank>Kenney.nl: Platformer Art Deluxe</a></p><p>Ideally, your heart bar will be easy to drop into your overall HUD/UI. It therefore makes sense to make it a separate scene. We&rsquo;ll start with an <i class=gd-HBoxContainer></i> which will keep things aligned. Set the <strong>Theme Overrides/Constants/Separation</strong> to .</p><p>Add a <i class=gd-TextureRect></i> child. Drag your heart texture into the <strong>Texture</strong> property and set the <strong>Stretch Mode</strong> to &ldquo;Keep&rdquo;. Name the node &ldquo;1&rdquo; and then press &ldquo;Ctrl-D&rdquo; to duplicate the node for as many hearts as you need (5 in this example). Your node setup should look like this:</p><p><a href=#image-e4fe508140249166509c1e0a1472588f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/heart_bar_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e4fe508140249166509c1e0a1472588f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/heart_bar_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=adding-a-script>Adding a script</h3><p>The script below will cover all three bar configurations for flexibility. You&rsquo;ll probably only need one in your game, so you can remove the code relating to the other modes.</p><p>To begin, we&rsquo;re going to load the textures we need and define our three bar modes:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Calling on the bar will cause it to display the passed value, based on the selected mode.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>We&rsquo;re not going to do any bounds checking on the value input. There are many ways you may have health implemented in your game, and so that&rsquo;s left to you.</p></div></div><p>First, the method. Here, we loop through the heart containers and set the visibility of each <i class=gd-TextureRect></i>:</p><div class=highlight></div><p> is very similar, except instead of hiding the icon, we change its texture to the empty container:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Finally, for the partially filled containers, we have a third texture and twice the number of possible values:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Here&rsquo;s an example using each of the bar modes:</p><p><a href=#image-fa77395eab16e89d52b908e40e08a221 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/heart_bar_04.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-fa77395eab16e89d52b908e40e08a221><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/heart_bar_04.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>Use this heart bar setup as a basis for your own HUD. This technique can be expanded to support a wide variety of information displays.</p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/heart_bars target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/heart_bars</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

