#: 4.x/3d/simple_airplane/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=arcade-style-airplane>Arcade-style Airplane</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want to make an airplane controller in 3D, but don&rsquo;t need a fully accurate flight-simulator.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>In this recipe, we&rsquo;re going to make a <em>simplified</em> airplane controller. By &ldquo;simplified&rdquo; we mean stripping things down to the basics. We&rsquo;re looking for the &ldquo;feel&rdquo; of flying a plane - one that you can just jump in and start flying effortlessly, with a minimal control scheme.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>This recipe is <em>not</em> an accurate flight simulator. We are not simulating aerodynamics, so this doesn&rsquo;t fly like a <em>real</em> airplane. We&rsquo;re going for simplicity and &ldquo;fun&rdquo; here, not accuracy.</p></div></div><h3 id=node-setup>Node setup</h3><p>We&rsquo;re going to use a <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i> for this. Since we won&rsquo;t be simulating actual flight physics (lift, drag, etc.), we don&rsquo;t need <i class=gd-RigidBody3D></i> in this case.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s our model setup:</p><p><a href=#image-9e17aac5a44abe9d6bd7faa3e76f5f1b class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-9e17aac5a44abe9d6bd7faa3e76f5f1b><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We&rsquo;re using a cylinder for the collision shape, sized to match the plane&rsquo;s fuselage. This will allow for detecting the ground, which is all we&rsquo;re concerned with for this demo.</p><p>To start the script, let&rsquo;s look at our plane&rsquo;s properties:</p><div class=highlight></div><h3 id=controls>Controls</h3><p>We&rsquo;ll need the following input actions for our controls. We&rsquo;re using a game controller in this demo, but you can add keyboard inputs as well if you like.</p><p><a href=#image-e27504cf43881db5aa0d05a13a3031d9 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e27504cf43881db5aa0d05a13a3031d9><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>This function captures the inputs and sets the input values. Note that increasing/decreasing throttle changes the , not the actual speed. This will allow us to acclerate/decelerate from the current speed to the target speed.</p><div class=highlight></div><h3 id=movement>Movement</h3><p>Movement happens in , first lerping the speed towards the target speed and then using :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>To test, add the plane to a test scene (don&rsquo;t forget a <i class=gd-Camera3D></i> ). Press the input and you should see the plane accelerate forward.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>We&rsquo;re using the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/interpolated_camera/>Interpolated Camera</a> recipe in this demo.</p></div></div><p><a href=#image-c72ec84276a9cfe4e3f0cf37565f2c98 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_03.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-c72ec84276a9cfe4e3f0cf37565f2c98><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_03.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Next, let&rsquo;s handle changing the pitch of the plane. Add this right after calling in :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Run the scene again and try pitching up and down:</p><p><a href=#image-7dc8c67b4d6eb94f088c1b02499fdce7 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_04.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-7dc8c67b4d6eb94f088c1b02499fdce7><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_04.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>After that, add the following for the turn input:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-90bbe54847dfa618e5d9c6517b3f6912 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_05.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-90bbe54847dfa618e5d9c6517b3f6912><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_05.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Notice that while the plane turns, it doesn&rsquo;t really look natural. Airplanes <em>bank</em> when they turn, so let&rsquo;s animate that by changing the rotation of the mesh:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-925bfe76b7f595e31cc525bff6b63682 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_06.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-925bfe76b7f595e31cc525bff6b63682><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_06.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Where is a reference to the <i class=gd-MeshInstance3D></i> in the plane scene (in the example, this is ).</p><p>The amount of roll is related to the so a shallow turn banks less. Going straight will &ldquo;auto&rdquo; level the plane.</p><p>That&rsquo;s it! You now have the basic flying controls working correctly, and it should feel comfortable and natural to fly around. Try adjusting the various properties to see how they affect the movement.</p><h3 id=landingtaking-off>Landing/taking off</h3><p>While the above is fine for flying, it doesn&rsquo;t handle the ground very well. Here, we&rsquo;ll simulate landing using a simplistic approach (by &ldquo;simplistic&rdquo;, we mean doing so in a very basic way - you&rsquo;ll probably want to expand on it depending on what your game may need).</p><p>First, we&rsquo;ll want to distinguish between being on the ground and being in the air. On the ground, we can slow down to 0; in the air, we must maintain the minimum airspeed. Also, when on the ground, we won&rsquo;t bank when turning (we don&rsquo;t want our wings digging into the ground!).</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Meanwhile, in the function, we&rsquo;ll also take into account when throttling down and when pitching down, and only allow takeoff if above :</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-39fddeae9876303312ae09568159a4fc class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_07.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-39fddeae9876303312ae09568159a4fc><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kb_plane_07.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=full-script>Full script</h3><p>Here&rsquo;s the full script:</p><div class=expand><input type=checkbox id=expand-6 aria-controls=expandcontent-6> <label class=expand-label for=expand-6><i class=\"fas fa-chevron-down\"></i> <i class=\"fas fa-chevron-right\"></i> Click to expand&mldr;</label><div id=expandcontent-6 class=expand-content><div class=highlight></div></div></div><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>You can adapt this technique to a variety of arcade-style flying games. For example, for mouse control, you could use the property of to set the pitch and turn input.</p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/3d_airplane_demo target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/3d_airplane_demo</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

