#: 4.x/3d/rotate_interpolate/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=smooth-rotation>Smooth rotation</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want to smoothly rotate a 3D object to point in a new direction.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>When you first encounter this problem, you may find yourself thinking in terms of <em>Euler angles</em> - the three values representing the angles to the <strong>x/y/z</strong> axes. While Godot will allow you to see the object&rsquo;s Euler angles in the property, it is not recommended to use them to work in 3D. There are a number of reasons why this the case, such as a problem called &ldquo;gimbal lock&rdquo;, where you lose one degree of freedom when one of your rotations reaches 90 degrees.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Info</div><div class=box-content><p>If you&rsquo;re interested in the background behind Euler angles and the problems they introduce, like gimbal lock, here&rsquo;s a <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zc8b2Jo7mno\" target=_blank>video that explains it well</a>.</p></div></div><p>We can avoid using 3D Euler angles in Godot by using the object&rsquo;s property. This property represents the body&rsquo;s position <em>and</em> orientation in space. It uses a mathematical construct called a <em>matrix</em> to do this, but you don&rsquo;t really need to understand the underlying math in order to make use of it.</p><h3 id=look_at>look_at()</h3><p>Let&rsquo;s say you have a 3D object such as a missile or arrow and you want it to point at its target. You can do this using the <i class=gd-Node3D></i> method :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This code would make our node () always point at the target&rsquo;s position, no matter how it moves.</p><p><a href=#image-0b0337b2affe76bde983ec8a87830dd0 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_rotate_01.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-0b0337b2affe76bde983ec8a87830dd0><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_rotate_01.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Note that requires 2 parameters: the target position, and an &ldquo;up vector&rdquo;. Imagine an airplane pointing its nose towards a target - there are an infinite number of ways it could be oriented, because the plane could roll about its axis. This second parameter is how you define what you want the final orientation to be.</p><h3 id=smooth-rotation>Smooth rotation</h3><p>The above code works, but it snaps the rotation instantly to the target. This might be fine if you have a very slow-moving target, but looks unnatural. It would look better if we move smoothly, or &ldquo;interpolated&rdquo;, the rotation smoothly between the starting orientation and the ending.</p><p>Godot has us covered here too. Rather than , we can use the object&rsquo;s method, which doesn&rsquo;t rotate the node, but returns the transform that <em>would</em> be looking at the target. Combine this with the method, which returns an intermediate transform between a current one and a target one, and we can smoothly transition between the current orientation and our desired one.</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-67e74e6b0922be917f22f914fc9d7817 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_rotate_02.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-67e74e6b0922be917f22f914fc9d7817><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_rotate_02.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Note that since operates on the , it can be used to interpolate both rotation <em>and</em> position of an object.</p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>That&rsquo;s it! Use this handy method to rotate your 3D objects, and stop thinking about angles!</p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

