#: 4.x/3d/characterbody3d_examples/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=characterbody3d-movement>CharacterBody3D: Movement</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You need a player-controlled 3D character body.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>For this recipe, we&rsquo;ll be using this adorable tank model:</p><p><a href=#image-bb5efa2cec52109dafe1ee5705a07d35 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_kinematic_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-bb5efa2cec52109dafe1ee5705a07d35><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_kinematic_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>You can grab this model on Itch.io: <a href=https://gtibo.itch.io/mini-tank target=_blank>https://gtibo.itch.io/mini-tank</a> or use any other model you&rsquo;d like. We won&rsquo;t be doing anything that&rsquo;s tank-specific here.</p><p>In the case of this asset, the download includes an OBJ file, and we&rsquo;ll find it more convenient if we import it as a scene:</p><p><a href=#image-530fea70550cebda9c3c98d9ea460c6e class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/obj_as_scene.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-530fea70550cebda9c3c98d9ea460c6e><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/obj_as_scene.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We can add the model to the scene, but we&rsquo;ll need a couple of additional nodes:</p><p><a href=#image-2982af1bdfd00a832b55937da09c5a9e class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/mini_tank_nodes.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-2982af1bdfd00a832b55937da09c5a9e><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/mini_tank_nodes.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>For the collision shape, we&rsquo;re just going to use a aligned and sized with the tank&rsquo;s treads. is a <i class=gd-Position3D></i> we&rsquo;ll use to place our following camera. It&rsquo;s placed behind and above the tank, angled down.</p><p>We&rsquo;ve also rotated the individual <i class=gd-MeshInstance3D></i> nodes degrees around the <strong>Y</strong> axis. This is because they were modeled facing towards <strong>+Z</strong>, but <strong>-Z</strong> is the forward direction in Godot, and we don&rsquo;t want our tank to look like it&rsquo;s backwards.</p><p>Before we add a script, open the &ldquo;Project Settings&rdquo; and add the following inputs on the &ldquo;Input Map&rdquo; tab:</p><table><thead><tr><th style=text-align:left>Input Action</th><th style=text-align:left>Key</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=text-align:left>forward</td><td style=text-align:left><strong>W</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=text-align:left>back</td><td style=text-align:left><strong>S</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=text-align:left>right</td><td style=text-align:left><strong>D</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=text-align:left>left</td><td style=text-align:left><strong>A</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Now let&rsquo;s add a script, starting with the required variables:</p><div class=highlight></div><p> is the tank&rsquo;s movement speed (forward and back), while defines how fast it can turn.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>Declaring properties with makes it easy to adjust them in the Inspector.</p></div></div><p>Using the method makes our movement code quite simple:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>With this code, we add the downward acceleration of gravity to the current velocity, get the user&rsquo;s input (more about that below), and call .</p><p>Next we need to define , where we&rsquo;ll process and apply the input actions:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Let&rsquo;s examine this more closely. Player input should affect horizontal movement: forward/back along the ground, and rotation around the tank&rsquo;s center. Movement in the <strong>Y</strong> direction should only be affected by gravity, which means we don&rsquo;t want to set it to every frame. This is why we&rsquo;re using the variable to temporarily hold that value while we assign a new velocity vector for the horizontal movement, then add it back in at the end.</p><p>For the forward and back movement, we&rsquo;re using so that we&rsquo;ll move in our body&rsquo;s <em>local</em> forward direction.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s the tank in action. We&rsquo;ve made a test scene with a <i class=gd-StaticBody3D></i> plane for the ground and an <i class=gd-Camera3D></i> using the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/interpolated_camera/index.html>Interpolated Camera</a> recipe.</p><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_kinematic_09.webm></video></p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>This is the basis of movement for any kind of kinematic character. From here you can add jumping, shooting, AI behavior, etc. See the related recipes for examples that build on this recipe.</p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/characterbody3d_examples target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/characterbody3d_examples</a></p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/g101/3d/>Intro to 3D</a></li><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/input/input_actions/>Input Actions</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

