#: 4.x/3d/basic_fps/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=basic-fps-character>Basic FPS Character</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You need to make a first-person shooter (FPS) character.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Start with a <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i> node, and add a <i class=gd-CollisionShape3D></i> to it. The <i class=gd-CapsuleShape3D></i> collision shape is the most common choice. Depending on your world setup, you may want to add additional shapes here, but for the purposes of this example, we&rsquo;ll stick to the basics.</p><p>We&rsquo;ll leave all the sizing at the default values, meaning the capsule will be 2 meters high. Move it up by m to align its bottom with the ground.</p><p>Next, add a <i class=gd-Camera3D></i> as a child of the body and move it up about m.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Where&rsquo;s the body?</div><div class=box-content><p>For this example, we&rsquo;ll leave the character &ldquo;bodyless&rdquo; - meaning we&rsquo;re not adding a mesh to display for the player&rsquo;s body. Depending on your setup, you may or may not need to see the player&rsquo;s body.</p></div></div><p>Attach a script to the body and start by defining some properties:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The function is the place to handle movement. Note that returns a 2-dimensional vector based on the combination of the forward/back/left/right keys. We want to use this vector to set the and components of the body&rsquo;s velocity (because is handled by gravity). Multiplying this vector by the body&rsquo;s ensures we account for rotation - forward should always be the <em>body&rsquo;s</em> forward vector.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Don&rsquo;t forget to add the input actions to your <strong>Input Map</strong> using the keys/inputs you prefer (W/A/S/D is typical, or you can use joystick axes if you prefer a controller).</p><p>Add the player to a &ldquo;World&rdquo; scene where you&rsquo;ve created some <i class=gd-StaticBody3D></i> nodes for the floor and some walls.</p><p>When you try to move, you&rsquo;ll notice you can move forward/back and left/right, but you can&rsquo;t rotate. That&rsquo;s what we&rsquo;ll handle next.</p><h2 id=mouse-control-in-3d>Mouse control in 3D</h2><p>First, we need the player to rotate left/right when we move the mouse the same way. Mouse input is represented in 2D, relative to the screen, so we need the movement of the mouse to rotate the player&rsquo;s body around its (vertical) axis. The property we defined above lets us adjust how many pixels of mouse movement translate to a degree of rotation.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Try the code again, and you&rsquo;ll see that you can now rotate with the mouse. However, you may find your mouse running outside the game window. This is the perfect time to add some code to capture your mouse. See <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/input/mouse_capture/>Input: Capturing the Mouse</a> for details.</p><p>Our updated code then becomes</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Finally, to look up/down, we&rsquo;ll use the motion of the mouse to tilt the camera. We don&rsquo;t want it to turn completely upside-down, though, so we&rsquo;ll the rotation to a reasonable value of 70 degrees.</p><div class=highlight></div><h2 id=holding-a-weapon>Holding a weapon</h2><p><a href=#image-d2936de01fd6e32ade5298cab1b53770 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/fps_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-d2936de01fd6e32ade5298cab1b53770><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/fps_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>An FPS character typically has a 3D mesh of a weapon positioned in front. Setting this up can be easy with a couple of Godot editor tricks.</p><p>Add your weapon mesh as a child of the <i class=gd-Camera3D></i>. Then, in the editor view menu, choose &ldquo;2 Viewports&rdquo; and set one of them to preview the camera. Then, you can move around the weapon and easily see how it will look from the player&rsquo;s perspective.</p><p>To add a little personality, try using an <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> to animate the weapon&rsquo;s position from side-to-side as the player moves.</p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/input/mouse_capture/>Input: Capturing the Mouse</a></li></ul><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/basic_fps target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/basic_fps</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

