#: 4.x/3d/spaceship/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=arcade-style-spaceship>Arcade-style Spaceship</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want to make a 3D spaceship that flies in an arcade/cinematic way. You&rsquo;re not looking for realistic physics, but more of a dog-fighting, &ldquo;Star Wars&rdquo;-style of spaceflight.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>To accomplish this, we&rsquo;ll use a <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i> for the ship. The three axis inputs (<em>pitch</em>, <em>roll</em>, and <em>yaw</em>) will rotate the body&rsquo;s around the corresponding axis. The direction of motion will always point forward.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>You can do this with <i class=gd-RigidBody3D></i> and get the same results. See the example project linked below, which includes a rigid body version as well.</p></div></div><h3 id=assets>Assets</h3><p>Spaceship models are from this asset pack:</p><p><a href=https://quaternius.com/packs/ultimatespaceships.html target=_blank>Ultimate Spaceships Pack by Quaternius</a></p><p>I&rsquo;ve chosen the &ldquo;Executioner&rdquo; ship model:</p><p><a href=#image-05cbad0f812b6555be21314cb2ac2cc3 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_ship_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-05cbad0f812b6555be21314cb2ac2cc3><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_ship_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Feel free to choose your favorite design.</p><h3 id=setup>Setup</h3><p>Select the file of the ship you want, and click the <em>Import</em> tab. Change the <em>Root Type</em> to <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i> and click &ldquo;Reimport&rdquo;. Then double-click the and you&rsquo;ll have a new inherited scene with a <i class=gd-KinematicBody3D></i> root and a <i class=gd-MeshInstance3D></i> child. Add a <i class=gd-CollisionShape3D></i> to the body.</p><p>In <em>Project Settings -> Input Map</em>, set up the following inputs:</p><ul><li>/ </li><li> / </li><li> / </li><li> / </li></ul><p>You can assign keys or controller inputs. Analog stick inputs will work best.</p><h3 id=movement>Movement</h3><p>To start the script, let&rsquo;s handle the forward movement. Pressing the throttle buttons smoothly increases/decreases the speed.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Make a test scene with a <i class=gd-Camera3D></i> to try it out. You can use a stationary camera or a <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/interpolated_camera/>chase camera</a>. Check that the ship accelerates and slows before moving on to the next step.</p><p><a href=#image-142f8524b4275f5278a5c7d51c49cddb class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_ship_02.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-142f8524b4275f5278a5c7d51c49cddb><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_ship_02.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=rotation>Rotation</h3><p>Now we can handle rotation in the three axes. Add the following variables at the top of the script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The three axis speeds will affect the &ldquo;handling&rdquo; of the ship. Experiment to find values the work for you and your desired flight style.</p><p>Next, add these lines to to capture the three axis inputs:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Finally, we need to rotate the ship&rsquo;s according to the inputs. Note how each input affects one axis of rotation:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-582ecc6693b0494bbbd965bf8e9c0116 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_ship_04.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-582ecc6693b0494bbbd965bf8e9c0116><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_ship_04.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=improvements>Improvements</h3><p>Currently the rotations are a little to &ldquo;sharp&rdquo;. The ship starts and stops rotating instantly, which feels a bit too unnatural. We can solve this with , and by adding one more configuration variable to set how &ldquo;floaty&rdquo; we&rsquo;d like the controls to be:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Change the three axis inputs in to the following:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now when stopping or changing direction, there&rsquo;s a little bit of inertia.</p><p><a href=#image-8f3ac74a702cc6534efcef73456947f4 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_ship_03.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-8f3ac74a702cc6534efcef73456947f4><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_ship_03.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h4 id=linking-rollyaw>Linking roll/yaw</h4><p>One problem with this control scheme is that it&rsquo;s awkward. Having to use a separate stick for the yaw input makes it difficult to control, especially when also shooting and using other controls. Many games solve this by linking the roll input to also apply a small amount of yaw. To do this, change the to around 1/4 to 1/2 of the .</p><p>In the function, change the line getting to the following:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This is another fun place to experiment by changing the roll and yaw speeds. For example, what if yaw was primary and roll smaller? What if other axes were linked? If your game has different ships, you can give them different values for variety in flight styles/performance.</p><h3 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h3><p>That&rsquo;s it, now you can fly! This controller is a great start for whatever space-based game you might have in mind. Add some other ships, and a few effects, and you&rsquo;re ready go:</p><p><video width=500 controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_ship_05.webm></video></p><h3 id=full-script>Full script</h3><p>Here&rsquo;s the complete script:</p><div class=highlight></div><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/interpolated_camera/>Interpolated Camera</a></li></ul><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/3d_spaceship target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/3d_spaceship</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

