#: 4.x/3d/assets/importing_assets/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=importing-assets>Importing Assets</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You&rsquo;ve downloaded (or created) a set of 3D assets, including rigged and animated characters, and you want to import it into Godot.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>For this example, we&rsquo;ll assume you&rsquo;ve downloaded the art packs linked in the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/assets/>section description</a> and unzipped them.</p><p>Before copying the files into your Godot project, notice that there are multiple versions of the assets in different file formats: OBJ, FBX, and GLTF. There are also some extra files such as examples and separate textures in case you want to modify them. We don&rsquo;t need all of that, and GLTF is the preferred import format for Godot. So make sure you&rsquo;re only dragging the folder or files (or , which is the binary version of the same) into your project folder.</p><p>Here, I&rsquo;ve taken the folder from the &ldquo;Dungeon&rdquo; pack and the folder from the &ldquo;Adventurers&rdquo; pack and dragged them into my project.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>There are a lot of files in the Dungeon pack - Godot may take a little time to read them all!</p></div></div><h2 id=importing-a-character>Importing a Character</h2><p>Select the file in the FileSystem tab, then click the <strong>Import</strong> tab at the top left.</p><p><a href=#image-e238abe9c2518688993bfcbbbb0a6f41 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_tab.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e238abe9c2518688993bfcbbbb0a6f41><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_tab.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Here you&rsquo;ll find some basic import settings, but we can go into more detail. Click <strong>Advanced</strong> button and you&rsquo;ll see a new window appear:</p><p><a href=#image-d1b43de81b4cdb01a10272b6eacab21f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_adv.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-d1b43de81b4cdb01a10272b6eacab21f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_adv.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>One the left you&rsquo;ll see all the data that is contained in the GLTF scene, including textures and animations. Note all the weapon options attached to the character and the extensive list of animations.</p><p>There&rsquo;s a preview of the character in the middle, and a set of options on the right side where you can adjust how the selected item is configured.</p><p>Since we will code our player as a <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i>, we can go ahead and specify that node type here. Click on the and on the right set the <strong>Root Type</strong> to <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i>.</p><h3 id=animations>Animations</h3><p>Scroll down to the list of animations. You&rsquo;ll see that there are many, but while some we&rsquo;ll only want to play once, such as attacks, others like &ldquo;Idle&rdquo; and &ldquo;Running&rdquo;, we&rsquo;d like to be looping. For any animation like this, select the animation name and set the <strong>Loop Mode</strong> to &ldquo;Linear&rdquo;. Do this for all of the &ldquo;Walking&rdquo;, &ldquo;Running&rdquo;, and &ldquo;Idle&rdquo; variations. When you&rsquo;re done, click the <strong>Reimport</strong> button at the bottom.</p><p><a href=#image-6958f4f68ee171d9aaad9568fa5b1096 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_loop.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6958f4f68ee171d9aaad9568fa5b1096><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_loop.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Setting Loop Automatically</div><div class=box-content><p>If you are making your own characters, you can skip this step by ensuring that your animations&rsquo; names end with . For details on this and other <em>import hints</em>, see <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.html#import-hints target=_blank>Import Hints</a> in the Godot documentation.</p></div></div><p>Right click in the <strong>FileSystem</strong> and choose <em>New Inherited Scene</em>.</p><p>In this scene you&rsquo;ll see all the models and the <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> where you can test out the animations.</p><h2 id=importing-world-items>Importing World Items</h2><p>Importing objects for the environment will be a similar process. As an example, let&rsquo;s use one of the dungeon walls. There are a lot of files in the dungeon pack, so type &ldquo;wall&rdquo; in the file filter to help find it:</p><p><a href=#image-2305c5097d1a1cfec5bce675c50f4f51 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_wall.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-2305c5097d1a1cfec5bce675c50f4f51><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_wall.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We&rsquo;ll want our dungeon walls to be solid, and it would be painful to manually create a <i class=gd-StaticBody3D></i> and collision shape for each one. Fortunately, when importing, Godot can do this for us.</p><p>In the import window, select the mesh object. On the right side, check the <strong>Physics</strong> box, and set the <strong>Shape Type</strong> to &ldquo;Simple Convex&rdquo; (feel free to check out the other options too).</p><p><a href=#image-13715641c9558ee9f47e8f209eef35ab class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_physics.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-13715641c9558ee9f47e8f209eef35ab><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_import_physics.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Click <strong>Reimport</strong>. Now when using this in the game, Godot will automatically create a <i class=gd-StaticBody3D></i> with a collision shape to match.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Automating Collision Shapes</div><div class=box-content><p>As above, there is an import hint for collision shapes as well. In your Blender project, appending (or some other variations) will let the importer know to do this step automatically. See the <em>import hints</em> link for details.</p></div></div><h2 id=automating-imports>Automating Imports</h2><p>While adding import hints is the preferred method when making your own assets, it&rsquo;s not something you can do when downloading an asset pack like the one we&rsquo;re using.</p><p>It is possible to write an <em>import script</em> that can run on every imported node of a particular type. For example, we could automate the creation of the static collision we did above.</p><p>As an example, the following script will loop through all the nodes of the imported object and create a static collision on each mesh it finds.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>In the <strong>Import</strong> tab, you can set this as the <strong>Import Script</strong>, and when you click <strong>Reimport</strong>, the collisions will be created.</p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>That concludes the overview of importing 3D assets into Godot.</p><p>See the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/assets/>section description</a> for examples of working with the 3D assets you&rsquo;ve imported.</p><h4 id=companion-video>Companion Video</h4><div style=position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden><iframe src=https://www.youtube.com/embed/XRUWhE4OnOY style=position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;border:0 allowfullscreen title=\"YouTube Video\"></iframe></div><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

