#: 4.x/3d/assets/character_controller/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=character-controller>Character Controller</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You&rsquo;ve imported a rigged, animated 3D character in Godot and set up its animations using <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i>. Now you need to implement movement: you need a character controller.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>In this recipe, we&rsquo;ll assume you&rsquo;ve already imported your character model and animations, and that you&rsquo;re set up <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> to handle transitioning and blending the animations. If you haven&rsquo;t yet, see <a href=/4.x/3d/assets/importing_assets/>Importing Assets</a> and <a href=/4.x/3d/assets/character_animation/>Character Animation</a> for details. As a reminder, we&rsquo;re using the art packs linked in the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/assets/>section description</a>.</p><h3 id=adding-collision>Adding collision</h3><p>We&rsquo;ve chosen <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i> as the root node of the imported scene, and it&rsquo;s complaining about a missing collision shape, so let&rsquo;s fix that first. Add a <i class=gd-CollisionShape3D></i> child and choose <i class=gd-CapsuleShape3D></i> as its <strong>Shape</strong> property.</p><p>Size and position the capsule to enclose the character&rsquo;s body. For reference, here are the values I used:</p><p><a href=#image-affa83d4ea345126b295fbde74306079 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3dchar_capsule.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-affa83d4ea345126b295fbde74306079><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3dchar_capsule.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Note that the imported rig is positioned so that its feet are on the &ldquo;ground&rdquo;, ie at the body&rsquo;s position. This will be helpful later, as the player&rsquo;s position will represent its position on the ground, rather than floating in mid-air if it were at the center of its body.</p><p>If you&rsquo;re familiar with Godot&rsquo;s 3D orientation, you&rsquo;ll also notice that the character is facing the <strong>+Z</strong> direction, which is backwards. Select the <i class=gd-Skeleton3D></i> node and set its <strong>Y</strong> <strong>Rotation</strong> to to correct this.</p><h3 id=input-actions>Input actions</h3><p>In the <strong>Input Map</strong>, we&rsquo;re using the following inputs: , , , , and . Assign them to whatever keys/buttons you prefer.</p><h3 id=camera>Camera</h3><p>There are many ways to handle a 3D camera that follows the player. For this example, we&rsquo;ll use a <i class=gd-SpringArm3D></i> as the camera &ldquo;mount&rdquo;.</p><p>The <i class=gd-SpringArm3D></i> node works by casting a ray and then moving its children to the collision point. Using this for a camera means nothing can get between the camera and the player, and we can implement zoom by varying this length.</p><p>Add one as a child of the root node, and then add a <i class=gd-Camera3D></i> as a child of that.</p><p>In the spring arm&rsquo;s properties, set <strong>Spring Length</strong> to , the <strong>Margin</strong> to , and the <strong>Position</strong> to .</p><p>We don&rsquo;t want the spring arm to collide with the player&rsquo;s capsule shape, so in the root <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i> set the collision layer to . Since the spring arm is checking collision layer , that will prevent the camera hitting the player&rsquo;s head.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Collision Layers</div><div class=box-content><p>Eventually, we&rsquo;ll want to organize our collision layers for various game objects: player, environment, enemies, etc.</p></div></div><h3 id=movement>Movement</h3><p>Now we are ready to add a script to the player. We&rsquo;ll start with the variables we&rsquo;ll need:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>And then, some references to the nodes we&rsquo;ll need to access:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ll use the reference to set the blend position for the Idle/Walk/Run blendspace and the trigger conditions for jumping. Select the <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> and you can see these properties in the Inspector:</p><p><a href=#image-58fc0a1df47d380ca035e9d682bf5d69 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3dchar_animtree_properties.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-58fc0a1df47d380ca035e9d682bf5d69><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3dchar_animtree_properties.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p> is a reference to the animation state machine, which we can use to call transitions between animations. See the <a href=/4.x/3d/assets/character_animation/>Character Animation</a> recipe for how we set these up.</p><p>Movement is a matter of getting the player&rsquo;s input and calling :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The player&rsquo;s input should be applied to the horizontal motion only (<strong>X</strong> and <strong>Z</strong> axes), since gravity is acting on the <strong>Y</strong> axis. For that reason, we&rsquo;ll temporarily zero out the , set the input, and then restore the value when we&rsquo;re done.</p><p>Note that we&rsquo;re rotating the input vector using the camera&rsquo;s rotation - our character is going to move forward in whatever direction the camera is facing.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Before we do anything else, this is a good point to test things out. You can make a quick test scene with a big <i class=gd-StaticBody3D></i> for the ground, or start making a scene using the dungeon pack assets.</p><p>You should be able to move forward/back/left/right (without any animations yet).</p><h3 id=camera-control>Camera Control</h3><p>Now let&rsquo;s get the camera working. We want to control the camera with mouse movement. We&rsquo;ll add a variable that lets us adjust the sensitivity.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Then, we want to detect mouse motion and rotate the spring arm accordingly. Rotating the arm around the <strong>X</strong> axis tilts it up and down (using the mouse&rsquo;s <em>y</em> motion), and rotating it around <strong>Y</strong> changes its facing direction (using the mouse&rsquo;s <em>x</em> motion). We also clamp the camera&rsquo;s tilt so that it doesn&rsquo;t go too far up/down.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Try it out and you should see when pressing &ldquo;forward&rdquo;, the character moves in the direction the camera faces.</p><p>Now we need to rotate the character so they face in the direction of movement.</p><p>We&rsquo;ll add a variable for the rotation speed, so that we don&rsquo;t snap instantly to the new heading.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>And then add this in , after :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Using ensures we&rsquo;ll always rotate the shortest direction to the new angle (rather than going the long way around from a 359° rotation to a 1° rotation, for example).</p><h3 id=iwr-animations>IWR Animations</h3><p>Now that we have movement and rotation, we need to choose animations. The idea is to take the character&rsquo;s horizontal velocity (the <em>x/z</em> movement) and use it to set the blend position in the blendspace we created.</p><p>In , we&rsquo;re setting the player&rsquo;s velocity. Just after that, we can set the blend position:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Since is in global space, but the character model is rotating, we need to transform into model space using the model&rsquo;s . Once we have that, we need to map that 3D vector to the 2D vector of the blend space, dividing by so that we&rsquo;ll get values between and . Also, <em>-z</em> is forward, but <em>+y</em> represents the blendspace forward animation, so we negate the value to make them match.</p><p>Note that you can get that parameter path by looking at the Inspector for the <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> - you can even drag it into the script window to fill it in.</p><h3 id=attacks>Attacks</h3><p>We can handle attacks by first adding an input action called , which I&rsquo;ve assigned to the left mouse button.</p><p>Since we have 3 separate attacks in the <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i>, we&rsquo;ll make a list of them:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Then, in , pick a random animation from the list when the action is pressed:</p><div class=highlight></div><h3 id=jumping>Jumping</h3><p>Jumping is a little bit more involved, because it involves three separate animations. As a reminder, this is how we set up the state machine:</p><p><a href=#image-7fdd67a7afc3ec351bbebbe3c60e6083 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_tree_jumping.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-7fdd67a7afc3ec351bbebbe3c60e6083><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_tree_jumping.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>First, we want to transition to the &ldquo;Jump_Start&rdquo; animation by setting . This triggers the transition in the state machine.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Next, we need to know when we touch the ground, so we can transition out of the &ldquo;Jump_Idle&rdquo; animation. To do this, we need to keep track of our grounded status by comparing it with the previous frame. Add a new variable at the top:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>And then this statement after the first one above:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Finally, there&rsquo;s the direct transition to &ldquo;Jump_Idle&rdquo; that happens if we step off a ledge:</p><div class=highlight></div><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>We&rsquo;ve now got a functional, controllable character with a chase camera and multiple animations. What&rsquo;s next?</p><p>See the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/assets/>section description</a> for more examples of working in 3D and for example Godot projects you can download.</p><h4 id=companion-video>Companion Video</h4><div style=position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden><iframe src=https://www.youtube.com/embed/AW3rT-7J8ag style=position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;border:0 allowfullscreen title=\"YouTube Video\"></iframe></div><footer class=footline></footer>"
