#: 4.x/3d/assets/level_design/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=designing-a-level>Designing a Level</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>We have several options to consider for designing our level:</p><h3 id=options>Options</h3><h4 id=gridmap>GridMap</h4><p>Using the built-in <i class=gd-GridMap></i> node allows you to layout meshes in a grid in the editor. It&rsquo;s similar in concept to the 2D <i class=gd-TileMap></i> node, though with less functionality.</p><p>This option has a few drawbacks:</p><ol><li>The GridMap functionality in Godot is fairly rudimentary (especially compared to its 2D equivalent) and hasn&rsquo;t been updated in some time.</li><li>Placement is limited to a fixed grid layout, so you don&rsquo;t have as much control over how your meshes are placed.</li></ol><h4 id=directly-placing-models>Directly placing models</h4><p>Alternatively, you can turn on grid snapping in the editor and just drag scenes directly from the <strong>FileSystem</strong> tab to place them. This has the advantage of being more flexible than , although a bit more manual in how you go about placing objects.</p><h4 id=external-tool>External tool</h4><p>A third option is to use a separate tool to design your levels and then import them to Godot. <a href=https://blender.org/ target=_blank>Blender</a> is a very popular choice for this. Odds are, if you&rsquo;re making your own 3D assets, you&rsquo;re probably already using Blender to do your modeling.</p><p>Using Blender, you can use your familiar modeling tools to create your level and then export that as a for Godot. You can even use Godot <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.html#import-hints target=_blank>Import Hints</a> to have Godot automatically create collision shapes, lights, etc in your imported level.</p><p>You can even use Godot&rsquo;s built-in <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_scenes.html#importing-blend-files-directly-within-godot target=_blank>Blender support</a> to make this even easier. Changes to your file will instantly appear in your Godot project.</p><h3 id=interactable-objects>Interactable objects</h3><h4 id=door>Door</h4><p>Find the file in the <strong>FileSystem</strong> and double-click to check the import settings and see that you&rsquo;ve created a static collision for both meshes (the door frame and the door). Right-click to create a &ldquo;New Inherited Scene&rdquo;.</p><p>Add an <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> node, which we&rsquo;ll use to animate the door opening. Your node tree should look like this:</p><p><strong>SS</strong></p><p>The &ldquo;wall_doorway_scaffold_door&rdquo; is the mesh that we want to rotate. We want to be able to open the door in either direction, so we&rsquo;re going to create two animations. Both of them rotate the door&rsquo;s <strong>Y</strong> rotation by , but &ldquo;open+&rdquo; opens in the <strong>+Z</strong> direction and &ldquo;open-&rdquo; does the opposite. This way, when the player interacts with the door, we can open it away from them, no matter which side they&rsquo;re on.</p><p>Add the <i class=gd-StaticBody3D></i> child of the door to a group called &ldquo;interactable&rdquo;. This is the object the player is going to detect. If it&rsquo;s in that group, the player will call on it.</p><p>Add a script to the door scene:</p><div class=highlight></div><h4 id=chest>Chest</h4><p>Find the file in the <strong>FileSystem</strong> and double-click to check the import settings and see that you&rsquo;ve created a static collision for both meshes (the chest body and lid). Right-click to create a &ldquo;New Inherited Scene&rdquo;.</p><p>Add an <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> and create an animation of the chest lid opening.</p><p>Similar to the door, add the static body to the &ldquo;interactable&rdquo; group and an function in its script:</p><div class=highlight></div><footer class=footline></footer>"
