#: 4.x/3d/assets/character_animation/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=character-animation>Character Animation</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You&rsquo;ve got a rigged, animated 3D character (either made by you or downloaded from a third party) and you want to set up its animations in Godot.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>In this recipe, we&rsquo;ll assume you&rsquo;ve already imported your character model and animations. If you haven&rsquo;t yet, see <a href=/4.x/3d/assets/importing_assets/>Importing Assets</a> for details. As a reminder, we&rsquo;re using the art packs linked in the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/assets/>section description</a>.</p><h3 id=prepping-the-character>Prepping the character</h3><p>We&rsquo;ve chosen <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i> for our character, so your scene should look like this (I&rsquo;ve collapsed the node since the mesh list is so long):</p><p><a href=#image-0daf4cce0ac198ecd63e057ee377657b class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3dcharacter_imported_scene.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-0daf4cce0ac198ecd63e057ee377657b><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3dcharacter_imported_scene.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>The first thing you probably noticed is that the character&rsquo;s hands are full! The artist has helpfully provided all the weapons & shields attached and oriented at the correct points. You can go down the list and hide the ones you don&rsquo;t want to see.</p><p><a href=#image-96759247fa632a11aeb33dd48eb8cd6e class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3dcharacter_default_pose.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-96759247fa632a11aeb33dd48eb8cd6e><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3dcharacter_default_pose.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=about-the-animationtree>About the AnimationTree</h3><p>With all of the animations we have available, it&rsquo;s going to quickly get very complicated to handle all of them in code. Think about how many statement&rsquo;s we&rsquo;d need to decide which animation to play at which time, depending on what the player is doing. While this is not bad if you only have a few animations, it quickly gets out of hand and becomes impractical.</p><p>Also, consider when the character is standing still: it should be playing the &ldquo;Idle&rdquo; animation. When the player presses &ldquo;forward&rdquo;, the character should move and switch to playing the &ldquo;Walking&rdquo; animation. This sudden transition is going to look jarring, so we&rsquo;d prefer if the two animations can be &ldquo;blended&rdquo; into a smoother transition.</p><p>The solution to these complex animation issues is to use the <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> node. This node is designed to control an <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> and has functionality to control how animations transition and blend together.</p><p>Add an <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> to the scene. In the <strong>Inspector</strong>, set <strong>Tree Root</strong> to a new , in <strong>Anim Player</strong> select the character&rsquo;s <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> node, and check the box next to <strong>Active</strong>.</p><p><a href=#image-da28652aa6fed7033eedef20b6bb26d6 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animtree_settings.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-da28652aa6fed7033eedef20b6bb26d6><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animtree_settings.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>You may notice that when the <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> is active, you can&rsquo;t choose animations in the <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i>. If you need to make any changes or test the animations, uncheck the tree&rsquo;s <strong>Active</strong> property while doing so.</p></div></div><h3 id=the-idlewalkrun-cycle>The Idle/Walk/Run Cycle</h3><p>There are a lot of animations provided with these models. For this example, we&rsquo;re going to focus on the idle-walk-run cycle, jumping, and attacking. If you want to include other animations, they&rsquo;ll be handled in a similar way.</p><p>In the <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i>, find the &ldquo;Idle&rdquo;, &ldquo;Running_A&rdquo;, &ldquo;Walking_Backwards&rdquo;, and &ldquo;Running_Strafe_Left&rdquo;/&ldquo;Running_Strafe_Right&rdquo; animations. Make sure they&rsquo;re all set to loop - you can test them by pressing the &ldquo;Play&rdquo; button: (▶). If any of them are not, reimport the character after setting them (see <a href=/4.x/3d/assets/importing_assets/>Importing Assets</a>).</p><p>Select the node and you&rsquo;ll see the panel open at the bottom of the window:</p><p><a href=#image-ae893e4d5ec7fea9904f70d36f2a955c class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animtree_empty.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-ae893e4d5ec7fea9904f70d36f2a955c><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animtree_empty.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>As an example, right-click in the empty space and choose <strong>Add Animation → Idle</strong>, then add the &ldquo;1H_Melee_Attack_Chop&rdquo; animation as well.</p><p>Select the <strong>Connect Nodes</strong> button and draw a connection from to . You should immediately see the &ldquo;Idle&rdquo; animation playing.</p><p>Now, we want to be able to transition from idle to attack and then back to idle when the attack animation finishes. Draw two more connection arrows to and from the attack. It won&rsquo;t quite work, however, you&rsquo;ll just be rapidly flickering between the two animations, because both are set to immediately transition.</p><p>To change the transition conditions, change to <strong>Select</strong> mode using the icon and then click on one of the connections. In the Inspector, you&rsquo;ll see the connection properties. For the connection from idle to attack, we want <strong>Advance/Mode</strong> to be &ldquo;Enabled&rdquo; (not &ldquo;Auto&rdquo;). This means it happens only when told to. Notice that the icon on the connection line changes color.</p><p>For the connection from attack to idle, set <strong>Switch Mode</strong> to &ldquo;At End&rdquo; and <strong>Advance Mode</strong> to &ldquo;Auto&rdquo;.</p><p>Now, when you press the ▶ button on the attack node, it will play and then transition back to idle as soon as it completes.</p><p>This gives you an idea how to set up different animations and transition between them. However, we want to do a little more here, so delete the two animations using the trash can icon, and let&rsquo;s set up a <em>blendspace</em>.</p><h3 id=blendspaces>Blendspaces</h3><p>Right-click in the empty space to create a new . Click on its name to rename it to (for idle-walk-run). Add a transition from so that the blendspace will start playing automatically.</p><p>Click the pencil icon to edit the blend space.</p><p><a href=#image-c5815fce7a3d13380a0bcb89af059067 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/blendspace_empty.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-c5815fce7a3d13380a0bcb89af059067><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/blendspace_empty.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>This 2D space represents the character&rsquo;s horizontal movement vector. When standing still that&rsquo;s , so click the <strong>Create Points</strong> button and click in the center of the grid to <strong>Add Animation → Idle</strong>.</p><p>At the center-top, add the &ldquo;Running_A&rdquo; animation, and center-bottom, &ldquo;Walking_Backwards&rdquo;. At the two horizontal ends, add the strafe animations.</p><p><a href=#image-2f6ba3fdb9877804fa1e26e93d2d1a8b class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/blendspace_complete.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-2f6ba3fdb9877804fa1e26e93d2d1a8b><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/blendspace_complete.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Now click the crosshair button to set the blend position and click to drag it around the grid. You should see the animations transition smoothly between the extremes.</p><p><video width=500 controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/blendtree_testing.webm></video></p><p>When you&rsquo;re done experimenting with the blendspace, click &ldquo;Root&rdquo; in the <strong>Path</strong> at the top of the panel to return to the root of the tree.</p><h3 id=setting-up-the-state-machine>Setting up the state machine</h3><p>The looping animations can be thought of as the &ldquo;heart&rdquo; of the animation tree. The character will spend most of its time playing these animations. Any other animations will branch off from it (like we did earlier with the attack).</p><p>In the image below, I&rsquo;ve done that with several other animations. Note the transition properties are set as we did in the example above.</p><p><a href=#image-38c36b04bf68a9f3bb22172eb194b265 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_tree_tree.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-38c36b04bf68a9f3bb22172eb194b265><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_tree_tree.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>You can also click to change the names the animations, as some of them are quite long.</p><p>The one animation that&rsquo;s different is jumping. The jump animation is split into three parts: &ldquo;start\"and &ldquo;land&rdquo;, which are played when the character starts jumping, and when the jump ends. The &ldquo;idle&rdquo; portion of the jump is a looping animation that plays as long as the character is in the air - if they fall a long way, for example.</p><p>Add the three jumping animations and link them like this:</p><p><a href=#image-89f5daed27fefcf343bdded1f414be04 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_tree_jumping.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-89f5daed27fefcf343bdded1f414be04><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_tree_jumping.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We need to be able to go straight from to in the event of falling off a ledge, but if pressing &ldquo;jump&rdquo;, we&rsquo;ll go through first.</p><p>In addition, we&rsquo;ve left the transition from to as &ldquo;Auto&rdquo;. Instead of changing it to &ldquo;Enabled&rdquo;, we&rsquo;ve added a <strong>Condition</strong> of to the transition:</p><p><a href=#image-545a1ff6a4677e17ddd503a8fa2ed004 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animtree_condition.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-545a1ff6a4677e17ddd503a8fa2ed004><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animtree_condition.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Similarly, the transition between and has a condition of .</p><p>We&rsquo;ll be able to set these conditions in code to trigger the transition.</p><p>Finally, if you&rsquo;re looking closely, you may notice that the transition from to does not look smooth, because the last frame and first frame of the two animations don&rsquo;t quite match up. We can solve this by selecting the transition between them and setting a small <strong>Xfade Time</strong> of , which will smooth it out nicely.</p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>We&rsquo;ve now set up our 3D character&rsquo;s animations and they&rsquo;re ready to use. By setting up the <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i>, it will now be much easier to select and transition between animations in the character&rsquo;s movement code.</p><p>See the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/assets/>section description</a> for more examples of working in 3D and for example Godot projects you can download.</p><h4 id=companion-video>Companion Video</h4><p><div style=position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden><iframe src=https://www.youtube.com/embed/YrNQCB34PAc style=position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;border:0 allowfullscreen title=\"YouTube Video\"></iframe></div><a href=https://youtu.be/YrNQCB34PAc target=_blank>https://youtu.be/YrNQCB34PAc</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

