#: 4.x/3d/healthbars/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=3d-unit-healthbars>3D Unit Healthbars</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want a floating &ldquo;healthbar&rdquo; for your 3D game objects (mobs, characters, etc.).</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>For this solution, we&rsquo;re going to re-use a 2D healthbar based on a <i class=gd-TextureProgressBar></i> node. It&rsquo;s already set up with textures and code for updating the value and color. If you already have something similar, feel free to use it here. In the example, we&rsquo;ll name this scene &ldquo;Healthbar2D&rdquo;.</p><p><a href=#image-c571f56a0b839bef182bac65a37db1e5 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/healthbar_example.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-c571f56a0b839bef182bac65a37db1e5><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/healthbar_example.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>If you need some assets, here are the three images used in the bar:</p><p><a href=#image-439bb7db3a52cb62d9001488d530d879 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/barHorizontal_green_mid%20200.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-439bb7db3a52cb62d9001488d530d879><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/barHorizontal_green_mid%20200.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p><a href=#image-2e5c6a9e9bbbc549e7811444f54d1e17 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/barHorizontal_yellow_mid%20200.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-2e5c6a9e9bbbc549e7811444f54d1e17><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/barHorizontal_yellow_mid%20200.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p><a href=#image-dc12827933757cf922a955813468500f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/barHorizontal_red_mid%20200.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-dc12827933757cf922a955813468500f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/barHorizontal_red_mid%20200.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>Re-using existing objects can save you a lot of time. Don&rsquo;t re-invent the wheel everytime you need a healthbar, camera, or other common object.</p></div></div><h3 id=project-setup>Project setup</h3><p>For our example &ldquo;mob&rdquo;, we&rsquo;ll start with a <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i> node. It&rsquo;s programmed to spawn and travel in a straight line. It also has the following code to handle damage:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-aba748f58c14b87472307b6bbf596a1c class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars01a.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-aba748f58c14b87472307b6bbf596a1c><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars01a.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Clicking on a unit deals one damage. Do ten damage, and the unit is destroyed. Now we need a visual representation of that using our 2D bar.</p><h3 id=2d-in-3d>2D in 3D</h3><p>We can display a 2D image in 3D using a <i class=gd-Sprite3D></i>. Add one to a new scene and name it &ldquo;Healthbar3D&rdquo;. First, we&rsquo;ll get it configured and sized, so set the <em>Texture</em> property to the green bar image.</p><p>The <i class=gd-Sprite3D></i> acts like any other 3D object - as we pan the camera around, our perspective on it changes. However, we want the healthbar to always &ldquo;face&rdquo; toward the camera so that we can see it.</p><p>In the Inspector, under <em>Flags</em>, set <em>Billboard</em> to &ldquo;Enabled&rdquo;.</p><p>Now try moving the camera to confirm that the texture is always facing you.</p><p><a href=#image-6b1484c3aeb47714fd0ec91e854fafcb class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars02.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6b1484c3aeb47714fd0ec91e854fafcb><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars02.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Add an instance of this scene to the scene and position the bar above the mob&rsquo;s body.</p><p><a href=#image-efb4075af8ea0428f54167b3c8a3bac0 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars04.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-efb4075af8ea0428f54167b3c8a3bac0><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars04.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=viewport-texture>Viewport texture</h3><p>We don&rsquo;t want the <i class=gd-Sprite3D></i> to show a static texture - we want it to display the 2D <i class=gd-TextureProgressBar></i>. We can do that using a <i class=gd-SubViewport></i> node, which can export a texture.</p><p>Add a <i class=gd-SubViewport></i> as a child of the <i class=gd-Sprite3D></i>. In the Inspector set <em>Transparent BG</em> to <strong>On</strong>.</p><p>We also need to set the size of the viewport to match the size of the healthbar texture, which is .</p><p>Instance the as a child of the <i class=gd-Viewport></i>. Your scene should look like this:</p><p><a href=#image-c78ef567e24b24cd91dcab2ce80deca8 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars_03a.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-c78ef567e24b24cd91dcab2ce80deca8><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars_03a.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>If the <i class=gd-SubViewport></i> were not a child of the <i class=gd-Sprite3D></i>, we could set it as the sprite&rsquo;s texture directly in the Inspector. Since it&rsquo;s a child, it won&rsquo;t be ready at the right time, so we&rsquo;ll need to set it in a script attached to the <i class=gd-Sprite3D></i>:</p><div class=highlight></div><h3 id=connecting-it-all-together>Connecting it all together</h3><p>In the mob&rsquo;s method, add the following after reducing the health:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Add the following to :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This calls the update method that already exists on the 2D bar, setting the progress bar&rsquo;s value and selecting the bar color:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Click on the mobs to see the health bars change.</p><p><a href=#image-e9310183c752b7faa74552f7fecfd8aa class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars_05a.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e9310183c752b7faa74552f7fecfd8aa><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_bars_05a.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h3><p>You can use this technique to display any other <i class=gd-Node2D></i> or <i class=gd-Control></i> nodes, such as <i class=gd-Label></i>, <i class=gd-VideoStreamPlayer></i>, etc. You can even use the <i class=gd-SubViewport></i> to &ldquo;project&rdquo; an entire 2D game in 3D space.</p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/3d_object_healthbars target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/3d_object_healthbars</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

