msgid "<h1 id=arcade-style-car>Arcade-style Car</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want to make an arcade-style car game, so you&rsquo;re looking for simplicity over realistic physics. In this recipe, you&rsquo;ll learn how to make a fun, driveable car using a rolling sphere.</p><p><video width=500 controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_01.webm></video></p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>There are a lot of ways to make a driving game. Different games need different levels of realism. If you&rsquo;re trying to make a light, arcade-style car, you don&rsquo;t need all of the features that Godot&rsquo;s <i class=gd-VehicleBody3D></i> node provides, such as supension, independently modeled wheels, etc.</p><p>Instead, we&rsquo;re going to use a single <i class=gd-RigidBody3D></i> sphere to handle the driving physics. The sphere will be invisible, and the car mesh will be placed at the sphere&rsquo;s location, making it look like it&rsquo;s the car that&rsquo;s driving.</p><p>As you can see in the preview clip above, the result looks remarkably good (and feels great to play!). Read on, and you&rsquo;ll see that the amount of code required is also surprisingly small.</p><h3 id=inputs>Inputs</h3><p>For control, we&rsquo;re going to add four inputs to the <em>Input Map</em>:</p><ul><li></li><li></li><li></li><li></li></ul><p>You can use keyboard input, game controller, or both. However, we recommend going with the analog stick for better steering.</p><h3 id=node-setup>Node setup</h3><p>The car is made with two main nodes: a <i class=gd-RigidBody3D></i> sphere for the physics, and a <i class=gd-MeshInstance3D></i> to display the car body. Here&rsquo;s the scene layout:</p><p>Here&rsquo;s how these nodes will interact: pressing &ldquo;accelerate&rdquo; will apply a force on the <i class=gd-RigidBody3D></i> in the direction the <i class=gd-MeshInstance3D></i> is facing, while the turning inputs will rotate the <i class=gd-MeshInstance3D></i>. As the ball rolls, it will carry the car mesh along with it (we&rsquo;ll ignore the ball&rsquo;s rotation).</p><h4 id=carmesh>CarMesh</h4><p>Here&rsquo;s the car model we&rsquo;ll use:</p><p><a href=#image-78f0d34d4c4db70feed3d954219a836f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-78f0d34d4c4db70feed3d954219a836f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>You can find this and other car models in Kenney&rsquo;s &ldquo;Car Kit&rdquo;, available here: <a href=https://kenney.nl/assets/car-kit target=_blank>https://kenney.nl/assets/car-kit</a>. Download the whole kit; you can use any of them that you choose. Note that this kit includes the models in multiple formats - you won&rsquo;t need all of them for your project. GLTF is the recommended format for use with Godot.</p></div></div><p>If you use the GLTF models, you shouldn&rsquo;t have adjust anything in the import settings.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s what the node tree looks like when importing the &ldquo;suv&rdquo; model:</p><p><a href=#image-b521066f5b16de295c7ff5092032a137 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_04a.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-b521066f5b16de295c7ff5092032a137><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_04a.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Note that the wheels & body are separate meshes. This will make it easy to add some visual appeal - like turning the wheels when steering.</p><h4 id=ball>Ball</h4><p>Add a sphere shape to the <i class=gd-CollisionShape3D></i>. We&rsquo;re using a radius of here, but you&rsquo;ll want to experiment with the size of the ball to get different driving behaviors.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s how to adjust the settings on the body:</p><ul><li><strong>Angular Damp: </strong> - this property will have a huge effect on the driving feel. A higher value will bring the car to a stop much faster.</li><li><strong>Gravity Scale: </strong> - Default gravity in Godot () feels a bit floaty, especially when going for an action feel. This will really matter if you plan to have jumps, hills, etc. in your world. You can set this globally in the <strong>Project Settings</strong> instead, if you prefer.</li><li><strong>Physics Material/Bounce: </strong> - Playing around with this value can be a lot of fun. Be careful going above 0.5, though!</li></ul><p>For the demo, we&rsquo;ve also added a spherical mesh to the collision shape for debugging purposes. You don&rsquo;t need this, but it helps when troubleshooting to have a visual of the ball rolling.</p><h4 id=raycast>RayCast</h4><p>Finally, add a <i class=gd-RayCast3D></i> node as a child of the <i class=gd-MeshInstance3D></i>. Set its <strong>Target Position</strong> to .</p><p><a href=#image-cee45a30124d8f27ef70a4d7d002de7c class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-cee45a30124d8f27ef70a4d7d002de7c><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We&rsquo;re going to use this for ground detection. When the car&rsquo;s in the air, steering and acceleration won&rsquo;t work. We can also use it to align the car mesh to a slope (if your game&rsquo;s track isn&rsquo;t flat).</p><p>Now we&rsquo;re ready to start coding.</p><h3 id=script>Script</h3><p>We&rsquo;ll begin the script with some node references we&rsquo;ll need:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Next, some variables configuring the car&rsquo;s behavior. See the comments describing each one&rsquo;s purpose.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>You can these if you&rsquo;d like to adjust them from the Inspector.</p><p>In we add a force to the body based on the direction the car is pointing, as well as keeping the car mesh positioned at the ball&rsquo;s position:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The next step is to get the inputs, but we&rsquo;ll also check if the ray is colliding with the ground first:</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>At this point, you can try it out. You should be able to accelerate forward and back (but not steer yet).</p></div></div><p>Next, still in the function, we&rsquo;ll rotate the car mesh based on the rotation input. We&rsquo;ll use (spherical linear interpolation) to do this smoothly:</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle warning\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-triangle\"></i> Warning</div><div class=box-content><p>Because of floating point imprecision, repeatedly rotating a transform will eventually cause it to become distorted. The scale can drift or the axes can become no-perpendicular. In any script where you&rsquo;re regularly rotating a transform, it&rsquo;s a good idea to use to correct any error before it accumulates.</p></div></div><p>You should try playing again at this point. You&rsquo;ll be able to control the car and drive around, and everything works pretty much as expected. However, there are a few more things to add that will improve the &ldquo;feel&rdquo; of the driving.</p><h3 id=final-touches>Final touches</h3><h4 id=1-align-with-slopes>1. Align with slopes</h4><p><strong>FIX THIS</strong></p><p>If you&rsquo;ve tried driving on a slope, you&rsquo;ve seen that the car mesh doesn&rsquo;t tilt at all, it always remains level. That looks unnatural, so let&rsquo;s use the process described in <a href=/godot_recipes/3.x/3d/3d_align_surface/>KinematicBody: Align with Surface</a> to fix that.</p><p>Add this code after rotating the mesh in :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>And the align function (notice how we&rsquo;re using again?):</p><div class=highlight></div><h4 id=2-turn-the-wheels>2. Turn the wheels</h4><p>It looks nice if the front wheels turn when you steer. Add some references to the front wheel meshes at the top of the script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>And right after getting input, add the following:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-39105fcf0778f60248139f78e6364ec8 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_05.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-39105fcf0778f60248139f78e6364ec8><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_05.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h4 id=3-tilt-the-body>3. Tilt the body</h4><p>This one adds lots of visual appeal. We&rsquo;re going to tilt the car&rsquo;s body based on the speed of the turn. Add a variable at the top of the script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The smaller this number, the more extreme the tilt effect will be. Between and works well for the SUV model.</p><p>Now add the following right after rotating the car mesh (in the statement):</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Observe the difference:</p><p><a href=#image-84e5c7a0c3b0b858d0847e51ffada6ab class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_06.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-84e5c7a0c3b0b858d0847e51ffada6ab><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_sphere_car_06.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=credits>Credits</h3><blockquote><p>The demo project seen here uses the following open-source/creative commons assets:</p><ul><li>Cars: <a href=https://kenney.nl/assets/car-kit target=_blank>Kenney Car Kit</a> by Kenney</li><li>Track: <a href=https://fertile-soil-productions.itch.io/modular-racekart-track-hilly-terrain-theme target=_blank>Modular Racekart Track</a> by Keith at Fertile Soil Productions</li></ul></blockquote><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/3d_car_sphere target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/3d_car_sphere</a></p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=http://kidscancode.org/godot_recipes/input/input_actions/ target=_blank>Input Actions</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
