msgid "<h1 id=mouse-input>Mouse Input</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want to detect mouse input.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p> is the base class for mouse events. It contains and properties. Inheriting from it are two classes: and .</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>You can assign mouse button events in the InputMap, so you can use them with .</p></div></div><h3 id=inputeventmousebutton></h3><p> contains a list of constants for each possible button, which will be reported in the event’s property. Note that the scrollwheel also counts as a button - two buttons, to be precise, with both and being separate events.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>Unlike regular buttons, mouse wheel clicks only produce events. There is no concept of a mouse wheel click being &ldquo;released&rdquo;.</p></div></div><div class=highlight></div><h3 id=inputeventmousemotion></h3><p>These events occur whenever the mouse moves. You can find the distance moved (in screen coordinates) with the property.</p><p>Here’s an example using mouse movement to rotate a 3D character:</p><div class=highlight></div><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

