#: 4.x/animation/using_animation_sm/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=using-the-animationtree-statemachine>Using the AnimationTree StateMachine</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>A common situation: you have a large number of animations, and it&rsquo;s becoming difficult to manage transitions between them. Your code has become full of statements, and every time you change something, it all breaks.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Use an <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> to create an animation <em>state machine</em>. This will allow us to organize our animations and most importantly, control the transitions between them.</p><h3 id=getting-started>Getting started</h3><p>For this demo, we&rsquo;ll be using the excellent &ldquo;Adventurer&rdquo; sprite by Elthen. You can get this and lots of other great art at <a href=https://elthen.itch.io/ target=_blank>https://elthen.itch.io/</a>.</p><p><a href=#image-a5c3158fd1f50cd70ccb6d6256e075fb class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/adventurer_sprite_sheet_v1.1.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-a5c3158fd1f50cd70ccb6d6256e075fb><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/adventurer_sprite_sheet_v1.1.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We&rsquo;ll also assume you&rsquo;ve already set up the character&rsquo;s animations using <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i>. Using the above spritesheet, we have the following animations: &ldquo;idle&rdquo;, &ldquo;run&rdquo;, &ldquo;attack1&rdquo;, &ldquo;attack2&rdquo;, &ldquo;hurt&rdquo;, and &ldquo;die&rdquo;.</p><h3 id=animationtree>AnimationTree</h3><p>Add an <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> node to the scene. In its <em>Tree Root</em> property, choose &ldquo;New AnimationNodeStateMachine&rdquo;.</p><p><a href=#image-70b06ca2fc1ae67189e7118541e52861 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animation_tree_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-70b06ca2fc1ae67189e7118541e52861><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animation_tree_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>An <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> is a node that controls animations created in <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i>. To let it access the existing animations, click &ldquo;Assign&rdquo; in the <em>Anim Player</em> property and select your animation node.</p><p>Now we can begin to set up our state machine in the <i class=gd-AnimationTree></i> panel:</p><p><a href=#image-d575258cde3099edabb4c9c63b4df444 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_tree_panel.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-d575258cde3099edabb4c9c63b4df444><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_tree_panel.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Note the warning. Set the <em>Active</em> property to &ldquo;On&rdquo; in the Inspector.</p><p>Right-click and choose &ldquo;Add Animation&rdquo;. Choose &ldquo;idle&rdquo;, and you&rsquo;ll see a small box representing that animation. Press its &ldquo;Play&rdquo; button and you should see the animation play. Do the same to add boxes for the other animations.</p><p>Now we can add connections. Click the &ldquo;Connect nodes&rdquo; button and drag between nodes to connect them. As an example, let&rsquo;s use the two attack animations:</p><p><a href=#image-0360f2d7db9a235cbc40cd8f9d3e5b3f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animation_tree_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-0360f2d7db9a235cbc40cd8f9d3e5b3f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animation_tree_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>When you select an animation, the tree will follow the connected path from the current node to the destination. However, in the configuration above, if you play &ldquo;attack2&rdquo; you won&rsquo;t see &ldquo;attack1&rdquo; along the way. That&rsquo;s because the default &ldquo;switch mode&rdquo; for a connection is &ldquo;Immediate&rdquo;. Click the &ldquo;Move/select&rdquo; button and then click on the connection between &ldquo;attack1&rdquo; and &ldquo;attack2&rdquo;. In the Inspector, change <strong>Switch Mode</strong> to &ldquo;At End&rdquo;. Do the same with &ldquo;attack2&rdquo; to &ldquo;idle&rdquo;. The connection icon changes from <i class=\"fas fa-play\"></i> to <i class=\"fas fa-step-forward\"></i>.</p><p>Now, with &ldquo;idle&rdquo; playing, if you click &ldquo;attack2&rdquo;, you&rsquo;ll see the two attacks play in sequence.</p><p>But now the animation stops on &ldquo;attack2&rdquo;. On its connection, set the <strong>Advance/Mode</strong> property to &ldquo;Auto&rdquo;. This will make the tree go back to &ldquo;idle&rdquo; after playing both animations. Note that the connection icon turns green to show this.</p><p><a href=#image-4566e7df98d050fd5c791f2fc16a71cc class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animation_tree_05.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-4566e7df98d050fd5c791f2fc16a71cc><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animation_tree_05.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Now the animations are played in sequence whenever they&rsquo;re triggered.</p><h3 id=calling-states-in-code>Calling states in code</h3><p>Here is the full tree for all of the animations:</p><p><a href=#image-11a63ec5fe7f3e09d1cbc6c5b3b99939 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_sm_final.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-11a63ec5fe7f3e09d1cbc6c5b3b99939><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/anim_sm_final.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Now let&rsquo;s set up the character to use these animations in a script.</p><div class=highlight></div><p> holds a reference to the state machine, which is an . To call a specific animation, you use , which will follow the connections to the given animation.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Here we have examples of functions we would call if the player is hurt or killed. For the other animations (running, attacking, etc.), we&rsquo;ll need to combine them with our input and movement code. determines whether we should be showing &ldquo;run&rdquo; or &ldquo;idle&rdquo;.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Note that we&rsquo;re using after traveling to the attack animations. This is so that we won&rsquo;t instead travel to the &ldquo;run&rdquo; or &ldquo;idle&rdquo; animations further down in the function.</p><p><a href=#image-de3a93304f0aa4574aae7943759d4f1c class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animation_tree_07.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-de3a93304f0aa4574aae7943759d4f1c><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/animation_tree_07.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>You can use the AnimationTreeStateMachine to handle</p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/ai_behavior_demos target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/ai_behavior_demos</a></p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/animation/spritesheet_animation/>Spritesheet animation</a></li><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/topdown_movement/#option-1-8-way-movement>Top-down character</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

