#: 4.x/animation/spritesheet_animation/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=spritesheet-animation>Spritesheet animation</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want to use a spritesheet containing 2D animations.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Spritesheets are a common way for 2D animations to be distributed. In a spritesheet, all of the animation frames are packed into a single image.</p><p>For this demo, we&rsquo;ll be using the excellent &ldquo;Adventurer&rdquo; sprite by Elthen. You can get this and lots of other great art at<a href=https://elthen.itch.io/ target=_blank>https://elthen.itch.io/</a>.</p><p><a href=#image-6b8ac798089deef55ab7664731b94ff7 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/adventurer_sprite_sheet_v1.1.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6b8ac798089deef55ab7664731b94ff7><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/adventurer_sprite_sheet_v1.1.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle warning\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-triangle\"></i> Warning</div><div class=box-content><p>Make sure the images in your spritesheet are laid out in a constant-sized grid. This will enable Godot to automatically slice them. If they&rsquo;re packed irregularly, you will not be able to use the following technique.</p></div></div><h3 id=node-setup>Node setup</h3><p>This animation technique uses a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> node to display the texture, and then we animate the changing frames with <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i>. This can work with any 2D node, but for this demo, we&rsquo;ll use a <i class=gd-CharacterBody2D></i>.</p><p>Add the following nodes to your scene:</p><p>Drag the spritesheet texture into the <em>Texture</em> property of the <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i>. You&rsquo;ll see the entire spritesheet displayed in the viewport. To slice it up into individual frames, expand the &ldquo;Animation&rdquo; section in the Inspector and set the <em>Hframes</em> to and <em>Vframes</em> to . <em>Hframes</em> and <em>Vframes</em> are the number of horizontal and vertical frames in your spritesheet.</p><p><a href=#image-60e7ba018881d86d74980212741d92f4 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/sprite_animation_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-60e7ba018881d86d74980212741d92f4><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/sprite_animation_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Try changing the <em>Frame</em> property to see the image change. This is the property we’ll be animating.</p><h3 id=adding-animations>Adding animations</h3><p>Select the <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> and click the “Animation” button followed by “New\" . Name the new animation “idle”. Set the animation length to and click the “Loop” button so that our animation will repeat (see below).</p><p>With the scrubber at time , select the <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> node. Set its <em>Animation/Frame</em> to , then click the key icon next to the value.</p><p><a href=#image-0bd455c1701e10fed4b1f14a940076fe class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/sprite_animation_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-0bd455c1701e10fed4b1f14a940076fe><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/sprite_animation_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>If you try playing the animation, you&rsquo;ll see it doesn&rsquo;t appear to do anything. That&rsquo;s because the last frame (12) looks the same as the first (0), but we&rsquo;re not seeing any of the frames in-between (1-11). To fix this, change the &ldquo;Update Mode&rdquo; of the track from its default value of &ldquo;Discrete&rdquo; to &ldquo;Continuous&rdquo;. You can find this button at the end of the track on the right side.</p><p><a href=#image-f48b9fae7303435c1c4a88eea7f19baf class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/sprite_animation_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-f48b9fae7303435c1c4a88eea7f19baf><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/sprite_animation_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Note that this will only work for spritesheets where the frames are already in order. If they are not, you&rsquo;ll have to keyframe each <em>Frame</em> seperately along the timeline.</p><p><a href=#image-a070b5acc769cc972649c953f4845357 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/sprite_animation_04.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-a070b5acc769cc972649c953f4845357><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/sprite_animation_04.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Feel free to add the other animations yourself. For example, the &ldquo;jump&rdquo; animation is on frames through .</p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=http://kidscancode.org/godot_recipes/2d/platform_character/ target=_blank>Platform character</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

