#: 4.x/g101/3d/101_3d_04/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=using-areas>Using Areas</h1><p>In the last part, we learned about transforms, a very important concept in 3D that records an object&rsquo;s local orientation in space, and used it to move our KinematicBody character. This time, we&rsquo;re going to look at another type of 3D node: the <i class=gd-Area3D></i>.</p><h2 id=areas>Areas</h2><p><i class=gd-Area3D></i> nodes are used for <em>detection</em> and <em>influence</em>. They can detect when objects overlap, and when an object enters or exits a region. An <i class=gd-Area3D></i> can also be used to override physics properties, such as gravity or damping, in a region.</p><p>We&rsquo;re going to use <i class=gd-Area3D></i> to create three types of objects:</p><ul><li>A coin the character can pick up</li><li>A bullet the character can shoot</li><li>Spikes that damage the character</li></ul><p>To begin, let&rsquo;s set up some collision layers so that we can more easily control what objects the areas can interact with. Open the Project Settings and find the &ldquo;Layer Names/3D Physics&rdquo; section. Name the first three layers &ldquo;environment&rdquo;, &ldquo;player&rdquo;, and &ldquo;pickups&rdquo;.</p><p><a href=#image-3ba15b3d857de2424c808b15bd5350ab class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-3ba15b3d857de2424c808b15bd5350ab><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=coin>Coin</h3><p>Create a new inherited scene from the object in the Kenney Platformer Kit (see <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/g101/3d/101_3d_02/>Part 2</a>). Right-click on the root node, choose &ldquo;Change Type&rdquo;, and pick <i class=gd-Area3D></i>. In the Inspector, set the collision mask and layer so that the coin is in &ldquo;pickups&rdquo; and scans &ldquo;player&rdquo;.</p><p><a href=#image-514353ae6ca3abffe4eb36cef6fddbd9 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-514353ae6ca3abffe4eb36cef6fddbd9><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Now we need a collision shape, so add a node and choose a for it. If you rotate it 90 degrees around <strong>z</strong>, it will fit the coin shape pretty well.</p><p><a href=#image-f457ed016ccdb3abf4f7d0fb25350669 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-f457ed016ccdb3abf4f7d0fb25350669><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Add a script to the Area and connect its signal, which we&rsquo;ll use to remove the coin:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Add a few coins to your scene and see if you can pick them up:</p><p><a href=#image-a57e84c0169dfddc5dd25cfe3cbfda4f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_04.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-a57e84c0169dfddc5dd25cfe3cbfda4f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_04.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=bullets>Bullets</h3><p>Make a new scene with an Area root, and name it &ldquo;Bullet&rdquo;. Add a , a , and a . I&rsquo;m using a sphere shape for the mesh and collision, scaled to a radius of so that it won&rsquo;t be too big compared with the character. The is to give the bullet a time limit. Set its <em>One Shot</em> and <em>Autostart</em> properties both on.</p><p>Add a script and connect the &rsquo;s signal and the <i class=gd-Area3D></i>&rsquo;s signal. Then add the following:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Since we want the bullet to start at a certain location and travel directly ahead of the player, we&rsquo;ll pass the player&rsquo;s transform to and use it to orient the bullet and make it move forward (remember in Godot&rsquo;s orientation, &ldquo;forward&rdquo; is along the negative <strong>z</strong> axis).</p><p>Now we can go to the character scene and add a node, placing it at the tip of the character&rsquo;s &ldquo;arrow&rdquo; mesh. Name this node &ldquo;Muzzle&rdquo;. Next, add the following code to the character&rsquo;s script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-da7337169f9cc7e5510859acb4cf72fe class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_05.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-da7337169f9cc7e5510859acb4cf72fe><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_05.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=spikes>Spikes</h3><p>Finally, we&rsquo;re going to add some danger to the environment. Make in instance of the object and make it an Area like we did with the coin. Add a script and connect the signal, but this time, we want the object to do something to the character:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now we need to define how the character reacts to taking &ldquo;damage&rdquo;. Since we haven&rsquo;t really built a health system, we&rsquo;re just going to &ldquo;bounce&rdquo; the character as if it got hurt. We need to add the following to the character script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The purpose of this code is to disable input temporarily so the player will fly backwards from the spikes. If we just tried to set the velocity, it would immediately be overridden by the velocity set by the key inputs. So add this at the beginning of :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Add a spike instance to your level and try it out:</p><p><a href=#image-6aeb4e9e148ef53f7458a41050b0b876 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_06.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6aeb4e9e148ef53f7458a41050b0b876><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_04_06.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping Up</h2><p>In this tutorial you learned how to use <i class=gd-Area3D></i> nodes to detect and react to 3D bodies.</p><p>In the next part: a collection of small improvements.</p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

