#: 4.x/g101/3d/101_3d_03/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=creating-a-3d-character>Creating a 3D Character</h1><p>In the last part, we covered how to import 3D objects and how to arrange them in a scene. In this installment, we&rsquo;ll add more objects to the scene, including a user-controlled character.</p><h2 id=building-the-scene>Building the Scene</h2><p>We&rsquo;re going to continue using the Kenney Platformer Kit we downloaded in <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/g101/3d/101_3d_02/>Part 2</a>. Select all the files and in the <strong>Import</strong> tab set their <strong>Root Type</strong> to <i class=gd-StaticBody3D></i>. Uncheck the <strong>Root Name</strong> property and click <strong>Reimport</strong>. Select and make a new inherited scene. Use the <strong>Create Single Convex Collision Sibling</strong> option on the mesh using the menu as you did in the last tutorial. Now you can save the scene - I recommend making a separate folder for this, as soon you&rsquo;re going to have a bunch of scenes representing the differently shaped platform parts.</p><p>Open the scene from the previous step with the &ldquo;Ground&rdquo; plane and the crates. Delete the crates and add an instance of the large block. We want to be able to place these blocks so that they line up. To do this, select &ldquo;Configure Snap&rdquo; from the &ldquo;Transform&rdquo; menu at the top of the Viewport and set <em>Translate Snap</em> to . Then click on the &ldquo;Snap Mode&rdquo; button (or press the <strong></strong> key). Now duplicate the block a few times and drag them to arrange.</p><p>If you like, go ahead and add scenes for some of the other platform blocks and arrange them into a pleasing level. Be creative!</p><p><a href=#image-2ca3efef623705c414042d0df2cb1782 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_scene_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-2ca3efef623705c414042d0df2cb1782><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_scene_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=adding-a-character>Adding a Character</h2><p>Now we&rsquo;re going to make a character so we can walk around on the platforms. Open a new scene and start with a <i class=gd-CharacterBody3D></i> named &ldquo;Character&rdquo;. This node behaves very much like its 2D equivalent (you&rsquo;ve already done the 2D tutorials, right?). It has a method that we&rsquo;ll use to perform the movement and collision detection.</p><p>Add a capsule-shaped <i class=gd-MeshInstance3D></i> and a matching <i class=gd-CollionShape3D></i>. Remember, you can add a to the mesh and set its <strong>Albedo/Color</strong> property to change the color.</p><p>The capsule is nice, but it&rsquo;s going to be hard to tell what direction it&rsquo;s facing. Let&rsquo;s add another mesh, this time with a shape. Set its <strong>Top Radius</strong> to , its <strong>Bottom Radius</strong> to and its <strong>Height</strong> to 0.5, then its <strong>x</strong> rotation to degrees. Now you have a nice cone shape. Arrange it so it&rsquo;s pointing out from the body along the <em>negative</em> <strong>z</strong> axis. (You can tell which way is negative because the gizmo arrows point in the positive direction).</p><p><a href=#image-6517754892c7f6535d460ebb983e528a class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_character_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6517754892c7f6535d460ebb983e528a><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_character_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>In this picture, we&rsquo;ve also added two sphere meshes for eyes to give a little more character. Feel free to add whatever details you like.</p><p>Let&rsquo;s also add a <i class=gd-Camera3D></i> to the scene, so it will follow the player around. Position the camera behind and above the character, angling it down a bit. Click the &ldquo;Preview&rdquo; button to check the camera&rsquo;s view.</p><p>Before we add a script, open the &ldquo;Project Settings&rdquo; and add the following inputs on the &ldquo;Input Map&rdquo; tab:</p><table><thead><tr><th style=text-align:left>Input Action</th><th style=text-align:left>Key</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=text-align:left>move_forward</td><td style=text-align:left><strong>W</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=text-align:left>move_back</td><td style=text-align:left><strong>S</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=text-align:left>strafe_right</td><td style=text-align:left><strong>D</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=text-align:left>strafe_left</td><td style=text-align:left><strong>A</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=text-align:left>jump</td><td style=text-align:left><strong>Space</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Now let&rsquo;s add a script to the body.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The code in is pretty straightforward: add gravity to accelerate in the positive <strong>Y</strong> direction (downward), call to check for input, and then use to move in the direction of the velocity vector.</p><p>In we check to see which key is pressed and then move in that direction. Run the program and test:</p><p><a href=#image-48a72c9798affc0e356c576ffc33c7c7 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_move_01.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-48a72c9798affc0e356c576ffc33c7c7><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_move_01.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>This is all good, but we need to be able to rotate using the mouse. Add the following code to the character&rsquo;s script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This will convert any mouse motion in the x direction into a rotation around the <strong>y</strong> axis.</p><p>Run the scene and confirm that moving the mouse rotates the character:</p><p><a href=#image-9d0a5d4a2a02fd2093fb0fa63c18d4b9 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_move_02.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-9d0a5d4a2a02fd2093fb0fa63c18d4b9><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_move_02.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>However, there&rsquo;s a problem. No matter which way we&rsquo;re facing, pressing <strong>W</strong> moves us along the <strong>Z</strong> axis of the world. Our movement is using global coordinates, but we need to move in the <em>object&rsquo;s</em> forward direction.</p><h3 id=the-power-of-transforms>The Power of Transforms</h3><p>This is where <em>transforms</em> come in. A transform is a mathematical <em>matrix</em> that contains the object&rsquo;s translation, rotation, and scale information all in one. In Godot it&rsquo;s stored in the data type. The position information is called the and the orientation information is in the .</p><p>Remember how the 3D gizmo can be set to &ldquo;Local Space Mode&rdquo;? When in this mode, the gizmo&rsquo;s <strong>X/Y/Z</strong> axes point along the <em>object&rsquo;s</em> axes. This is the same as the of the transform. The basis contains three objects called , , and that represent these directions. We can use this to ensure that pressing the <strong>W</strong> key will always move us in the object&rsquo;s forward direction.</p><p>Change the function like so:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>By multiplying the input vector by the , we <em>apply</em> that transformation to the vector. Since the basis represents the object&rsquo;s rotation, we&rsquo;ve now converted forward and back to point along the object&rsquo;s <strong>Z</strong> axis, and the strafe keys along its <strong>X</strong>.</p><p><a href=#image-825c98413c8d2795a7ae90679a1e4a2a class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_move_03.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-825c98413c8d2795a7ae90679a1e4a2a><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_move_03.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=jumping>Jumping</h3><p>Let&rsquo;s add one more movement to the player: jumping.</p><p>Add these lines to the end of :</p><div class=highlight></div><h3 id=improving-the-camera>Improving the camera</h3><p>You may have noticed that the if the character stands near an obstacle, the camera can &ldquo;clip&rdquo; inside the object, which doesn&rsquo;t look nice. While coding a good 3D camera can be a complex topic on its own, we can use a built-in Godot node to get a pretty good solution.</p><p>Delete the <i class=gd-Camera3D></i> from the character scene and add a <i class=gd-SpringArm3D></i>. This node can act as a moving arm that holds the camera while detecting collisions. It will move the camera closer if there&rsquo;s an obstacle.</p><p>In its properties, set <strong>Spring Length</strong> to , and set its <strong>Position</strong> to , which is at the character&rsquo;s head. Note the yellow line indicating the <strong>Spring Length</strong>. The camera will move along this line - at its end whenever possible, but moving closer if an obstacle is there.</p><p>Add back a <i class=gd-Camera3D></i> as a child of the <i class=gd-SpringArm3D></i>, and try running the game again. You can experiment with rotating the spring arm (around its <strong>x</strong> axis to point down slightly, for example) until you find something you like.</p><h3 id=what-about-first-person>What about first person?</h3><p>If you&rsquo;re curious how you would do this in first person, see the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/basic_fps/index.html>Basic FPS Character</a> recipe. You&rsquo;ll notice several similarities with the 3rd person script we wrote above.</p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping Up</h2><p>In this tutorial you learned how to build a more complex scene, and how to write movement code for a user-controlled character. You also learned about transforms, which are a very important concept in 3D - you&rsquo;re going to be using a lot in the future.</p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

