#: 4.x/g101/gdscript/gdscript_01/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=getting-started>Getting started</h1><h2 id=overview>Overview</h2><p>Writing scripts and attaching them to nodes and other objects is how you build behavior and game mechanics into your game. For example, a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> node automatically displays an image, but to move it across the screen, you&rsquo;ll add a script that tells it how fast, in what direction, and so on.</p><p>You can think of it as the coding version of using the Inspector - GDScript knows all about Godot nodes and how to access them, plus it allows you to change them dynamically.</p><p>GDScript is Godot&rsquo;s built-in language for scripting and interacting with nodes. The <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/gdscript/gdscript_basics.html target=_blank>GDScript documentation</a> on the Godot website is a great place to get an overview of the language, and I highly recommend taking the time to read through it.</p><p><strong>Is GDScript Python?</strong></p><p>You&rsquo;ll often read comments to the effect that &ldquo;GDScript is based on Python&rdquo;. That&rsquo;s somewhat misleading; GDScript uses a <em>syntax</em> that&rsquo;s modeled on Python&rsquo;s, but it&rsquo;s a distinct language that&rsquo;s optimized for and integrated into the Godot engine. That said, if you already know some Python, you&rsquo;ll find GDScript looks very familiar.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle warning\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-triangle\"></i> Warning</div><div class=box-content><p>Many tutorials (and Godot in general) assume that you have at least <em>some</em> programming experience already. If you&rsquo;ve never coded before, you&rsquo;ll likely find learning Godot to be a challenge. Learning a game engine is a large task on its own; learning to code at the same time means you&rsquo;re taking on a lot. If you find yourself struggling with the code in this section, you may find that working through an introductory programming lesson (Python is a good option) will help you grasp the basics.</p></div></div><h2 id=structure-of-a-script>Structure of a script</h2><p>The first line of any GDScript file must be , where is some existing built-in or user-defined class. For example, if you&rsquo;re attaching a script to a <i class=gd-CharacterBody2D></i> node, then your script would start with . This states that your script is taking all the functionality of the built-in object and <em>extending</em> it with additional functionality created by you.</p><p>In the rest of the script, you can define any number of variables (aka &ldquo;class properties&rdquo;) and functions (aka &ldquo;class methods&rdquo;).</p><h2 id=creating-a-script>Creating a script</h2><p>Let&rsquo;s make our first script. Remember, any node can have a script attached to it.</p><p>Open the editor and add a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> node to empty scene. Right-click on the new node, and choose &ldquo;Attach Script&rdquo;. You can also click the button next to the search box.</p><p><a href=#image-683888049a500847388aa321a96501d6 class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_attach.png?width=250\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:250 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-683888049a500847388aa321a96501d6><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_attach.png?width=250\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Next you need to decide where you want the script saved and what to call it. If you&rsquo;ve named the node, the script will automatically be named to match it (so unless you&rsquo;ve changed anything this script will likely be called &ldquo;sprite2d.gd&rdquo;).</p><p>Now the script editor window opens up, and this is your new, empty sprite script. Godot has automatically included some lines of code, as well as some comments describing what they do.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Since the script was added to a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i>, the first line is automatically set to . Because this script extends the <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> class, it will be able to access and manipulate all the properties and methods that a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> node provides.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Properties and methods</div><div class=box-content><p><em>Properties</em> and <em>methods</em> are two terms which specifically mean <em>variables</em> and <em>functions</em> that are defined in an object. Programmers tend to use the terms interchangeably.</p></div></div><p>After that is where you&rsquo;re going to define all the variables you will use in the script, the &ldquo;member variables&rdquo;. You define variables with the &lsquo;var&rsquo; keyword - as you can see by the comment examples.</p><p>Go ahead and delete the comments and let&rsquo;s talk about this next piece.</p><p>Now we see a function called . In GDScript you define a function with the keyword &ldquo;func&rdquo;. The function is a special one that Godot looks for and runs whenever a node is added to the tree, for example when we hit &ldquo;Play&rdquo;.</p><p>Let&rsquo;s say that when the game starts, we want to make sure the sprite goes to a particular location. In the Inspector, we want to set the <strong>Position</strong> property. Notice that it&rsquo;s in the section called &ldquo;Node2D&rdquo; - that means this is a property that <em>any</em> <i class=gd-Node2D></i> type node will have, not just <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i>s.</p><p>How do we set the property in code? One way to find the name of the property is by hovering over it in the Inspector.</p><p><a href=#image-12c2fc03a22d023a07776a82a50c1be0 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-12c2fc03a22d023a07776a82a50c1be0><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Godot has a great built-in help/reference tool. Click on &ldquo;Classes&rdquo; at the top of the Script window and search for Node2D and you&rsquo;ll see a help page showing you all the properties and methods the class has available. Looking down a bit you can see in the &ldquo;Member Variables&rdquo; section - that&rsquo;s the one we want. It also tells us the property is of the type &ldquo;Vector2&rdquo;.</p><p><a href=#image-9db36db66c9e6db9123e634c46669768 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-9db36db66c9e6db9123e634c46669768><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Let&rsquo;s go back to the script and use that property:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Notice how the editor is making suggestions as you type. Godot uses vectors for lots of things, and we&rsquo;ll talk more about them later. For now, let&rsquo;s type Vector2, and the hint tells us to put two floats for and .</p><p>Now we have a script that says &ldquo;When this sprite starts, set its position to &rdquo;. We can try this out by pressing the &ldquo;Play Scene&rdquo; button.</p><p><a href=#image-74db3adf9de269be22a26ae17fd9de94 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-74db3adf9de269be22a26ae17fd9de94><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Learning tip</div><div class=box-content><p>When first learning to code, beginners often ask &ldquo;How do you memorize all these commands?&rdquo; Just like any other skill, it&rsquo;s not a matter of memorization, it&rsquo;s about practice. As you use things more, the things you do frequently will &ldquo;stick&rdquo; and become automatic. Until then, it&rsquo;s a great idea to keep the reference docs handy. Use the search function whenever you see something you don&rsquo;t recognize. If you have multiple monitors, keep a copy of the <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/ target=_blank>web docs</a> open on the side for quick reference.</p></div></div><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>Congratulations on making your first script in GDScript! Before moving on, make sure you understand everything we did in this step. In the next part, we&rsquo;ll add some more code to move the sprite around the screen.</p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

