#: 4.x/g101/gdscript/gdscript_02/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=moving-sprites>Moving sprites</h1><p>In the previous step, we added a script to a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> and used that script to set its position.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s the script we have so far:</p><div class=highlight></div><h2 id=moving-the-sprite>Moving the sprite</h2><p>Now we want to make the sprite move around the screen. Godot runs at <em>60 fps</em> (frames per second) by default, and we&rsquo;d like to the sprite to move a little bit each frame. Add this code to the script and run it to see what happens.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Did you see the sprite moving to the right? Let&rsquo;s break down what&rsquo;s happening.</p><p>Just as the function was called when the node was first started (by pressing &ldquo;Play&rdquo;), the function is called every frame. This means any code you write in this function will be executed every of a second. This is what the represents - the length of the frame in seconds.</p><p>What are we asking Godot to do every frame here? Take the sprite&rsquo;s coordinate and add to it.</p><p>The result is that we have a sprite that&rsquo;s moving pixels per second to the right.</p><div class=expand><input type=checkbox id=expand-1 aria-controls=expandcontent-1> <label class=expand-label for=expand-1><i class=\"fas fa-chevron-down\"></i> <i class=\"fas fa-chevron-right\"></i> More info</label><div id=expandcontent-1 class=expand-content><p>For more information about and why we use it in game development, see <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/basics/understanding_delta/>Understanding delta</a>.</p></div></div><h2 id=using-vectors>Using vectors</h2><p>What if we want to move the sprite diagonally? That means we need to change the and coordinates at the same time. is a <strong>vector</strong> - it contains both coordinates in one quantity. Vectors can be added, so let&rsquo;s change the code to this:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Run the code and you should see the sprite moving down and to the right.</p><p>Let&rsquo;s make this a little easier to deal with by using <strong>variables</strong>:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ve taken this quantity and made it a variable. You <strong>declare</strong> a variable by using the keyword , giving the variable a name, and then assigning a value with .</p><p>It&rsquo;s often more convenient to declare variables at the top where they&rsquo;re easy to find. We&rsquo;ll also find it more convenient later, when we start making multiples of this sprite.</p><h2 id=randomizing-the-movement>Randomizing the movement</h2><p>Let&rsquo;s mix this up and choose a random direction for the sprite to move in.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>That&rsquo;s a lot, so let&rsquo;s break it down:</p><ol><li><p>First, we&rsquo;re setting to point to the right. is a built-in <em>constant</em> that represents the vector .</p></li><li><p>Next, we&rsquo;re using &rsquo;s method to rotate the vector. In the parentheses of it expects an angle.</p></li><li><p>Since we want to rotate a random amount, we use to get a random number between and .</p><div class=expand><input type=checkbox id=expand-2 aria-controls=expandcontent-2> <label class=expand-label for=expand-2><i class=\"fas fa-chevron-down\"></i> <i class=\"fas fa-chevron-right\"></i> About angles</label><div id=expandcontent-2 class=expand-content></div></div></li><li><p>Finally, we multiply by another random number to give it a random speed.</p></li></ol><p>Try running the scene a couple of times and you&rsquo;ll see the sprite move in different directions.</p><div class=highlight></div><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

