#: 4.x/math/dot_cross_product/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=vectors-using-dot-and-cross-product>Vectors: Using Dot and Cross Product</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You&rsquo;d like to understand what is meant by <em>dot product</em> and <em>cross product</em>.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>In this recipe we&rsquo;ll introduce the concept of vector <em>dot product</em> and <em>cross product</em> and how they might be used.</p><h3 id=dot-product>Dot product</h3><p>Dot product is an operation on two vectors that returns a scalar. It is often visualized as the <em>projection</em> of vector A onto vector B:</p><p><a href=#image-c9d628e5697d5d98220ef66110ce42ea class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_04.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-c9d628e5697d5d98220ef66110ce42ea><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_04.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>This is the formula for calculating the dot product:</p><p><a href=#image-83a0324ceab3d84498157b233492d1e0 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-83a0324ceab3d84498157b233492d1e0><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Where is the angle between the two vectors and is the magnitude of .</p><p>This is very useful when both vectors are normalized (i.e. their magnitudes are ), then the formula simplifies to:</p><p><a href=#image-47d4c5c5ee0d21cb8742dd4cb6eb55f1 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-47d4c5c5ee0d21cb8742dd4cb6eb55f1><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>This shows that the dot product is directly related to the angle between the two vectors. Since and , the result of the dot product can tell you how closely aligned two vectors are:</p><p><a href=#image-50d7af6c8a3b2ae6eff68f848df885d2 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_05.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-50d7af6c8a3b2ae6eff68f848df885d2><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_05.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>See below for how we can apply this fact in a practical example.</p><h3 id=cross-product>Cross product</h3><p>The cross product of two vectors is a third vector that is perpendicular to both of them. Its magnitude is related to their magnitudes and the angle between them.</p><p><a href=#image-a3aeef0edddfb2a9305d5725c2c1396d class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_06.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-a3aeef0edddfb2a9305d5725c2c1396d><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_06.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Once again, if we&rsquo;re using normalized vectors, the result is simplified: it will be directly related to the angle and its magnitude will range from -1 to 1.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>Since the cross product is perpendicular to both vectors, we would need to be working in 3D. In most 2D frameworks, including Godot, the 2D method returns a scalar value representing the result&rsquo;s magnitude.</p></div></div><h3 id=practical-applications>Practical applications</h3><p>Consider this animation, showing how the results of and change in relation to the changing angle:</p><p><a href=#image-1d6c14e0338f3be9504fb5d688a1e61d class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_01.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-1d6c14e0338f3be9504fb5d688a1e61d><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/dot_cross_01.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>This demonstrates two common applications of these methods. If the red vector is our object&rsquo;s forward direction, and the green shows the direction towards another object:</p><ul><li>Dot product: Using the result, we can tell if the object is in front of () or behind () us.</li><li>Cross product: Using the result, we can tell if the object is to the left () or right ().</li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

