#: 4.x/math/transforms/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=transforms>Transforms</h1><p>Before reading this, make sure you have an understanding of vectors and how they&rsquo;re used in game development. If you don&rsquo;t, I recommend you read this introduction I wrote for the Godot documentation: <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/tutorials/math/vector_math.html target=_blank>Vector Math</a>.</p><h2 id=2d-transforms>2D Transforms</h2><p>In 2D space, we use the familiar X-Y coordinate plane. Remember that in Godot, as in most computer graphics applications, the <strong>Y</strong> axis points downward:</p><p><a href=#image-a1401eb43ce68beaddf2230d3dad68e7 class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_coordinate_plane.png?width=250px\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:250px loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-a1401eb43ce68beaddf2230d3dad68e7><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_coordinate_plane.png?width=250px\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>To begin, let&rsquo;s consider this spaceship floating in space:</p><p><a href=#image-908931a7704a34b5be1b2dacfc4a629a class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_rocket1.png?width=250px\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:250px loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-908931a7704a34b5be1b2dacfc4a629a><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_rocket1.png?width=250px\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>The ship is pointing in the same direction as the <strong>X</strong> axis. If we wanted it to move forward, we could add to its <strong>X</strong> coordinate and it would move to the right:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>But what happens when the ship rotates?</p><p><a href=#image-bca49bffbde1fc6501341fbc84610c9e class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_rocket2.png?width=250px\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:250px loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-bca49bffbde1fc6501341fbc84610c9e><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_rocket2.png?width=250px\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>How do we move the ship forward now? If you remember Trigonometry from school, you might be starting to think about angles, sine and cosine and doing something like . While this would work, there&rsquo;s a much more convenient way: the <em>Transform</em>.</p><p>Let&rsquo;s look at the rotated ship again, but this time, let&rsquo;s also imagine that the ship has its own <strong>X</strong> and <strong>Y</strong> axes that it carries with it, independent of the global axes:</p><p><a href=#image-6080dd3893dca88ad548c045c3192e9c class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_rocket3.png?width=250px\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:250px loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6080dd3893dca88ad548c045c3192e9c><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_rocket3.png?width=250px\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>These &ldquo;local&rdquo; axes are contained in the object&rsquo;s .</p><p>Knowing this, we can move the ship forward by moving it along its own <strong>X</strong> axis and we won&rsquo;t have to worry about angles and trig functions. To do this in Godot, we can use the property, which is available to all <a href=[https://link]%28https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/class_node2d.html%29><i class=gd-Node2D></i></a> derived nodes.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This code says &ldquo;Add the transform&rsquo;s x vector multiplied by 10.&rdquo; Let&rsquo;s break down what that means. The contains and properties that represent those local axes. They are <em>unit vectors</em>, which means their length is . Another term for unit vector is <em>direction vector</em>. They tell us the direction the ship&rsquo;s <strong>x</strong> axis is pointing. We then multiply by to scale it to a longer distance.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>The property of a node is <em>relative</em> to its parent node. If you need to get the global value, it&rsquo;s available in .</p></div></div><p>In addition to the local axes, the transform also contains a component called the . The origin represents the <em>translation</em>, or change in position.</p><p>In this picture, the blue vector is the . It is equal to the object&rsquo;s vector.</p><p><a href=#image-0f8dc5e2884cb5669e20eb904f6be9be class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_rocket4.png?width=250px\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:250px loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-0f8dc5e2884cb5669e20eb904f6be9be><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/0_2d_rocket4.png?width=250px\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=converting-between-local-and-global-space>Converting Between Local and Global Space</h3><p>You can convert coordinates from local to global by applying the transform. For convenience, and include helper functions for this: and :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Let&rsquo;s use the example of an object in the 2D plane and convert mouse clicks (global space) into coordinates relative to the object:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>See the <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/class_transform2d.html target=_blank>Transform2D docs</a> for a list of the available properties and methods.</p><h2 id=3d-transforms>3D Transforms</h2><p>In 3D space, the concept of transforms applies in the same way as in 2D. In fact, it becomes even more necessary, as using angles in 3D can lead to a variety of problems, as we&rsquo;ll see in a bit.</p><p>3D nodes inherit from the base node <i class=gd-Node3D></i>, which contains the transform information. The 3D transform requires more information than the 2D version. Position is still held in the property, but rotation is in a property called , which contains three unit vectors representing the body&rsquo;s local <strong>X</strong>, <strong>Y</strong>, and <strong>Z</strong> axes.</p><p>When you select a 3D node in the editor, the gizmo that appears allows you to manipulate the transform.</p><p><a href=#image-cc4cf0569cb4f9dbeb357a38184b7d0f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_intro_gizmo.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-cc4cf0569cb4f9dbeb357a38184b7d0f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_intro_gizmo.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Local Space Mode</div><div class=box-content><p>In the editor, you can see and manipulate the body&rsquo;s local orientation by clicking the &ldquo;Local Space Mode&rdquo; button. <a href=#image-3c0d791678cb5f111eb87203a3b5618a class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_intro_local_space.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-3c0d791678cb5f111eb87203a3b5618a><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_intro_local_space.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy> </a>When in this mode, the 3 colored axis lines represent the body&rsquo;s local basis axes.</p></div></div><p>As in 2D, we can use the local axes to move an object forward. In Godot&rsquo;s 3D orientation (<strong>Y-up</strong>), this means that by default the body&rsquo;s <strong>-Z</strong> axis is the forward direction. To move forward:</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Tip</div><div class=box-content><p>Godot has default vector values defined, for example: . See <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/class_vector2.html target=_blank>Vector2</a> and <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/class_vector3.html target=_blank>Vector3</a> for details.</p></div></div><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

