#: 4.x/math/interpolation/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=interpolation>Interpolation</h1><p><strong>Linear Interpolation</strong>, or its commonly-used abbreviation <strong>lerp</strong>, is a term that comes up often in game development. If you&rsquo;ve never come across it before it can seem mysterious and highly-technical, but as you&rsquo;ll see in this tutorial, it&rsquo;s actually a straightforward concept with a wide variety of applications in game programming.</p><h2 id=numeric-interpolation>Numeric Interpolation</h2><p>The core formula for linear interpolation is this:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>In this formula, and represent the two values and is the amount of interpolation, typically expressed as a value between (which returns ), and (which returns ). The function finds a value the given amount between the two. For example:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>It&rsquo;s called <em>linear</em> interpolation because the path between the two points is a straight line.</p><p>You can animate a node&rsquo;s properties with . For example, if you divide the elapsed time by the desired duration, you&rsquo;ll get a value between zero and one you can use to alter a property smoothly over time. This script scales a sprite up to five times its starting size while fading it out (using ) over two seconds:</p><div class=highlight></div><h2 id=vector-interpolation>Vector interpolation</h2><p>You can also interpolate between vectors. Both and provide methods for this.</p><p>For example, to find a vector that&rsquo;s halfway between a node&rsquo;s forward and left direction vectors:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The following example moves a Sprite node towards the mouse click position. Each frame the node moves 10% of the way to the target. This results in an &ldquo;approach&rdquo; effect, where the object&rsquo;s speed becomes slower the closer it gets to the target.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>For more advanced applications of interpolation, see .</p><footer class=footline></footer>"
