#: 4.x/kyn/raycast2d/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=raycast2d>RayCast2D</h1><h2 id=hahahugoshortcode91s0hbhbraycast2d><i class=gd-RayCast2D></i>RayCast2D</h2><p><em>Raycasting</em> is a common technique in game development. &ldquo;Casting a ray&rdquo; means extending a line from a point until it collides with something or reaches its limit.</p><h3 id=node-properties>Node properties</h3><p>Add a <i class=gd-RayCast2D></i> node and take a look at the Inspector:</p><p><a href=#image-0eaf1c8397cf88fc9bea20fd23f2f1ba class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kyn_raycast2d_01_4.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-0eaf1c8397cf88fc9bea20fd23f2f1ba><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kyn_raycast2d_01_4.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Here are the main properties you&rsquo;ll need to understand:</p><ul><li><strong>Enabled</strong></li></ul><p>Turn this off to disabled the raycast work.</p><ul><li><strong>Exclude Parent</strong></li></ul><p>This property causes the ray to ignore collisions with the parent object. Enabled by default.</p><ul><li><strong>Target Position</strong></li></ul><p>This is the destination point of the ray. <em>Note:</em> This is in <em>local</em> coordinates.</p><p>Also, take note of the <em>Collide With</em> section. By default the ray will only detect bodies, so you&rsquo;ll need to go here if you want to detect areas as well or instead.</p><h3 id=useful-functions>Useful functions</h3><p>You can see the full list of the node&rsquo;s functions in the <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_raycast2d.html target=_blank>API Documentation</a>. Here are the some of the most useful ones:</p><ul><li></li></ul><p>Boolean function, lets you know if the ray is colliding with something.</p><ul><li></li></ul><p>If the ray is colliding, this will return the position of the collision (in <em>global</em> coordinates).</p><ul><li></li></ul><p>If the ray is colliding, this function will return a reference to the colliding object.</p><ul><li></li></ul><p>Another useful piece of information, this is the normal of the collided object at the point of collision.</p><h3 id=example-uses>Example uses</h3><p>There are many uses for raycasts: visibility (can A see B, or is there an obstacle between?), proximity (am I close to a wall/ground/obstacle?), etc. Here are a couple of practical examples in use:</p><h4 id=1-shooting>1. Shooting</h4><p>Fast-moving projectiles often have the problem of &ldquo;tunneling&rdquo; through obstacles - they are moving too fast for the collision to be detected in a single frame. As an alternative, you can use a <i class=gd-RayCast2D></i> to represent the path (or a laser, etc.).</p><p>Here&rsquo;s a player sprite with a raycast attached to the end of the gun. The is set to .</p><p><a href=#image-14638110716a3defa734fc67c00bc87f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kyn_raycast2d_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-14638110716a3defa734fc67c00bc87f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kyn_raycast2d_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>When the player shoots, you check to see if the ray is colliding with something:</p><div class=highlight></div><h4 id=2-edge-detection>2. Edge detection</h4><p>Consider a platformer enemy that walks on platforms, but you don&rsquo;t want it to fall off the edges. Add two downward-pointing raycasts to the mob like so:</p><p><a href=#image-5dcf49efee5ac4c723a14a817f10ed88 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kyn_raycast2d_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-5dcf49efee5ac4c723a14a817f10ed88><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kyn_raycast2d_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>In the mob&rsquo;s script, check for when the ray <em>stops</em> colliding. That means you&rsquo;ve found the edge and should turn around:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Here&rsquo;s what it looks like in action:</p><p><a href=#image-e32135465fff1c028efc870a8ea9eb21 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kyn_raycast2d_04.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e32135465fff1c028efc870a8ea9eb21><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kyn_raycast2d_04.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
