#: 4.x/ai/changing_behaviors/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=changing-behaviors>Changing Behaviors</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want your AI-controlled entity to switch between different behaviors.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>For this example, we’ll assume an enemy with the following behaviors:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Patrol</strong></p><p>The &ldquo;Patrol&rdquo; state moves along a pre-defined path (or stands still if there&rsquo;s no path assigned).</p></li><li><p><strong>Chase</strong></p><p>The &ldquo;Chase&rdquo; state moves the enemy towards the player. See <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/ai/chasing/>Chasing the player</a> for how to make this behavior.</p></li><li><p><strong>Attack</strong></p><p>In this state, the player is in range of a melee attack, so the enemy stops moving and executes its attack.</p></li></ul><p>These behaviors are states - the enemy can only be in one of these states at a time, and certain events, such as the player getting near, will cause a transition to another state.</p><p>To determine the state transitions, we have two <i class=gd-Area2D></i> nodes on the enemy: an outer one called &ldquo;DetectRadius&rdquo; and an inner called &ldquo;AttackRadius&rdquo;. The player entering or exiting these areas will trigger the related behavior.</p><p><a href=#image-a7deba5b43f6d086433bb72680419c2e class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/behaviors_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-a7deba5b43f6d086433bb72680419c2e><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/behaviors_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We&rsquo;ve chosen a rectangular shape for in this example due to the shape of the enemy&rsquo;s attack. Any shape is fine as long as it&rsquo;s smaller than the .</p><p>Connect the and signals of both these areas. If you&rsquo;re using collision layers (and you should be), set them so that they can only detect the player (or any other body you want to be chased/attacked).</p><p>Now let&rsquo;s examine the enemy&rsquo;s script:</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=highlight></div><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

