#: 4.x/physics/asteroids_physics/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=asteroids-style-physics-using-rigidbody2d>Asteroids-style Physics (using RigidBody2D)</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want to use a <i class=gd-RigidBody2D></i> to create a semi-realistic spaceship, a la <em>Asteroids</em>.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Using <i class=gd-RigidBody2D></i> can be tricky. Because it&rsquo;s controlled by Godot&rsquo;s physics engine, you need to apply forces rather than moving it directly. Before doing anything with rigid bodies, I highly recommend looking at the <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_rigidbody2d.html target=_blank>RigidBody2D API doc</a>, and we&rsquo;ll refer to it as we work through this example.</p><p>For this example, we&rsquo;ll use the following node setup:</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Sprite orientation</div><div class=box-content><p>Don&rsquo;t forget to orient your sprite correctly. An object that is not rotated should be pointing along the <strong>+X</strong> axis, i.e. to the right. If your sprite&rsquo;s art is drawn facing in another direction, rotate the <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> (not the parent body) to align it correctly.</p></div></div><p>We&rsquo;ll use the following inputs in the <strong>Input Map</strong>:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Key</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td><strong>w</strong> or ↑</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><strong>d</strong> or →</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><strong>a</strong> or ←</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Add a script to the body, and let&rsquo;s define some variables:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The first two variables are how we&rsquo;ll control the ship&rsquo;s &ldquo;handling&rdquo;. is going to affect acceleration and top speed. controls how fast the ship rotates.</p><p> and are going to be set by pressing the inputs. Let&rsquo;s do that next:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>If we&rsquo;re pressing the input, we&rsquo;ll set the vector to the ship&rsquo;s forward direction, while will be based on the rotate inputs.</p><p>We can start flying by applying those values in :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>It works, but you&rsquo;ll notice that it&rsquo;s very hard to control. The rotation is too fast, and it accelerates to a high speed before going offscreen. This is where we want to break from &ldquo;real&rdquo; space physics. In space, there&rsquo;s no friction, but our Asteroids-style ship will be a lot easier to control if it coasted to a stop when we&rsquo;re not thrusting. We can control this with <em>damping</em>.</p><p>In the <i class=gd-RigidBody2D></i> properties, you&rsquo;ll find <strong>Linear/Damp</strong> and <strong>Angular/Damp</strong>. Set these to and respectively, and they&rsquo;ll slow the movement/rotation as well as causing them to stop.</p><p>Feel free to experiment with these values and how they interact with the and </p><h3 id=screen-wrapping>Screen wrapping</h3><p>Wrapping around the screen is really teleportation: when the ship goes off the right side of the screen, you teleport it to the left side. However, if you just tried to change the , you&rsquo;d find that it instantly snapped back. This is because the physics engine is trying to control the position as well.</p><p>The solution to this is to use the callback of the rigid body. In this function, you can safely update the physics properties of the object without conflicting with what the physics engine is doing.</p><p>Let&rsquo;s get the screensize at the top of the script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Then add the new function:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>As you can see, the function includes a parameter called . This object is the <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_physicsdirectbodystate2d.html target=_blank>PhysicsDirectBodyState2D</a> of our body. It contains all of the current physics properties such as the forces, velocity, position, etc.</p><p>From the state, we grab the current transform, modify it to wrap around the screen using , and then set it back to the current state.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s how it looks:</p><p><a href=#image-47ff963bc8a3db2736cd90a3427777b0 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/asteroids_wrap.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-47ff963bc8a3db2736cd90a3427777b0><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/asteroids_wrap.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=warping>Warping</h3><p>Let&rsquo;s look at one more example of using to alter the body&rsquo;s state without issues. Let&rsquo;s add a &ldquo;warp&rdquo; mechanic - when the player presses the input, the ship will teleport to a random spot on the screen.</p><p>First, we&rsquo;ll add a new variable for this:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Then, in , we&rsquo;ll set a random position:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Finally, in , if there&rsquo;s a set, we&rsquo;ll use it and then clear it:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-3d3b38131914a1509c2fd6b7e4c31e32 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/asteroids_warp.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-3d3b38131914a1509c2fd6b7e4c31e32><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/asteroids_warp.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/asteroids_physics target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/asteroids_physics</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
