#: 4.x/physics/smooth_rigid_move/index.html
msgid "RigidBody2D: Move to Target :: Godot 4 Recipes"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/physics/smooth_rigid_move/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=rigidbody2d-move-to-target>RigidBody2D: Move to Target</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want a rigid body to move to a target position.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Using <i class=gd-RigidBody2D></i> can be tricky. Because it&rsquo;s controlled by Godot&rsquo;s physics engine, you need to apply forces rather than moving it directly. Before doing anything with rigid bodies, I highly recommend looking at the <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_rigidbody2d.html target=_blank>RigidBody2D API doc</a>.</p><p>To move a body, we need to apply a push in a given direction - a <em>force</em>. Once the body is moving, we want the force to get smaller as we get closer to the final position.</p><p>This is the perfect situation to use the function. It will tell us how far away the target is, and we can use that to set the force.</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Use linear damp</div><div class=box-content><p>If you try this using the default <i class=gd-RigidBody2D></i> settings, you will notice that the body shoots right past the target. This is due to the body&rsquo;s <strong>Linear/Damp</strong> property, which has a default setting (found in the <em>Project Settings</em> of ). This value represents &ldquo;friction&rdquo; and controls how quickly a moving rigid body will come to a stop when no force is applied. Increasing this value will ensure that your body coasts to a stop at the target. Experiment with how this value and the interact to get the exactly the movement you&rsquo;re looking for.</p></div></div><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/physics/smooth_rigid_rotate/>RigidBody2D: Look at Target</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

