msgid "<h1 id=rigidbody2d-drag-and-drop>RigidBody2D: Drag and Drop</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want to pick up and move rigid bodies with the mouse.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Working with rigid bodies can be tricky. Godot&rsquo;s physics engine controls their movements, and interfering with that can often lead to unexpected results. The key is to make use of the body&rsquo;s property. This applies equally well in 2D or 3D.</p><h3 id=body-setup>Body setup</h3><p>We&rsquo;ll start with our rigid body object, adding a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> and <i class=gd-CollisionShape2D></i>. You can also add a if you want to set <em>Bounce</em> and <em>Friction</em> properties.</p><p>We&rsquo;re going to use the rigid body&rsquo;s property to remove it from the control of the physics engine while we&rsquo;re dragging it. Since we still want it to be movable, we need to set the <strong>Freeze Mode</strong> to &ldquo;Kinematic&rdquo;, rather than the default value of &ldquo;Static&rdquo;.</p><p>Place the body in a group called &ldquo;pickable&rdquo;. We&rsquo;ll use this to allow for multiple instances of the pickable object in the main scene. Attach a script to the body and connect the its signal.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ll emit a signal when a mouse click is detected, including a reference to the body. Since there can be many bodies, we&rsquo;ll let the main scene manage whether a body can be dragged or if there&rsquo;s already one in the state.</p><p>If the body is being dragged, we update its position to follow the mouse.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Finally, these are the two functions to call when the body is picked up and dropped. Changing the to removes the body from physics engine processing. Note that other objects can still collide with it. If you don&rsquo;t want that, you can disable the and/or here as well. Just remember to re-enable them when dropping.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>In the function, after we change back to `false, the body will return to the physics engine&rsquo;s control. By passing in an optional impulse value, we can add the ability to &ldquo;throw&rdquo; the object on release.</p><h3 id=main-scene>Main scene</h3><p>Create a main scene with some static body obstacles or a <i class=gd-TileMap></i> and instance a few copies of the pickable body.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s the script for the main scene. We start by connecting the signal on any pickable bodies that are in the scene.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Next, we have the function we connect the signal to. The connected function sets so that we know something is currently being dragged, and calls the body&rsquo;s method.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Lastly, when the mouse is released during dragging, we can perform the reverse actions.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Note the use of to pass the impulse to the object - be careful with this! You may find yourself launching the rigid bodies at high speeds, especially if the bodies have low values. It&rsquo;s probably a good idea to scale this to a reasonable value and it to some maximum. Experiment to find out what works for you.</p><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/rbody_drag.webm></video></p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/rigidbody_drag_drop target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/rigidbody_drag_drop</a></p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

