#: 4.x/physics/character_vs_rigid/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=character-to-rigid-body-interaction>Character to Rigid Body Interaction</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want your character body to interact with rigid bodies.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>This recipe applies equally well in both 2D and 3D nodes.</p></div></div><p>By default, a <i class=gd-CharacterBody2D></i> moved with or will not push any <i class=gd-RigidBody2D></i> it collides with. The rigid body doesn&rsquo;t react at all, and behaves just like a <i class=gd-StaticBody2D></i>.</p><p><a href=#image-ef21b9113ecca042b55eff7062471a42 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/char_push_default.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-ef21b9113ecca042b55eff7062471a42><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/char_push_default.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>In some cases, this might be all you need. However, if you want to be able to push the bodies, you&rsquo;ll need to make some changes.</p><p>For this example, we&rsquo;ll use the 2D character described in the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/platform_character/>Platform character</a> recipe. This example uses the most common movement method for character bodies: . If you&rsquo;re using , you&rsquo;ll need to adjust the examples below accordingly.</p><p>You have two options when deciding how to interact with rigid bodies:</p><ol><li>You can just push them, ignoring physics. If you&rsquo;re familiar with Godot 3.x, this is equivalent to the &ldquo;infinite inertia&rdquo; option.</li><li>You can give them a push based on the character&rsquo;s imagined &ldquo;mass&rdquo; and velocity. This will give you a &ldquo;realistic&rdquo; result - pushing heavy bodies a little, and lighter bodies a lot.</li></ol><p>We&rsquo;ll try out both options below.</p><h3 id=infinite-inertia>Infinite Inertia</h3><p>This option has its pros and cons. The biggest pro is, you don&rsquo;t need any extra code. You just need to correctly set the collision layers/masks of the objects. For this example, we&rsquo;ve defined three physics layers:</p><p><a href=#image-1b36a15f393658b0123838c928699e32 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_physics_layers_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-1b36a15f393658b0123838c928699e32><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_physics_layers_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>For the rigid body, we&rsquo;ve placed it on the &ldquo;items&rdquo; layer (layer 3), and left the mask at the default (masking all layers):</p><p><a href=#image-384d6a52f40eb97c99671d733a58aae2 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/physics_layers_box.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-384d6a52f40eb97c99671d733a58aae2><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/physics_layers_box.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Then, we&rsquo;ve placed the player on the &ldquo;player&rdquo; layer (layer 2), and configured the mask to ignore the &ldquo;items&rdquo;:</p><p><a href=#image-9300daed0d189a78e30bf17fbced1311 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/physics_layers_player.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-9300daed0d189a78e30bf17fbced1311><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/physics_layers_player.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Running the game, we now see we can push the boxes around. Note that the mass of the box doesn&rsquo;t matter - they&rsquo;ll all be pushed the same.</p><p><a href=#image-c70547687b605c77a3aa3af0598bebc0 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/char_push_inf.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-c70547687b605c77a3aa3af0598bebc0><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/char_push_inf.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Here, you can also see the downside of this option. Because the physics of the boxes is being ignored, they can clip through walls and you can&rsquo;t jump on top of them.</p><p>For some games, this will be fine. If you want to prevent the clipping, you&rsquo;ll need to go with option 2.</p><h3 id=applying-impulses>Applying impulses</h3><p>To give the colliding body a &ldquo;push&rdquo; we&rsquo;ll need to apply an impulse. An impulse is an instantaneous &ldquo;kick&rdquo; - think of a bat hitting a ball. This is as opposed to a force, which is a continuous &ldquo;push&rdquo; on an object.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The collision normal points <em>out</em> of the rigid body, so we reverse it to point away from the character and apply the factor. Now pushing works again, but it won&rsquo;t force the rigid bodies through walls:</p><p><a href=#image-671da1aa4f3ad796f2cbc85ae77bfaed class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/char_push_impulse.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-671da1aa4f3ad796f2cbc85ae77bfaed><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/char_push_impulse.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>You&rsquo;ll need to adjust the in relation to the mass of your rigid bodies. Too high a force will still cause clipping, while too low will prevent pushing at all.</p><p>Experiment to find the settings that work for your particular game.</p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/character_vs_rigid target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/character_vs_rigid</a></p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/platform_character/>Platform character</a></li></ul><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-videoi-watch-video><i class=\"fas fa-video\"></i> Watch Video</h2><div style=position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden><iframe src=https://www.youtube.com/embed/SJuScDavstM style=position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;border:0 allowfullscreen title=\"YouTube Video\"></iframe></div><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

