#: 4.x/2d/coyote_time/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=coyote-time>Coyote Time</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>Your platformer jumping feels &ldquo;off&rdquo;. Players don&rsquo;t have good control and sometimes they &ldquo;miss&rdquo; jumping off the edge of platforms.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>The answer to this problem is to use a technique called &ldquo;coyote time&rdquo;. This gives the player a greater feeling of control and a little &ldquo;wiggle room&rdquo; around the process of jumping from the edges of platforms.</p><p>&ldquo;Coyote time&rdquo; works like this:</p><p>If the player walks off the edge of a platform, for a few frames afterward, we still allow them to jump as if they were still on the ground.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Origins</div><div class=box-content><p>The name &ldquo;coyote time&rdquo; comes from the famous cartoon coyote, who wouldn&rsquo;t fall until he looked down:</p><p><a href=#image-6ea41fda19a183a6eda121625d939a2c class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/coyote.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6ea41fda19a183a6eda121625d939a2c><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/coyote.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p></div></div><p>We&rsquo;re going to add this to an already existing platform character. See the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/platform_character/>Platform character</a> recipe for how to set one up.</p><p>To handle the timing, we&rsquo;ll add a <i class=gd-Timer></i> node called and set it to <strong>One Shot</strong>.</p><p>There are a few new variables we&rsquo;ll need to keep track of coyote time:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Since we&rsquo;re using frames to set the duration, we can translate that to time when setting the <i class=gd-Timer></i>&rsquo;s length in :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Each frame we&rsquo;ll store the current value of to be used in the following frame, so put this in after the :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>When we detect the jump input, we need to check if the character is on the floor <em>or</em> in coyote time:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Coyote time begins if the player walks off the edge of a platform. That means that they are no longer on the floor, but were on the floor in the previous frame. We can check that like this, and start the timer if we did just transition from on- to off-floor:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The tells us when the coyote state ends:</p><div class=highlight></div><div class=\"box notices cstyle tip\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-lightbulb\"></i> Implementing in 3D</div><div class=box-content><p>You can apply the same process to 3d characters.</p></div></div><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>The character in the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/moving_platforms>Moving Platforms</a> project has coyote time implemented.</p><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/2d_moving_platforms target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/2d_moving_platforms</a></p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/platform_character/>Platform character</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

