#: 4.x/2d/2d_align_surface/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=characterbody2d-align-with-surface>CharacterBody2D: align with surface</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You need your character body to align with the surface it&rsquo;s standing on.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>We&rsquo;ll start with a basic kinematic platform character. See the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/platform_character/>Platform character</a> recipe for details.</p><p>We have the following code for movement:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_align_01.webm></video></p><p>As you can see, there are a couple of problems. First, the character flies off the slope when running. It&rsquo;s also sliding down the slope when there&rsquo;s no input.</p><p>We can partially solve this by switching from to :</p><div class=highlight></div><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_align_02.webm></video></p><p>Now we have an upward &ldquo;hop&rdquo; when we stop on the way up the slope. This is because our velocity is set to by the lack of input, but the is not.</p><h2 id=orienting-the-velocity>Orienting the velocity</h2><p>We can fix this by orienting our velocity relative to the slope. To illustrate, let&rsquo;s first rotate the character to align with the slope. We can do this by checking the floor normal when we&rsquo;re on the floor:</p><div class=highlight></div><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_align_03.webm></video></p><p>This hasn&rsquo;t changed anything about the movement yet, but it does help us visualize what we need to do. When we&rsquo;re on the slope, our local transform looks like this:</p><p><a href=#image-cf0288416287849c441bf3942870b140 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_align_04.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-cf0288416287849c441bf3942870b140><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_align_04.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Now when we move, we want our velocity to align with our local axis (the red arrow), and gravity/jump to align with local (the green arrow). We can keep our input code the same, and just assume that is always calculated in the local coordinate system. The only problem will be that expects the velocity vector to be in <em>global</em> coordinates. Let&rsquo;s adjust to account for this:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ve changed a few things here, so let&rsquo;s look at them carefully.</p><ul><li>The vector is now our local down vector, so it will always point directly into the slope.</li><li>The parameter is also changed to the local up direction ().</li><li>We convert velocity to global by rotating it to match the player&rsquo;s rotation, then revert the resulting velocity back to local by doing the reverse.</li></ul><p>The result:</p><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_align_05.webm></video></p><h2 id=wrapping-up>Wrapping up</h2><p>This technique allows for a wide range of possible platformer-style movement schemes. For example, you can do fun things like this:</p><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_align_06.webm></video></p><p>Here&rsquo;s the full script:</p><div class=highlight></div><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/platform_character>Platform character</a></li><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/physics/godot3_kinematic2d/>Using KinematicBody2D</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

