#: 4.x/2d/using_ysort/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=using-y-sort>Using Y-Sort</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>Many 2D games use a &ldquo;3/4 view&rdquo; perspective, giving the impression that the camera is looking at the world at an angle. To make this work, objects that are &ldquo;farther&rdquo; away need to be rendered behind &ldquo;nearer&rdquo; objects. In practice, that means we want to &ldquo;y-sort&rdquo; - making the drawing order tied to the object&rsquo;s coordinate. The higher on the screen, the farther away and therefore lower the render order.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s an example of the problem:</p><p><a href=#image-de52a322a0c58d16afbc60617c9fbbb3 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-de52a322a0c58d16afbc60617c9fbbb3><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>These objects are being drawn in the default render order: tree order. They are arranged like this in the scene tree:</p><p><a href=#image-fd79ccd23f21b7e1ca20f6917282bcab class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_06.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-fd79ccd23f21b7e1ca20f6917282bcab><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_06.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Godot has a built-in option to change the render order: on any <i class=gd-CanvasItem></i> node (<i class=gd-Node2D></i> or <i class=gd-Control></i>), we can enable the <strong>Y Sort Enabled</strong> property. When this is enabled, all child nodes are then y-sorted.</p><p>In the above example, we can enable the property on the <i class=gd-TileMap></i> node. However, there&rsquo;s still a problem:</p><p><a href=#image-71669fd0364604c1bfc0304083e0ae3b class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-71669fd0364604c1bfc0304083e0ae3b><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>The draw order is based on each object&rsquo;s coordinate. By default, that is the object&rsquo;s center:</p><p><a href=#image-06ad0c27bede9b4c60d963ab204e9b16 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_04.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-06ad0c27bede9b4c60d963ab204e9b16><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_04.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Since we want to give the impression that the objects are on the &ldquo;ground&rdquo;, we can solve this by offsetting each object&rsquo;s sprite so that the object&rsquo;s is aligned with the <em>bottom</em> of the sprite:</p><p><a href=#image-74aab39e2d0f7d288b3583a3c591d6e3 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_05.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-74aab39e2d0f7d288b3583a3c591d6e3><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_05.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Now things look a lot better:</p><p><a href=#image-d724ae0ab79c2828abe856f5a6953009 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_02.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-d724ae0ab79c2828abe856f5a6953009><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/ysort_02.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/using_ysort target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/using_ysort</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

