#: 4.x/2d/grid_pathfinding/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=pathfinding-on-a-2d-grid>Pathfinding on a 2D Grid</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You have a grid-based environment and you&rsquo;d like to set up pathfinding to allow navigation.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Godot provides a number of methods for pathfinding. For this recipe, we&rsquo;ll consider the algorithm.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> About A*</div><div class=box-content><p>A* is a widely-used algorithm for finding the shortest path between two points. It can be used in any graph-based data structure, not just a grid.</p></div></div><p> is a specialized version of Godot&rsquo;s more generic class. Because it&rsquo;s specialized for using with a grid, it&rsquo;s quicker and easier to set up because you don&rsquo;t have to manually add all the individual grid cells and their connections.</p><h3 id=setting-up-the-grid>Setting up the Grid</h3><p>The most important configuration decision is the size of the cells and the size of the grid itself. We&rsquo;ll use for this example, and we&rsquo;ll use the window size to determine how many cells fit on the screen, but everything will work the same regardless of cell size.</p><p>Add this code to a <i class=gd-Node2D></i>.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>In this code, we divide the size of the screen by the to calculate how big the whole grid will be. This lets us set the property of the .</p><p>The property will come into play when we ask for a path between two points. Using means the path will be calculated from the center of each cell rather than the corners.</p><p>Finally, we need to call after setting or changing any of the &rsquo;s properties.</p><h3 id=drawing-the-grid>Drawing the Grid</h3><p>For the purposes of this demo, we&rsquo;ll draw the grid on the screen in code. In a game application, you&rsquo;ll probably have a <i class=gd-TileMap></i> or some other visual representation of your world.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s some code to draw the grid:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This gives us a nice visual of the grid:</p><p><a href=#image-15a46aa3c539a5c4eeb2931c8d5f7a34 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-15a46aa3c539a5c4eeb2931c8d5f7a34><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=drawing-the-path>Drawing the Path</h3><p>In order to find a path, we need a start and end point. Add these variables at the top of the script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>And a couple of lines in to show them:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We can find the path between the two points using the method, but we also need to visualize it. We can use a <i class=gd-Line2D></i>, so add one to the scene.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s how we can get the path, and add the resulting points to the <i class=gd-Line2D></i>:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Here&rsquo;s the result:</p><p><a href=#image-633707ba7fbe99edac3f9ee56234abe9 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-633707ba7fbe99edac3f9ee56234abe9><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Note that we have a diagonal line between the two points. This is because, by default, the path will use diagonals. This can be modified by changing the :</p><ul><li> - The default value, uses diagonals.</li><li> - All movement is orthogonal.</li><li> - This allows diagonals, but prevents the path going &ldquo;between&rdquo; diagonally placed obstacles.</li><li> - This allows diagonals only in &ldquo;open&rdquo; areas, not near obstacles.</li></ul><p>Modifying this property can give you very different results, so make sure to experiment based on your setup. Let&rsquo;s add this in the function:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now we only have orthogonal moves:</p><p><a href=#image-b86c407a25d563b1a5ba6aecee09530c class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-b86c407a25d563b1a5ba6aecee09530c><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=adding-obstacles>Adding Obstacles</h3><p>We can also add obstacles to the grid. By marking a cell as &ldquo;solid&rdquo;, the path will not include that cell. A cell can be toggled solid/not solid by using the function.</p><p>Let&rsquo;s add some code to draw our walls (when they exist), by finding any solid cells and coloring them in:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Call this function in .</p><p>Then, we can use the mouse to click on cells and toggle their state:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Note that we&rsquo;re checking first - this will prevent errors from being thrown if we click outside the grid boundaries.</p><p>Now we can see the effect of obstacles on the path:</p><p><a href=#image-ed53d1bf01fe98db9d032137f62d7e5e class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_04.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-ed53d1bf01fe98db9d032137f62d7e5e><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_04.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=choosing-a-heuristic>Choosing a Heuristic</h3><p>A big factor that affects the resulting path is what <em>heuristic</em> you choose to use. The term &ldquo;heuristic&rdquo; refers to a &ldquo;best guess&rdquo;, and in the context of pathfinding just means: what direction should we try first when moving toward the goal?</p><p>For example, the Euclidean distance uses the Pythagorean theorem to estimate the path to try:</p><p><a href=#image-7b9ea3855f84c65369b4433dfa554f50 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_03.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-7b9ea3855f84c65369b4433dfa554f50><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_03.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>While Manhattan distance only considers distance in N/S or E/W directions:</p><p><a href=#image-2a0943569d4b9036c487f326f11292a5 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_manhattan.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-2a0943569d4b9036c487f326f11292a5><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_manhattan.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>And the Octile heuristic results in a path like this:</p><p><a href=#image-701af8e2aabf55eaacd4c1afed1f94e4 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_octile.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-701af8e2aabf55eaacd4c1afed1f94e4><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/astar_grid_octile.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>You can choose the heuristic using this property:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Which of these works best (results in the most pleasing paths) depends on the nature of your environment. Is it mostly wide-open spaces with few obstacles scattered around? Or is it a maze of twisty passages? Make sure to experiment with your specific project.</p><p>Download the example project below to experiment with this setup yourself. In addition to placing walls, you can use the right/middle mouse buttons to move the end/start locations.</p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project&rsquo;s example code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/grid_pathfinding target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/grid_pathfinding</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

