#: 4.x/2d/car_steering/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=car-steering>Car steering</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You need to create a 2D top-down car controller.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>When approaching this problem, beginners often wind up creating something that handles nothing like a real car. Some common mistakes you&rsquo;ll find in amateur car games:</p><ul><li>A car doesn&rsquo;t rotate around its center. Put another way, a car&rsquo;s rear wheels don&rsquo;t slide side-to-side. (Unless it&rsquo;s drifting, but we&rsquo;ll talk about that later.)</li><li>A car can <em>only</em> turn when it&rsquo;s moving - it can&rsquo;t spin in place.</li><li>A car isn&rsquo;t a train; it&rsquo;s not on rails. Turning at high speeds should involve some sliding (drifting).</li></ul><p>There are many approaches to 2D car physics, mainly depending on how &ldquo;realistic&rdquo; you want to be. For this solution, we&rsquo;re going for an &ldquo;arcade&rdquo; level of realism, meaning we&rsquo;ll prioritize action over realism.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>The method below is based on the algorithm found here: <a href=http://engineeringdotnet.blogspot.com/2010/04/simple-2d-car-physics-in-games.html target=_blank>http://engineeringdotnet.blogspot.com/2010/04/simple-2d-car-physics-in-games.html</a></p></div></div><p>The recipe below is broken into 5 parts, each adding a different feature to the car&rsquo;s movement. Feel free to mix-and-match for your needs.</p><h3 id=scene-setup>Scene setup</h3><p>Here&rsquo;s the car scene setup:</p><p>Add whatever sprite texture you like. For this demo, we&rsquo;ll use art from <a href=https://kenney.nl/assets/racing-pack target=_blank>Kenney&rsquo;s Racing Pack</a>. <i class=gd-CapsuleShape2D></i> is a good choice for the collision, so that the car won&rsquo;t have sharp corners to get caught on obstacles.</p><p>We&rsquo;ll also use four input actions: &ldquo;steer_right&rdquo;, &ldquo;steer_left&rdquo;, &ldquo;accelerate&rdquo;, and &ldquo;brake&rdquo; - set them to whatever key inputs you prefer.</p><h3 id=part-1-movement>Part 1: Movement</h3><p>The first step is to code the movement based on the algorithm described above.</p><p>Start with a few variables:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Set to a value that works with your sprite.</p><p> will be the amount that the wheels are turned.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>Since we&rsquo;re using keyboard controls, turning is all-or-nothing. If you&rsquo;re using an analog joystick, you can instead vary this value based on the distance the stick moves.</p></div></div><div class=highlight></div><p>Each frame, we need to check for input and calculate steering. Then we pass the resulting to . We&rsquo;ll define those two function next:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Here we check for user input and set the velocity. Note: the speed of is temporary so that we can test movement. We&rsquo;ll address it in the next part.</p><p>Here is where we implement the algorithm from the link:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Run the project and the car should move and turn. It&rsquo;s still very unnatural though - the car starts and stops instantly. To fix that, we&rsquo;ll add acceleration into the calculation.</p><h3 id=part-2-acceleration>Part 2: Acceleration</h3><p>We&rsquo;ll need another setting variable and one to track the car&rsquo;s overall acceleration:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Change the input code to apply acceleration instead of directly changing the car&rsquo;s .</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Once we&rsquo;ve got our acceleration, we can apply it to the velocity like so:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now when you run, the car should gradually increase its speed. Careful: we don&rsquo;t have any way to slow down yet!</p><h3 id=part-3-frictiondrag>Part 3: Friction/drag</h3><p>A car experiences two different deceleration forces: <em>friction</em> and <em>drag</em>.</p><ul><li><p>Friction is the force applied by the ground. It&rsquo;s very high if driving on sand, but very low if driving on ice. Friction is proportional to velocity - the faster you&rsquo;re going the stronger the force.</p></li><li><p>Drag is the force resulting from wind resistance. It&rsquo;s based on the car&rsquo;s cross-section - a large truck or van experiences more drag than a sleek race car. Drag is proportional to the velocity <em>squared</em>.</p></li></ul><p>This means that friction is more significant when moving slowly, but drag becomes dominant at high speeds. We&rsquo;ll add both of these forces to our calculation. As a bonus, the values of these quantities will also give our car a maximum speed - the point where the force from the engine can&rsquo;t overcome the drag force any longer.</p><p>Here are our starting values for these quantities:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>As you can see in this graph, these values mean that at a speed of the drag force overcomes the friction force.</p><p><a href=#image-3efb7a809ccbbfb621a93ee64a1062ac class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/car_graph_friction.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-3efb7a809ccbbfb621a93ee64a1062ac><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/car_graph_friction.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>You can play with the values here to see how they change: <a href=https://www.desmos.com/calculator/e4ayu3xkip target=_blank>https://www.desmos.com/calculator/e4ayu3xkip</a></p><p>In we&rsquo;ll call a function to calculate the current friction and apply it to the acceleration force.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>First, we&rsquo;ll set a minimum speed. This will ensure that the car doesn&rsquo;t keep creeping forward at very low speeds as friction never quite brings the velocity to zero.</p><p>Then we calculate the two forces and add them to the total acceleration. Since they&rsquo;re both negative, they&rsquo;ll affect the car in the opposite direction.</p><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/car_friction.webm></video></p><h3 id=part-4-reversebrake>Part 4: Reverse/Brake</h3><p>We&rsquo;ll need two more settings variables:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Add the input to :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This is fine for coming to a stop, but we also want to be able to put the car in reverse. Currently, that won&rsquo;t work, because the acceleration is always being applied in the &ldquo;heading&rdquo; direction, which is forward. When we&rsquo;re reversing, we need to accelerate backward.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We can find whether we&rsquo;re accelerating forward or backward using the dot product. If the two vectors are aligned, the result will be greater than . If the movement is in the opposite direction the car&rsquo;s facing, then the dot product will be less than and we must be moving backward.</p><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/car_reverse.webm></video></p><h3 id=part-5-driftslide>Part 5: Drift/slide</h3><p>We could stop here and you&rsquo;d have a satisfactory driving experience. However, the car still feels like it&rsquo;s &ldquo;on rails&rdquo;. Even at top speed, the turns are perfect, as if the tires have perfect &ldquo;grip&rdquo;.</p><p>At high speeds (or even low ones, if desired), the turning force should cause the tires to slip and result in a fishtailing/sliding motion.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ll apply these values when calculating the steering. Currently, the velocity is instantly set to the new heading. Instead, we&rsquo;ll use interpolation - - to cause it to only &ldquo;turn&rdquo; partway towards the new direction. The &ldquo;traction&rdquo; values will determine how &ldquo;sticky&rdquo; the tires are.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Here, we select which traction value to use and apply to the .</p><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/car_drift.webm></video></p><h3 id=adjustments>Adjustments</h3><p>At this point, we have a large number of settings that control the car&rsquo;s behavior. Adjusting them can drastically change how the car drives. To make experimenting with different values easier, download the project for this recipe below. When you run the game, you&rsquo;ll see a set of sliders you can use to change the car&rsquo;s behavior as you drive (press to show/hide the slider panel).</p><p><a href=#image-e279e9a79178e9085a6e6309d93717f9 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/car_sliders.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e279e9a79178e9085a6e6309d93717f9><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/car_sliders.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/math/interpolation/>Gamedev Math: Interpolation</a></li></ul><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/2d_car_steering target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/2d_car_steering</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
