#: 4.x/2d/screen_shake/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=screen-shake>Screen Shake</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You want to create a &ldquo;screen shake&rdquo; effect.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>The &ldquo;screen shake&rdquo; effect is a popular way to add a dynamic feel to your game. When used well, it adds a lot of appeal. When misused, players hate it. Keep this in mind when adding it to your game and use wisely.</p><p>The effect is quite simple: when an event occurs - an explosion, a heavy object hitting an obstacle, etc. - we want to rapidly displace the camera in small increments for a brief time.</p><p>There are many ways to implement this effect. For this recipe, we&rsquo;ll be emulating the technique outlined in the following GDC talk:</p><div style=position:relative;padding-bottom:56.25%;height:0;overflow:hidden><iframe src=https://www.youtube.com/embed/tu-Qe66AvtY style=position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;height:100%;border:0 allowfullscreen title=\"YouTube Video\"></iframe></div><p>To summarize, the camera will have a property, measuring how much the camera is shaking. Every time something happens that should shake the camera, we&rsquo;ll add a bit more trauma - a large amount for big events or a little for small ones. Over time, the trauma decreases.</p><h3 id=trauma>Trauma</h3><p>We&rsquo;ll start by implementing the camera. Make a new scene with a <i class=gd-Camera2D></i> node, name it , and attach a script.</p><p>First, define the parameters that control the shake behavior:</p><div class=highlight></div><p> is the relationship between and the actual movement of the camera (ie ). A square (2) or cube (3) relationship is typically the best, but feel free to experiment.</p><p>We&rsquo;ve also added a variable so that you can place an instance of this camera anywhere you like and set the target node it will follow.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The function is what we call to start or increase shaking. Pass it a value from to .</p><div class=highlight></div><p>In we&rsquo;ll move the camera to track its target. If there is any trauma, we&rsquo;ll use to reduce it and then call to move the camera.</p><h3 id=random-displacement>Random displacement</h3><p>The function applies a random movement to the camera based on the current level (raised to the given power). In 2D, we have two translational and one rotational movement to calculate. Picking a random number between and is an easy way to get a movement in either direction.</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-8c2fb44422532bbc9a42b4cc3818373c class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/3.x/img/shake_rand01.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-8c2fb44422532bbc9a42b4cc3818373c><img src=/godot_recipes/3.x/img/shake_rand01.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>This looks OK, but we can make it look better.</p><h3 id=using-noise>Using noise</h3><p>The drawback with using for the movement is that it can change greatly from frame to frame, making for a rather erratic feel. To get a more &ldquo;smooth&rdquo; randomness, we can turn to something called <em>noise</em>.</p><p>Noise, or more specifically <em>gradient noise</em> is a method for generating more natural-looking &ldquo;random&rdquo; patterns. To do this, Godot provides the <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/class_opensimplexnoise.html target=_blank>OpenSimplexNoise</a> class.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Info</div><div class=box-content><p>Probably the most well-known gradient noise algorithm is called <a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perlin_noise target=_blank>Perlin noise</a>. Because it and its successor, <a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simplex_noise target=_blank>simplex noise</a>, are protected by patents, Godot uses an algorithm called <a href=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenSimplex_noise target=_blank>OpenSimplex</a> to generate noise.</p></div></div><p> works by generating a 3D &ldquo;cloud&rdquo; of points. Each point has a value ranging from to . Below are two examples of noise generated by . In the images, each pixel&rsquo;s white value is mapped to the noise value at that point.</p><p><a href=#image-b7723d9f2975b65b56e89f5a4e893508 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/3.x/img/2d_noise_example.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-b7723d9f2975b65b56e89f5a4e893508><img src=/godot_recipes/3.x/img/2d_noise_example.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>As you can see, while &ldquo;noisy&rdquo;, if you pick a particular pixel, adjacent pixels may vary unpredictably, but won&rsquo;t suddenly swing wildly from one extreme to another. The behavior can be adjusted to suit your needs, but we won&rsquo;t go into the details of configuring OpenSimplexNoise here. See the <a href=#related-recipes>related recipes</a> section below for links to more information.</p><p>Add the following code at the top of the script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Then, in the function:</p><div class=highlight></div><p> returns the noise value at a given coordinate. We don&rsquo;t want to use the same noise value for all three offsets, so we&rsquo;re arbitrarily choosing three different (but distant) values in the noise, and then using the steadily increasing value to &ldquo;move&rdquo; along the gradient at each point.</p><p><a href=#image-c8ae7a3eeaa19fc3a31cde8c2fa77e37 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/3.x/img/shake_noise01.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-c8ae7a3eeaa19fc3a31cde8c2fa77e37><img src=/godot_recipes/3.x/img/shake_noise01.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>Download the project file here: <a href=/godot_recipes/3.x/files/screen_shake.zip>screen_shake.zip</a></p></div></div><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/3.x/math/noise/>Noise</a></li><li><a href=http://kidscancode.org/godot_recipes/ai/platform_character target=_blank>Platform character</a></li></ul><h4 id=like-video>Like video?</h4><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/2d/moving_platforms/index.html
msgid "Moving Platforms :: Godot 4 Recipes"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/2d/moving_platforms/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=moving-platforms>Moving Platforms</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You need moving platforms in your 2D platformer.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>There are several ways to approach this problem. In this recipe, we&rsquo;ll use <i class=gd-AnimatableBody2D></i>s for our platform and move it with a <i class=gd-Tween></i>. This allows for a variety of movement styles while minimizing the amount of code we need to write.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Info</div><div class=box-content><p>You can also implement this moving platform technique using an <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> rather than a tween. Much of the setup will be the same, but rather than tween code, you&rsquo;ll animate the body&rsquo;s property.</p></div></div><h3 id=setting-up>Setting up</h3><p>We&rsquo;ll start with a basic platformer setup using the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/platform_character/>Platform character</a> recipe. The basic movement from that recipe will work fine with the platforms. If you&rsquo;ve modified it or used your own, everything should still work the same.</p><h3 id=creating-the-platform>Creating the platform</h3><p>The platform scene contains the following nodes:</p><ul><li><i class=gd-Node2D></i> (&ldquo;MovingPlatform&rdquo;): The parent is there to act as the &ldquo;anchor&rdquo; or start point for the platform. We&rsquo;ll animate the platform&rsquo;s relative to this parent node.<ul><li><i class=gd-AnimatableBody2D></i>: This represents the platform itself. This is the node that will move.<ul><li><i class=gd-Sprite2D></i>: You can use a sprite sheet here, individual images, or even a <i class=gd-TileMap></i>.</li><li><i class=gd-CollisionShape2D></i>: Don&rsquo;t make the hitbox too big, or the player will appear to be &ldquo;hovering&rdquo; off the edge of the platform.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><p>Set up the <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i>&rsquo;s <strong>Texture</strong> and the collision shape appropriately. In the <i class=gd-AnimatableBody2D></i>, set the <strong>Sync to Physics</strong> property &ldquo;On&rdquo;. Since we&rsquo;re moving the body in code, this ensures that it&rsquo;s moved during the physics step, keeping it in sync with the player and other physics bodies.</p><p>Now add a script to the root <i class=gd-Node2D></i>:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ve used a few of <i class=gd-Tween></i>&rsquo;s options here to make everything work smoothly:</p><ul><li>: ensures that all movement takes place during the physics processing step.</li><li>: this makes the tween repeat.</li><li>: by default, all changes would happen at that same time. This makes the two happen one after another: moving to one end of the offset, then back to the start.</li></ul><p>Using the two exported properties, you can adjust the platform&rsquo;s movement. Set the to determine where the tween moves relative to its starting point, and the to determine how long it takes to complete the cycle.</p><p>Add some platforms in your level/world and try them out:</p><p><video controls src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/moving_platform4.webm autoplay></video></p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/2d_moving_platforms target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/2d_moving_platforms</a></p><h2 id=related-recipes>Related recipes</h2><ul><li><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/2d/platform_character/>Platform character</a></li></ul><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

