#: 4.x/2d/grid_movement/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=grid-based-movement>Grid-based movement</h1><h2 id=problem>Problem</h2><p>You need a 2D character that moves in a grid pattern.</p><h2 id=solution>Solution</h2><p>Grid- or tile-based movement means the character&rsquo;s position is restricted. They can only stand on a particular tile - never between two tiles.</p><h3 id=character-setup>Character setup</h3><p>Here are the nodes we&rsquo;ll use for the player:</p><ul><li><i class=gd-Area2D></i> (&ldquo;Player&rdquo;): Using an <i class=gd-Area2D></i> means we can detect overlap (for picking up objects or colliding with enemies).<ul><li><i class=gd-Sprite2D></i>: You can use a sprite sheet here (we&rsquo;ll set up the animation below).</li><li><i class=gd-CollisionShape2D></i>: Don&rsquo;t make the hitbox too big. Since the player will be standing on the center of a tile, overlaps will be from the center.</li><li><i class=gd-RayCast2D></i>: For checking if movement is possible in the given direction.</li><li><i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i>: For playing the character&rsquo;s walk animation(s).</li></ul></li></ul><p>Add some input actions to the Input Map. We&rsquo;ll use &ldquo;up&rdquo;, &ldquo;down&rdquo;, &ldquo;left&rdquo;, and &ldquo;right&rdquo; for this example.</p><h3 id=basic-movement>Basic movement</h3><p>We&rsquo;ll start by setting up the tile-by-tile movement, without any animations or interpolation.</p><div class=highlight></div><p> should be set to match the size of your tiles. In a larger project, this can be set by your main scene when instancing the player. We&rsquo;re using 64x64 tiles in the example below.</p><p>The dictionary maps the input action names to direction vectors. Make sure you have the names spelled the same here and in the Input Map (capitalization counts!).</p><div class=highlight></div><p> allows us to &ldquo;round&rdquo; the position to the nearest tile increment, and adding a half-tile amount makes sure the player is centered on the tile.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Here&rsquo;s the actual movement code. When an input event occurs, we check the four directions to see which one matched, then pass it to to change the position.</p><p><a href=#image-3f5e3e92638f1dc79c424d862da67c12 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/grid_example1.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-3f5e3e92638f1dc79c424d862da67c12><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/grid_example1.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=collision>Collision</h3><p>Now we can add some obstacles. You can add <i class=gd-StaticBody2D></i>s to manually add some obstacles (enable snapping to make sure they&rsquo;re aligned with the grid) or use a TileMap (with collisions defined), as in the example below.</p><p>We&rsquo;ll use the <i class=gd-RayCast2D></i> to determine whether a move to the next tile is allowed.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>When changing a raycast&rsquo;s property, the physics engine won&rsquo;t recalculate its collisions until the next physics frame. lets you update the ray&rsquo;s state immediately. If it&rsquo;s not colliding, then we allow the move.</p><p><a href=#image-1f8acff6ac9b23001ff6569cd8a2de0d class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/grid_example2.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-1f8acff6ac9b23001ff6569cd8a2de0d><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/grid_example2.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Note</div><div class=box-content><p>Another common method is to use 4 separate raycasts, one for each direction.</p></div></div><h3 id=animating-movement>Animating movement</h3><p>Lastly we can interpolate the position between tiles, giving a smooth feel to the movement. We&rsquo;ll use the node to animate the property.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Add a reference to the <i class=gd-Tween></i> node and a variable to set our movement speed.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ll ignore any input while the tween is running and remove the direct change so that the tween can handle it.</p><div class=highlight></div><p><a href=#image-dd7ec893c05a75fa6eb0b7a0d38196f6 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/grid_example3.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-dd7ec893c05a75fa6eb0b7a0d38196f6><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/grid_example3.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Experiment with different tween transitions for different movement effects.</p><h2 id=i-classfas-fa-code-branchi-download-this-project><i class=\"fas fa-code-branch\"></i> Download This Project</h2><p>Download the project code here: <a href=https://github.com/godotrecipes/2d_grid_movement/ target=_blank>https://github.com/godotrecipes/2d_grid_movement/</a></p><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

