#: 4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_03/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=coding-the-player>Coding the Player</h1><p>In the last section, we configured the project and downloaded the game art. Now we&rsquo;re ready to start coding - starting with the player-controlled ship.</p><h2 id=adding-a-script>Adding a script</h2><p>Writing scripts and attaching them to nodes and other objects is how you build behavior and game mechanics into your game. Our scene displays the ship, defines its collision hitbox, etc., but it can&rsquo;t move, and nothing would happen if it collided. We&rsquo;ll write code to add this functionality to the ship.</p><p>Select the node and click the <strong>Attach script</strong> button:</p><p><a href=#image-c4b1c86a78cdcb589c596fffe525e974 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_13.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-c4b1c86a78cdcb589c596fffe525e974><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_13.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>You don&rsquo;t need to change any of the options on the <strong>Attach Node Script</strong> window, so just click <strong>Create</strong> and you&rsquo;ll be taken to the script editor.</p><p>Let&rsquo;s look at the first line of the script, which has automatically been added.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This line defines what type of object this script should be attached to. It means that the script will have access to all the functionality that an <i class=gd-Area2D></i> provides.</p><p>Your line should always match the type of node the script is attached to.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle info\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-info-circle\"></i> Accessing scripts</div><div class=box-content><p>A script on its own doesn&rsquo;t do much of anything. Scripts <em>define</em> additional functionality for whatever object they&rsquo;re attached to. You will never be accessing a variable in some script, you&rsquo;ll be accessing a property of an <em>object</em>, which is <em>defined</em> by that script. This is a very important distinction.</p></div></div><h2 id=movement>Movement</h2><p>We&rsquo;ll start by making the ship move around the screen. Let&rsquo;s start with some code that does the following:</p><ul><li>Detect what input(s) the player is pressing</li><li>Move the ship in the direction of the input</li></ul><div class=highlight></div><p>Let&rsquo;s break this down line-by-line:</p><ul><li>Adding in front of a variable allows you to adjust its value in the <strong>Inspector</strong>.</li></ul><p><a href=#image-d0c83a6b3c8a505a8e1815b089ed4151 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_14.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-d0c83a6b3c8a505a8e1815b089ed4151><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_14.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><ul><li>The function is called once every frame by the engine. Any code we place in this function will be executed every frame.</li><li> checks the pressed state of the four given inputs and produces a vector pointing in that direction.</li><li>Finally, we move the ship&rsquo;s by adding that input vector, scaling it to the desired speed, and multipling by .</li></ul><div class=expand><input type=checkbox id=expand-2 aria-controls=expandcontent-2> <label class=expand-label for=expand-2><i class=\"fas fa-chevron-down\"></i> <i class=\"fas fa-chevron-right\"></i> Links to more information</label><div id=expandcontent-2 class=expand-content><ul><li>Understanding vectors: <a href=https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/tutorials/math/vector_math.html target=_blank>Vector Math</a></li><li>What is ? <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/basics/understanding_delta/>Understanding delta</a></li></ul></div></div><p>Run the scene by clicking the <strong>Run Current Scene</strong> button, and try moving around.</p><p><a href=#image-b2576bbac53dbe1fc3d7ab2b869dfda0 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_15.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-b2576bbac53dbe1fc3d7ab2b869dfda0><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_15.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=staying-on-screen>Staying on screen</h3><p>One problem we have is that if you keep moving, you&rsquo;ll go off the screen. We need to lock the player&rsquo;s property inside the bounds of the screen rectangle. Add this line at the top of the script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The here tells Godot not to set the value of the variable until the node has entered the scene tree. Effectively, it means &ldquo;wait until the game starts&rdquo;, because there&rsquo;s no window to get the size of until the game is running.</p><p>The next step is to <em>clamp</em> the position within the bounds of that rectangle. , which is what is, has a method we can use. Put this line right after setting the :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Run the scene again and try moving off the edges. You&rsquo;ll notice that half of the ship still goes off screen. This is because the ship&rsquo;s is the center of the . Since we know our ship is , we can change the to include extra pixels:</p><div class=highlight></div><h3 id=matching-animation-to-direction>Matching animation to direction</h3><p>Now that the ship is moving, we can choose the &ldquo;tilted&rdquo; ship images when moving left or right, as well as the matching &ldquo;Booster&rdquo; animation.</p><p>To tell which direction we&rsquo;re moving, we can check the value of the vector. Depending on whether it&rsquo;s positive (right), negative (left), or zero (not moving), we can choose the value of the and the of the .</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Once again, play the scene and verify that the images change when moving left/right. Verify that everything works as intended before moving to the next step.</p><p>The next step will be to create the scene and let the player shoot.</p><table><thead><tr><th><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_02/><i class=\"fas fa-arrow-left\"></i> Prev</a></span></th><th style=text-align:right><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_04/>Next <i class=\"fas fa-arrow-right\"></i></a></span></th></tr></thead></table><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

