#: 4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_05/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=shooting>Shooting</h1><p>The scene provides us with a reusable object we can <em>instantiate</em> whenever the player shoots.</p><h2 id=adding-to-the-player>Adding to the player</h2><p>Let&rsquo;s head back to the script and add a few new variables:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The two variables let you configure them in the <strong>Inspector</strong> so that you can adjust the cooldown time. Set the by clicking the property and choosing the file.</p><p> is what programmers call a <em>flag</em> - a Boolean variable that controls a certain condition. In this case it determines whether the player is allowed to shoot or not. During the cooldown period, this variable will be .</p><p>Next, we&rsquo;ll add a function similar to the one we made for the . This will let us set initial values for the player, as well as resetting them when the game restarts.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This places the player at the bottom center of the screen - a good place to start. It also ensures that the cooldown timer has the correct wait time.</p><p>The function will be called whenever we press the &ldquo;shoot&rdquo; input.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The first thing this function does is check if the player is allowed to shoot. If it isn&rsquo;t, will end the function immediately.</p><p>If the player is allowed to shoot, then we set the flag to , and start the cooldown timer. Then we create a new bullet and add it to the game, calling its function to make sure it&rsquo;s placed in the correct position (just above the player&rsquo;s ship).</p><p>We can call this function when the player is pressing the key. Add this to the end of the function, after the line:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ll also need to connect the signal of .</p><div class=highlight></div><p>When the cooldown ends, we can allow shooting again.</p><p>Go ahead and run the scene and try pressing the shoot action.</p><p><a href=#image-f8b4c7769024c5147e5c0cc86ff9de42 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_17.gif alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-f8b4c7769024c5147e5c0cc86ff9de42><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_17.gif alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Adding instances to the tree</div><div class=box-content><p>Notice that we&rsquo;ve added the new bullets as children of the SceneTree root (), and not to the player ship. This is important because if we made the bullets children of the ship, then they would be &ldquo;attached&rdquo; to it when it moves.</p></div></div><h2 id=next-steps>Next steps</h2><p>Shooting&rsquo;s no fun without something to shoot at. We&rsquo;ll start making the enemies soon, but first we need a scene where we can bring the player, enemies, and other game objects together.</p><table><thead><tr><th><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_04/><i class=\"fas fa-arrow-left\"></i> Prev</a></span></th><th style=text-align:right><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_06/>Next <i class=\"fas fa-arrow-right\"></i></a></span></th></tr></thead></table><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

