#: 4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_02/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=designing-the-player-scene>Designing the Player Scene</h1><p>In the last section, we configured the project and downloaded the game art. Now we&rsquo;re ready to start coding - starting with the player-controlled ship.</p><h2 id=setting-up-the-ship-scene>Setting up the Ship Scene</h2><p>A common part of the Godot workflow is creating scenes. As discussed earlier, a <em>scene</em> in Godot is nothing more than a collection of <em>nodes</em>. In most Godot projects, each game object is configured as a scene, with nodes that provide it with the desired functionality, and optionally some code to customize its behavior.</p><h3 id=choosing-nodes>Choosing nodes</h3><p>The first step is to decide what kind of node to start with. The first node you add to the scene is called the <em>root node</em>. A scene&rsquo;s root node should generally be the one that primarily defines the game object&rsquo;s behavior. Then you attach <em>child</em> nodes to add additional functionality.</p><p>So what should our game&rsquo;s ship be? Let&rsquo;s break down the requirements, and look at what nodes might be useful to meet them.</p><p>The ship needs to:</p><ul><li><p><em>Move in 2D space</em>. For this, a basic <i class=gd-Node2D></i> would suffice, as that&rsquo;s the node that has , , and other 2D-related properties. However, it has no appearance.</p></li><li><p><em>Display an image</em>. <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> is the node for this. Since it&rsquo;s also a <i class=gd-Node2D></i>, we&rsquo;d still be able to move it around.</p></li><li><p><em>Detect getting hit</em>. The enemies will be shooting and flying around on the screen, so we&rsquo;ll need to know when the ship is hit. We don&rsquo;t have a need for solid objects - they&rsquo;re not going to bounce off each other or transfer momentum - we just need to know when they touch. For this, an <i class=gd-Area2D></i> would be perfect. It can detect touching other objects, has positional properties, but it has no appearance of its own.</p></li></ul><p>Looking at this list, the <i class=gd-Area2D></i> provides the main functionality. We can attach a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> to display the ship image, and then we&rsquo;ll have everything we need.</p><h2 id=building-the-scene>Building the scene</h2><p>In the <strong>Scene</strong> tab, click the <strong>+</strong> button or the <strong>+ Other Node</strong> button to add the first node. Start typing <i class=gd-Area2D></i> and choose it from the list. Once it&rsquo;s in the <strong>Scene</strong> tab, click the node&rsquo;s name to rename it to , and press to save the scene.</p><h3 id=displaying-the-ship>Displaying the ship</h3><p>With the node selected, add another node: a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i>. To keep things organized, let&rsquo;s rename this node to .</p><p>From the <strong>FileSystem</strong> tab, drag the file from the art pack and drop it in the <strong>Texture</strong> property of the <strong>Inspector</strong>.</p><p><a href=#image-40c164d1cc67274868907afe7b80bcfb class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_player_ship.png?width=100\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:100 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-40c164d1cc67274868907afe7b80bcfb><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_player_ship.png?width=100\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>The first thing you&rsquo;ll notice is that there seem to be three ships! The image from the art pack also includes versions of the ship going to the left/right. We can use this - in the <strong>Animation</strong> section of the <strong>Inspector</strong>, set <strong>Hframes</strong> to . Now, changing the <strong>Frame</strong> property will move between the three different versions. Leave it at for now.</p><p><a href=#image-b5f83060459c09272849a77fdfe08f0f class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_04.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-b5f83060459c09272849a77fdfe08f0f><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_04.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=adding-a-collision-shape>Adding a collision shape</h3><p>You may also have noticed the yellow warning triangle on the <i class=gd-Area2D></i> node. If you click it, you&rsquo;ll see the warning is telling us that the area doesn&rsquo;t have a shape. We need to define its shape, and we can do that by adding a <i class=gd-CollisionShape2D></i> node as a child of the .</p><p>In the <strong>Inspector</strong> for this node, you&rsquo;ll see a <strong>Shape</strong> property that currently shows . If you click in this box, you&rsquo;ll see a dropdown that allows you to select from a variety of shapes. Choose <i class=gd-RectangleShape2D></i> and you&rsquo;ll see a light blue square appear over the ship. You can adjust the size of the shape by dragging the orange circles, or you can click on the shape in the <strong>Shape</strong> property to expand it and fill in the <strong>Size</strong> manually.</p><p><a href=#image-2de15c247c063212a4e0a9e76ccb8ea0 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_05.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-2de15c247c063212a4e0a9e76ccb8ea0><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_05.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=exhaust>Exhaust</h3><p>The ship will look much more dynamic with a little animation. Included in the art pack are some animations of exhaust flames named &ldquo;Boosters&rdquo;. There are three: one for each version of the ship (left, forward, and right).</p><p>To display these, select the node and add a child <i class=gd-AnimatedSprite2D></i> node and name it &ldquo;Boosters&rdquo;.</p><p>In the <strong>Inspector</strong>, under the <strong>Animation</strong> section, you&rsquo;ll find a property called <strong>Sprite Frames</strong>, which is currently . Click it to create a , then click the item to open the animation panel at the bottom of the editor window.</p><p><a href=#image-678120fb5500614f4771a2fb45ffc019 class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_06.png?width=800\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:800 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-678120fb5500614f4771a2fb45ffc019><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_06.png?width=800\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Double-click the &ldquo;default&rdquo; animation to rename it to &ldquo;forward&rdquo;. Then, to add the animation images, click the <strong>Add frames from sprite sheet</strong> button:</p><p><a href=#image-9f38d6a6f076323710aafb894566d672 class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_07.png?width=800\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:800 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-9f38d6a6f076323710aafb894566d672><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_07.png?width=800\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Choose the image and you&rsquo;ll see the <strong>Select Frames</strong> window, allowing you to choose the frames you want.</p><p><a href=#image-6935922211622aac2ba2fc9d57aadaa2 class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_08.png?width=800\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:800 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6935922211622aac2ba2fc9d57aadaa2><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_08.png?width=800\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>There are only two frames in this animation, but the grid isn&rsquo;t correct. Change the <strong>Size</strong> values to match the image sizes: . Then, click both frames to select them and click the <strong>Add 2 Frame(s)</strong> button.</p><p><a href=#image-afe454eaac6a676c4f43cff61a12a159 class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_09.png?width=800\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:800 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-afe454eaac6a676c4f43cff61a12a159><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_09.png?width=800\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Now that you&rsquo;ve added the two frames, press the <strong>Play</strong> button to run the animation. You can also toggle the <strong>Autoplay on Load</strong> button so that the animation will start automatically.</p><p><a href=#image-6c0bbecaf25f621761d266ad744e828d class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_10.png?width=800\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:800 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-6c0bbecaf25f621761d266ad744e828d><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_10.png?width=800\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>It&rsquo;s a little slow, so change the speed to .</p><p>Add two more animations by clicking the <strong>Add Animation</strong> button, naming them and .</p><p><a href=#image-80d83644256ec834eff80abdcedd18c6 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_11.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-80d83644256ec834eff80abdcedd18c6><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_11.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Repeat the process, adding the left and right &ldquo;Booster&rdquo; sprite sheets.</p><h3 id=gun-cooldown>Gun cooldown</h3><p>The last node we&rsquo;ll need to complete the player setup is a <i class=gd-Timer></i> to control how fast the player can shoot. Add the as a child of and name it . Set its <strong>One Shot</strong> property to &ldquo;On&rdquo;. This means that when the timer ends, it won&rsquo;t automatically restart. In the player&rsquo;s code, we&rsquo;ll start the timer when the player shoots, and they won&rsquo;t be able to shoot again until the timer runs out.</p><h3 id=next-steps>Next steps</h3><p>That completes the player scene setup. We&rsquo;ve added the nodes to give the player ship the functionality it will need in the game. In the next section, we&rsquo;ll add some code to enable the player to control the ship, make it shoot, and detect when it collides with things.</p><table><thead><tr><th><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_01/><i class=\"fas fa-arrow-left\"></i> Prev</a></span></th><th style=text-align:right><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_03/>Next <i class=\"fas fa-arrow-right\"></i></a></span></th></tr></thead></table><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

