#: 4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_09/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=ui-and-score>UI and Score</h1><p>The last main piece of our game is the <em>user interface</em> (UI). We need a way to show the player the score and other information. To do this, we&rsquo;ll use a variety of <i class=gd-Control></i> nodes - the nodes Godot provides for building UIs.</p><h2 id=ui-scene>UI scene</h2><p>Start the scene with a <i class=gd-MarginContainer></i> and name it .</p><p><em>Containers</em> are <i class=gd-Control></i> nodes that are designed to control the size and position of their children. Using them makes it easier to position and move Control nodes without having to do it manually. The <i class=gd-MarginContainer></i> makes sure its children don&rsquo;t get too close to the edge.</p><p>In the <strong>Inspector</strong> under <strong>Theme Overrides/Constants</strong> set all four <strong>Margin</strong> values to . Then, in the menu bar at the top of the viewport, set the anchors to the <strong>Top Wide</strong> preset.</p><p><a href=#image-1159aa49853c38d293b2ef1791beefc5 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_21.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-1159aa49853c38d293b2ef1791beefc5><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_21.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Next, we&rsquo;ll add an <i class=gd-HBoxContainer></i>. This type of container organizes its children horizontally. Under that, add a <i class=gd-TextureProgressBar></i>, which will represent our ship&rsquo;s shield level. Name it .</p><p>Unfortunately, there&rsquo;s not a good image in the art pack to use for a progress bar (there is one, but it isn&rsquo;t formatted in an easy way to work with). Instead, we&rsquo;ll use the two images below. One is a green bar and the other is a white outline. Save them in your project folder.</p><p><a href=#image-5ccf8e179f30ca2a63c8fa046622d903 class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/bar_foreground.png?width=100\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:100 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-5ccf8e179f30ca2a63c8fa046622d903><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/bar_foreground.png?width=100\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy> </a><a href=#image-5f9f27655ae8e0dc1228a0d906f3d2cf class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/bar_background.png?width=100\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:100 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-5f9f27655ae8e0dc1228a0d906f3d2cf><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/bar_background.png?width=100\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>In the <strong>Texture</strong> section, drag the foreground image to the <strong>Progress</strong> and the background image to the <strong>Under</strong> texture. The first thing you&rsquo;ll notice is that it&rsquo;s very small. Let&rsquo;s first under <strong>Layout</strong> set <strong>Custom Minimum Size</strong> to . You&rsquo;ll notice that the orange selection rectangle got bigger, but the image didn&rsquo;t. Well, we don&rsquo;t want the image to just stretch, or it would look bad. Instead we&rsquo;ll check the <strong>Nine Patch Stretch</strong> box, and then set the four <strong>Stretch Margin</strong> values to .</p><p>You should now see a long, unfilled bar. To see what it looks like when filled, change the <strong>Value</strong> property in the <strong>Range</strong> section to anything between and .</p><p><a href=#image-240d22371b0b8a405b3114905c5dbbd4 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_22.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-240d22371b0b8a405b3114905c5dbbd4><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_22.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>On the right side, we&rsquo;d like to show the score. Now, we could just use a <i class=gd-Label></i> node and add a font, but that&rsquo;s not very fun. The art pack includes a lovely pixel set of digits that we could use instead. We&rsquo;ll just need to do a little coding to chop it up and show the corect digit(s).</p><h2 id=score-counter>Score counter</h2><p>Start a new scene and add an <i class=gd-HBoxContainer></i>. Name it then set it to <strong>Top Wide</strong> and set the <strong>Alignment</strong> to &ldquo;End&rdquo;. Also, set the <strong>Theme Overrides/Constants/Separation</strong> to (you need to check the box next to the property).</p><p>In this container, we&rsquo;ll have a string of <i class=gd-TextureRect></i> nodes showing each digit. We&rsquo;ll start by adding one and then duplicating it.</p><p>Name the <i class=gd-TextureRect></i> . Under <strong>Texture</strong>, select &ldquo;New AtlasTexture&rdquo;, then click the box to open it. Drag into the <strong>Atlas</strong> property, then set the <strong>Region</strong> to . Set <strong>Stretch Mode</strong> to &ldquo;Keep Aspect Centered&rdquo;.</p><p>Select the node and press 7 times to create duplicates of the node. The picture below shows what you should see after this step:</p><p><a href=#image-e3955974544789fb881f2fac1ccccb96 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_23.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-e3955974544789fb881f2fac1ccccb96><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_23.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>We now have an issue, though. Even though we&rsquo;ve duplicated the <i class=gd-TextureRect></i> to create 8 unique copies, they are all using the same in the <strong>Texture</strong> property. This means that when we change the <strong>Region</strong> to show a different digit, it will change on <em>all</em> the digits.</p><p>This is because objects (such as ) are loaded into memory and then shared - there&rsquo;s really only one texture. While this is very efficient, because you don&rsquo;t waste memory loading the same image multiple times, it means that when we <em>do</em> want things to be unique, we have to specify it.</p><p>On each of the nodes, click the down arrow next to the and select &ldquo;Make Unique&rdquo;.</p><p><a href=#image-ce8fae9cd8d1ca0c8aa0723466704fc0 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/make_unique.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-ce8fae9cd8d1ca0c8aa0723466704fc0><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/make_unique.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Now we&rsquo;ll add a script to that will choose the correct <strong>Region</strong> values for whichever digit it needs to display.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We start by making a list of the coordinates in the image where each digit is found. Then, will format the number to an 8 digit number (for example, would become ). Then, for each digit, we can apply the correct coordinates from the array.</p><h2 id=scripting-the-ui>Scripting the UI</h2><p>Go back to the scene and add the to the <i class=gd-HBoxContainer></i>, then add a script to .</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ll call these functions from whenever we need to update the score or the shield.</p><h2 id=adding-the-ui-to-main>Adding the UI to main</h2><p>Now in the scene add a <i class=gd-CanvasLayer></i> node, and instance the as its child. The node creates another drawing layer, so our UI will be drawn on top of the rest of the game.</p><p>Change this function in :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Run the game and see that your score goes up when shooting enemies.</p><h2 id=player-shield>Player shield</h2><p>We can also add the shield to the player&rsquo;s script. Add these new lines at the top of :</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This syntax tells Godot that we want to call the function whenever the variable has its value set.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We can also connect the ship&rsquo;s signal so that we can detect when an enemy hits the ship:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>And in the enemy bullet, add some damage to the shield when it hits:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Finally, we need to connect the player&rsquo;s signal to the function in the UI that updates the shield bar. You can do this in the Inspector by selecting the node in the Main scene. Under the Node tab, double-click the signal to open the &ldquo;Connect a Signal&rdquo; window. In this window, select the node and type in the <strong>Receiver Method</strong> box.</p><p><a href=#image-d88fc553e135804af9cbf7b66f20aa4c class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_24.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-d88fc553e135804af9cbf7b66f20aa4c><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_24.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Run the game again and check that your shield depletes when you get hit by a bullet or an enemy.</p><h2 id=next-steps>Next steps</h2><p>We&rsquo;re almost done with the basic functionality. We just need a way to start and end the game.</p><table><thead><tr><th><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_08/><i class=\"fas fa-arrow-left\"></i> Prev</a></span></th><th style=text-align:right><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_10/>Next <i class=\"fas fa-arrow-right\"></i></a></span></th></tr></thead></table><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

