#: 4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_07/index.html
msgid "Enemies :: Godot 4 Recipes"
msgstr ""

#: 4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_07/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=enemies>Enemies</h1><p>Now that our player can shoot, let&rsquo;s give them something to shoot at.</p><h2 id=setting-up-the-scene>Setting up the scene</h2><p>We&rsquo;ll use an <i class=gd-Area2D></i> for the enemy, since we need it to detect overlap - either with the player&rsquo;s bullets, or with the player itself.</p><p>Here&rsquo;s are the nodes we&rsquo;ll need:</p><p>Select the area node and click the <strong>Node</strong> tab next to the <strong>Inspector</strong>. Under <strong>Groups</strong>, type &ldquo;enemies&rdquo; an click <strong>Add</strong>. Remember the code we wrote on the bullet? It looks for objects in the &ldquo;enemies&rdquo; group.</p><p>In the sprite&rsquo;s <strong>Texture</strong>, add and set its <strong>Animation/Hframes</strong> to .</p><p>As you&rsquo;ve done before, add a rectangular collision shape and size it to fit. Enable <strong>One Shot</strong> on both timer nodes.</p><p>In the <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i>, add an animation called &ldquo;bounce&rdquo; and set it to looping and autoplay. Set the <strong>Snap</strong> at the bottom of the animation panel to .</p><p>Select the sprite node and press the key icons next to <strong>Texture</strong> and <strong>Hframes</strong> to create tracks for them. We&rsquo;re doing this because later we&rsquo;ll add an &ldquo;explosion&rdquo; animation that will use different values for these properties.</p><p>Now we&rsquo;ll key the individual <strong>Frames</strong> values we want. Start with keying <strong>Frames</strong> each seconds to values in this order, , , . Finally, key again and put it immediately after. This will make a &ldquo;pulsing&rdquo; animation where the sprite grows and then bounces a little at the end. The animation setup should look like this:</p><p><a href=#image-149d16bc0e6b60c10ff675887ab57234 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_20.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-149d16bc0e6b60c10ff675887ab57234><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_20.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Press the play button to see it in action. Feel free to adjust it if you&rsquo;d like.</p><p>Now add another animation called &ldquo;explode&rdquo;. Set its length to seconds.</p><p>Change the sprite&rsquo;s <strong>Texture</strong> to and keyframe that property. Since this image has a different number of frames than the enemy image, we also need to change <strong>Hframes</strong> to and keyframe that.</p><p>Now keyframe <strong>Frame</strong> to at time and to at time . Play the animation to see it in action.</p><h2 id=enemy-script>Enemy script</h2><p>The enemies will spawn at the top of the screen in a grid. After a random amount of time, they&rsquo;ll descend toward the player and then return to the top if they weren&rsquo;t destroyed. Periodically, they&rsquo;ll also shoot at the player.</p><p>Add a script, and start with the variables:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>The variable is going to keep track of the enemy&rsquo;s starting position so that after it moves, it can return to its original location. We&rsquo;ll set it when the enemy is spawned and we call its function.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>When we spawn our enemies we&rsquo;ll call this function and pass it a position vector representing where on the screen the enemy should go. Note that we actually spawn it <em>above</em> the top of the screen (negative value). This is so that we can animate it coming onto the screen using a tween. We also randomize the two timers so that all enemies won&rsquo;t be moving and shooting at the same time.</p><p>Connect both of the timers&rsquo; signals.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We can start moving when the timer runs out, and we&rsquo;ll also shoot, but we haven&rsquo;t made a bullet yet, so that part will come later. Now that we&rsquo;re changing the , we can move using it.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Now if the isn&rsquo;t , we&rsquo;ll see the enemy move down the screen. When it goes off the bottom, we start it all over again.</p><p>We&rsquo;ve already written the code in the bullet scene that calls on the enemies it hits, so let&rsquo;s add that too.</p><div class=highlight></div><p>In this function, we stop moving, play the explosion animation, and then delete the enemy when it&rsquo;s finished. The call makes sure to turn off on the enemy. This is so that while the enemy is exploding, another bullet can&rsquo;t hit it again.</p><p>Add the signal at the top of the script:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We&rsquo;ll use that signal to let the main scene know that the player just earned some points.</p><h2 id=spawning-enemies>Spawning enemies</h2><p>Now let&rsquo;s go to the scene and add these enemies to the game. Add a script to and start by loading the enemy scene:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>Spawning enemies ordinarily won&rsquo;t happen until we&rsquo;ve pressed the &ldquo;Start&rdquo; button to begin the game, but since we haven&rsquo;t made that yet, we&rsquo;ll just spawn them immediately:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>This makes 27 enemies and positions them in a grid in the top half of the screen. We also make sure to connect the signal of each, so we need to create that function:</p><div class=highlight></div><p>We don&rsquo;t have a way to display the score yet, but we&rsquo;ll get to that soon.</p><p>Play the scene and you should see a bunch of enemies appear at the top and periodically fall down the screen. Next, we&rsquo;ll make them shoot.</p><table><thead><tr><th><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_06/><i class=\"fas fa-arrow-left\"></i> Prev</a></span></th><th style=text-align:right><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_08/>Next <i class=\"fas fa-arrow-right\"></i></a></span></th></tr></thead></table><footer class=footline></footer>"
