#: 4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_01/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=project-setup>Project Setup</h1><p>This first game project will guide you through making your first Godot Engine game. While you don&rsquo;t need any previous experience, it&rsquo;s expected that you&rsquo;ve at least read through the <a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/g101/start/>Godot 101: Getting Started</a> section. There, you&rsquo;ll learn about the editor interface and how to get around the Godot UI.</p><div class=\"box notices cstyle note\"><div class=box-label><i class=\"fa-fw fas fa-exclamation-circle\"></i> Why start with 2D?</div><div class=box-content><p>In a nutshell, 3D games are much more complex than 2D ones. However, many of the underlying game engine features you&rsquo;ll need to know are the same. You should stick to 2D until you have a good understanding of Godot&rsquo;s workflow. At that point, the jump to 3D will feel much easier.</p></div></div><p>Open Godot and start a new project. You can name it anything you&rsquo;d like - we&rsquo;re going with &ldquo;Classic Shmup&rdquo;, since this is a traditional shoot-em-up style game.</p><h2 id=downloading-the-art>Downloading the art</h2><p>You can download the art we&rsquo;ll be using for the game from itch.io: <a href=https://grafxkid.itch.io/mini-pixel-pack-3 target=_blank>Mini Pixel Pack by Grafxkid</a></p><p>Unzip the art pack and copy it into your project by dropping the folder in the <strong>FileSystem</strong> tab.</p><p><a href=#image-2bb7b5bcb4c622375852d926e656deed class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_01.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-2bb7b5bcb4c622375852d926e656deed><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_01.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h2 id=project-settings>Project settings</h2><p>Next, we need to set up some project-wide settings. Open <strong>Project Settings</strong> and check the &ldquo;Advanced Settings&rdquo; toggle in the upper-right.</p><ul><li><p>In the <strong>Display/Window</strong> section:</p><ul><li><strong>Viewport Width</strong> & <strong>Viewport Height</strong> to , .</li><li><strong>Window Width Override</strong> & <strong>Window Height Override</strong> to , .</li><li><strong>Stretch/Mode</strong> to .</li></ul></li></ul><p>These settings will ensure the game is the right size. Because we&rsquo;re using pixel art, the images themselves are very small, so an old-school resolution like is perfect. However, on a modern monitor, that&rsquo;s a fairly small window, so the other settings let us scale that up proportionally. If you have a 1080p monitor, you can make the override values instead. You&rsquo;ll also be able to resize the window when the game is running.</p><ul><li>In the <strong>Rendering/Textures</strong> section under <strong>Canvas Textures</strong>, set <strong>Default Texture Filter</strong> to . This will ensure that our beautiful pixel art stays nice and crisp, looking like the image on the right, not the one on the left:</li></ul><p><a href=#image-d8795d96ffe96c4e8be868f2fe7fc19e class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_02.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-d8795d96ffe96c4e8be868f2fe7fc19e><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_02.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><ul><li>Click the <strong>Input Map</strong> tab at the top of the <strong>Project Settings</strong> window. This is where we can set up the inputs we want to use in the game. In the &ldquo;Add New Action&rdquo; box, type the following, hitting after each to add it to the list of actions: , , , , . To assign key(s) to each named input, click the <strong>+</strong> button to its right and press the key on your keyboard. When you&rsquo;re done, you should have something like this:</li></ul><p><a href=#image-d6ffea2eaab39480052048a87307d185 class=lightbox-link><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_03.png?width=500\" alt=alt style=height:auto;width:500 loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-d6ffea2eaab39480052048a87307d185><img src=\"/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_03.png?width=500\" alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Feel free to use other keys if you&rsquo;d rather use a different setup.</p><h2 id=next-steps>Next steps</h2><p>That takes care of setting up - now we&rsquo;re ready to get started! In the next section, we&rsquo;ll create the player-controlled spaceship.</p><table><thead><tr><th><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/><i class=\"fas fa-arrow-left\"></i> Prev</a></span></th><th style=text-align:right><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_02/>Next <i class=\"fas fa-arrow-right\"></i></a></span></th></tr></thead></table><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

