#: 4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_06/index.html
msgid "<h1 id=main-scene>Main Scene</h1><p>Before we can make enemies, powerups, or any other game objects, we need a place where they can all exist together with the player. In most games, this would be called a &ldquo;level&rdquo; or &ldquo;main&rdquo; scene, and that&rsquo;s what we&rsquo;ll call it here.</p><p>Start the scene with a <i class=gd-Node2D></i> called &ldquo;Main&rdquo; and save it.</p><h2 id=creating-the-background>Creating the background</h2><p>Add a <i class=gd-Sprite2D></i> child. Name this sprite &ldquo;Background&rdquo; and add the as its texture.</p><p>This image has two frames, each pixels in size. We&rsquo;d like the image to tile across the full size of the screen, so start with the following settings:</p><ul><li><p>Under <strong>Offset</strong> set <strong>Centered</strong> to &ldquo;off&rdquo;. This makes the image&rsquo;s top left corner start at the origin rather than its center.</p></li><li><p>Under <strong>Region</strong>, turn <strong>Enabled</strong> &ldquo;on&rdquo;, and then set the <strong>Rect</strong> to a width of and a height of . This makes the image stretch to the size of the screen.</p></li><li><p>Under <strong>Texture</strong> change <strong>Repeat</strong> to . This causes the image to repeat over the full size of the screen.</p></li></ul><p>Now add the player to the scene by selecting the node and clicking the <strong>Instantiate Child Scene</strong> button.</p><p><a href=#image-21c600ceac7897a15113a2780d0bd414 class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_18.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-21c600ceac7897a15113a2780d0bd414><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_18.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><h3 id=animating-the-background>Animating the background</h3><p>We can make the scene more dynamic by animating the background. While we could do this in code by changing the property every frame, we&rsquo;ll use an <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> node instead; add one as a child of .</p><p>At the bottom of the editor window, you&rsquo;ll see the <strong>Animation</strong> panel. There&rsquo;s a lot of information there, so let&rsquo;s look at how it&rsquo;s laid out:</p><p><a href=#image-0b4895dfbe667f576c066fb0daca2a6b class=lightbox-link><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_19.png alt=alt style=height:auto;width:auto loading=lazy> </a><a href=javascript:history.back(); class=lightbox id=image-0b4895dfbe667f576c066fb0daca2a6b><img src=/godot_recipes/4.x/img/2d_101_19.png alt=alt class=lightbox-image loading=lazy></a></p><p>Click the <strong>Animation</strong> button and choose <strong>New Animation</strong>. You can name the new animation . Set its <strong>Length</strong> to and toggle the <strong>Looping</strong> and <strong>Autoplay</strong> buttons.</p><p>Animations work by adding <em>tracks</em> that represent properties that you want the <i class=gd-AnimationPlayer></i> to control. In the timeline of the player, you&rsquo;ll add <em>keyframes</em> that define what value you want the property to have at that particular time.</p><p>We can add keyframes to the animation by clicking the key icon that now appears next to every property in the <strong>Inspector</strong>. Make sure the scrubber (the blue indicator on the timeline) is at time , then select the and click the key next to <strong>Region/Rect</strong>. You&rsquo;ll be asked if you want to create a new track and then you&rsquo;ll see the new track added to the animation panel, with a small dot representing the keyframe you&rsquo;ve just added. Drag the scrubber to time and then change the <strong>y</strong> value of the <strong>Region/Rect</strong> property to . Click the key to add another keyframe.</p><p>Now when you press <strong>Play</strong> on the animation, you should see the background slowly scrolling behind the player.</p><h2 id=next-steps>Next steps</h2><p>The main scene is now ready for us to add enemies. In the next step we&rsquo;ll make a single enemy scene, as we did with the bullets, and then instantiate that multiple times.</p><table><thead><tr><th><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_05/><i class=\"fas fa-arrow-left\"></i> Prev</a></span></th><th style=text-align:right><span class=\"btn cstyle transparent\"><a href=/godot_recipes/4.x/games/first_2d/first_2d_07/>Next <i class=\"fas fa-arrow-right\"></i></a></span></th></tr></thead></table><footer class=footline></footer>"
msgstr ""

